1.2 Matrices as Linear Maps






NoteDefinition: Linear Map



A map \(T:V\to W\) is linear if, for all vectors \(\bx,\by\in V\) and scalars \(\alpha,\beta\), \[
\begin{align}
T(\alpha\bx+\beta\by)=\alpha T(\bx)+\beta T(\by).
\end{align}
\] Linearity says that the response to a combination of inputs is the same combination of the individual responses.








NoteDefinition: Matrix



A matrix \(\bA\in\fR^{m\times n}\) is a rectangular array of scalars with \(m\) rows and \(n\) columns. It represents a linear map from \(\fR^n\) to \(\fR^m\) once bases have been selected in the domain and range.








NoteTheorem: The Columns Determine the Map



Let \(\be_1,...,\be_n\) be the standard basis of \(\fR^n\), and let \(T:\fR^n\to\fR^m\) be linear. If the \(j\)th column of \(\bA\) is \(\ba_j=T(\be_j)\), then \[
\begin{align}
T(\bx)=\bA\bx=\sum_{j=1}^n x_j\ba_j.
\end{align}
\] Thus a linear map is completely determined by what it does to a basis.








TipProof





Every vector has the standard-coordinate expansion \(\bx=\sum_{j=1}^n x_j\be_j\). Linearity gives \[
\begin{align}
T(\bx)=T\left(\sum_{j=1}^n x_j\be_j\right)
    =\sum_{j=1}^n x_jT(\be_j)
    =\sum_{j=1}^n x_j\ba_j,
\end{align}
\] which is precisely the definition of the matrix-vector product \(\bA\bx\).









TipRemark



This column view is more informative than memorizing the row-by-column rule. The set of all possible outputs \(\bA\bx\) is the span of the columns. A zero output means that one linear combination of the columns vanishes. Existence and uniqueness for linear systems will grow directly from these two observations.








NoteExample



For \[
\begin{align}
\bA=\begin{pmatrix}1&2\\3&-1\end{pmatrix},
    \qquad
    \bx=\begin{pmatrix}4\\5\end{pmatrix},
\end{align}
\] the product is best read as \[
\begin{align}
\bA\bx
    =4\begin{pmatrix}1\\3\end{pmatrix}
     +5\begin{pmatrix}2\\-1\end{pmatrix}
    =\begin{pmatrix}14\\7\end{pmatrix}.
\end{align}
\] The same calculation can be read row by row: each output coordinate is a weighted sum of the input coordinates.

1.4 Transpose and Symmetry






NoteDefinition: Matrix Transpose



The transpose of \(\bA\in\fR^{m\times n}\) is the matrix \(\bA^T\in\fR^{n\times m}\) defined by \[
\begin{align}
(\bA^T)_{ij}=a_{ji}.
\end{align}
\] It turns columns into rows and rows into columns.








NoteDefinition: Symmetric and Skew-Symmetric Matrices



A square matrix \(\bA\) is symmetric if \(\bA^T=\bA\) and skew-symmetric if \(\bA^T=-\bA\).








TipRemark



Every square matrix splits uniquely into symmetric and skew-symmetric parts: \[
\begin{align}
\bA=\frac{\bA+\bA^T}{2}+\frac{\bA-\bA^T}{2}.
\end{align}
\] Symmetric matrices will be especially important because they possess real eigenvalues and orthogonal eigenvectors.








NoteTheorem: Properties of the Transpose



For conformable matrices and any scalar \(\alpha\), \[
\begin{align}
(\bA^T)^T&=\bA,&
    (\bA+\bB)^T&=\bA^T+\bB^T,\\
    (\alpha\bA)^T&=\alpha\bA^T,&
    (\bA\bB)^T&=\bB^T\bA^T.
\end{align}
\]








TipProof





The first three identities follow entry by entry. For the product, \[
\begin{align}
((\bA\bB)^T)_{ij}
    =(\bA\bB)_{ji}
    =\sum_k a_{jk}b_{ki}
    =\sum_k(\bB^T)_{ik}(\bA^T)_{kj}
    =(\bB^T\bA^T)_{ij}.
\end{align}
\]









NoteCorollary: Reversal Law for Multiple Factors



Repeated use of the product rule gives \[
\begin{align}
(\bA_1\bA_2\cdots\bA_k)^T=\bA_k^T\cdots\bA_2^T\bA_1^T.
\end{align}
\]








NoteCorollary: Outer Product is Symmetric of Rank One



For every nonzero \(\bx\in\fR^n\), the outer product \(\bx\bx^T\) is symmetric and has rank one. Moreover, \[
\begin{align}
\bx^T\bx=\sum_{i=1}^n x_i^2&gt;0.
\end{align}
\]

2.2 Projection as Best Approximation
The coefficient used in the proof of Cauchy–Schwarz has a geometric meaning. It selects the point on the line through \(\by\) that is closest to \(\bx\).






NoteDefinition: Projection onto a Nonzero Vector



For \(\by\neq\bzero\), the orthogonal projection of \(\bx\) onto the line spanned by \(\by\) is \[
\begin{align}
\operatorname{proj}_{\by}(\bx)
  =\frac{\langle\bx,\by\rangle}{\langle\by,\by\rangle}\by.
\end{align}
\] The residual \(\bx-\operatorname{proj}_{\by}(\bx)\) is orthogonal to \(\by\).








NoteTheorem: Closest Point on a Line



Let \(\by\neq\bzero\) and let \[
\begin{align}
\alpha=\frac{\langle\bx,\by\rangle}{\langle\by,\by\rangle}.
\end{align}
\] Then \(\alpha\by\) is the unique closest point to \(\bx\) among all scalar multiples of \(\by\): \[
\begin{align}
\|\bx-\alpha\by\|\leq\|\bx-\beta\by\|
  \qquad\text{for every }\beta\in\fR.
\end{align}
\]








TipProof





The residual \(\bx-\alpha\by\) is orthogonal to \(\by\). For any scalar \(\beta\), \[
\begin{align}
\bx-\beta\by=(\bx-\alpha\by)+(\alpha-\beta)\by.
\end{align}
\] The two terms on the right are orthogonal. The Pythagorean theorem gives \[
\begin{align}
\|\bx-\beta\by\|^2
  =\|\bx-\alpha\by\|^2+(\alpha-\beta)^2\|\by\|^2.
\end{align}
\] The second term is non-negative and vanishes only when \(\beta=\alpha\).









NoteDefinition: Orthogonal Complement



For a subspace \(S\subseteq\fR^n\), its orthogonal complement is \[
\begin{align}
S^\perp=\{\bz\in\fR^n:\langle\bz,\bs\rangle=0\text{ for every }\bs\in S\}.
\end{align}
\]








NoteTheorem: Projection onto a Subspace with an Orthonormal Basis



Let the columns of \(\bQ\in\fR^{n\times k}\) be an orthonormal basis for a subspace \(S\). Then \[
\begin{align}
\bP=\bQ\bQ^T
\end{align}
\] is the orthogonal projector onto \(S\). Every vector has the unique decomposition \[
\begin{align}
\bx=\bP\bx+(\bI-\bP)\bx,
  \qquad
  \bP\bx\in S,
  \qquad
  (\bI-\bP)\bx\in S^\perp.
\end{align}
\] Moreover, \(\bP\bx\) is the unique point in \(S\) closest to \(\bx\).








TipProof





The vector \(\bP\bx=\bQ(\bQ^T\bx)\) is a linear combination of the columns of \(\bQ\), so it lies in \(S\). For any vector \(\bs=\bQ\bc\in S\), \[
\begin{align}
\langle(\bI-\bP)\bx,\bs\rangle
  &=\bc^T\bQ^T(\bI-\bQ\bQ^T)\bx \\
  &=\bc^T(\bQ^T-\bQ^T\bQ\bQ^T)\bx \\
  &=\bc^T(\bQ^T-\bI_k\bQ^T)\bx = 0.
\end{align}
\] Thus \((\bI-\bP)\bx\in S^\perp\). For any \(\bs\in S\), \[
\begin{align}
\bx-\bs=(\bx-\bP\bx)+(\bP\bx-\bs)
\end{align}
\] is an orthogonal decomposition because \(\bP\bx-\bs\in S\) and \(\bx-\bP\bx\in S^\perp\). By the Pythagorean theorem, \[
\begin{align}
\|\bx-\bs\|^2
  =\|\bx-\bP\bx\|^2+\|\bP\bx-\bs\|^2.
\end{align}
\] Since \(\|\bP\bx-\bs\|^2 \geq 0\) with equality if and only if \(\bs = \bP\bx\), this proves that \(\bP\bx\) is the unique closest point.









TipRemark



For an orthonormal basis, the projection coefficients are simply the inner products \(\bQ^T\bx\). If the basis columns are stored in a general full-rank matrix \(\bV\), the closest vector \(\bV\bc\) instead satisfies \[
\begin{align}
\bV^T\bV\bc=\bV^T\bx.
\end{align}
\] The matrix \(\bV^T\bV\) records all pairwise inner products between the basis vectors. Orthogonality reduces this matrix to the identity. This is the first reason orthogonal bases simplify computation.

3.4 Condition Number as Distortion
An invertible matrix can stretch some directions and compress others. A solve with that matrix must undo both effects. The condition number measures the total distortion.






NoteDefinition: Condition Number



For a nonsingular square matrix \(\bA\) and an induced norm, \[
\begin{align}
\kappa(\bA)=\|\bA\|\,\|\bA^{-1}\|.
\end{align}
\]








NoteTheorem: Two-Sided Stretching Bound



For every nonzero vector \(\bx\), \[
\begin{align}
\frac{1}{\|\bA^{-1}\|}
  \leq
  \frac{\|\bA\bx\|}{\|\bx\|}
  \leq
  \|\bA\|.
\end{align}
\] The ratio between the largest possible upper stretch and the guaranteed lower stretch is \(\kappa(\bA)\).








TipProof





The upper bound follows directly from the definition of the induced norm: \[
\begin{align}
\frac{\|\bA\bx\|}{\|\bx\|} \leq \max_{\bz\neq\bzero} \frac{\|\bA\bz\|}{\|\bz\|} = \|\bA\|.
\end{align}
\] For the lower bound, write \(\bx = \bA^{-1}(\bA\bx)\). Consistency applied to \(\bA^{-1}\) yields \[
\begin{align}
\|\bx\| = \|\bA^{-1}(\bA\bx)\| \leq \|\bA^{-1}\|\,\|\bA\bx\|.
\end{align}
\] Since \(\bx\neq\bzero\) and \(\bA\) is nonsingular, \(\|\bA\bx\| &gt; 0\). Dividing by \(\|\bA^{-1}\|\,\|\bx\|\) gives \[
\begin{align}
\frac{1}{\|\bA^{-1}\|} \leq \frac{\|\bA\bx\|}{\|\bx\|}.
\end{align}
\] Combining the two inequalities establishes the bound.



If \(\kappa(\bA)\) is near one, the matrix treats every direction comparably. If it is large, at least one direction is compressed relative to another. Recovering a component in a strongly compressed direction requires amplification by \(\bA^{-1}\). Consequently, ill-conditioned linear systems can turn small data errors into large solution errors. the referenced result makes this statement quantitative.






WarningExercise




Compute. Three induced norms. For \[
\begin{align}
\bA=\begin{pmatrix}1&-2\\3&4\end{pmatrix},
\end{align}
\] compute \(\|\bA\|_1\) and \(\|\bA\|_\infty\). Estimate \(\|\bA\|_2\) from below by testing at least four unit vectors and from above using \(\|\bA\|_F\).
Derive. Submultiplicativity from amplification. Prove \(\|\bA_1\cdots\bA_k\|\leq\prod_{j=1}^k\|\bA_j\|\). Explain why this bound may be conservative when the direction of greatest stretch for one factor is not mapped into the direction of greatest stretch for the next.
Explore. Entries do not determine action. Compare \[
\begin{align}
\bA=\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\1&1\end{pmatrix},
     \qquad
     \bB=\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\-1&-1\end{pmatrix}.
\end{align}
\] Both matrices have the same entry magnitudes. Find their ranges and null spaces, identify directions they annihilate, and explain why an entrywise description misses this structure.
Derive. Duality and transpose. Starting from the definition of a dual norm, prove \(\|\bA\|_1=\|\bA^T\|_\infty\). Then formulate the corresponding statement for an arbitrary norm and its dual.
Derive. Frobenius geometry. Verify the inner-product axioms for \(\langle\bA,\bB\rangle_F\). Prove the matrix Cauchy–Schwarz inequality \(|\langle\bA,\bB\rangle_F|\leq\|\bA\|_F\|\bB\|_F\) by treating the entries as vectors.
Explore. Conditioning as a thin ellipse. Let \(\bA=\operatorname{diag}(1,\varepsilon)\) with \(0&lt;\varepsilon&lt;1\). Describe the image of the Euclidean unit circle, compute \(\|\bA\|_2\), \(\|\bA^{-1}\|_2\), and \(\kappa_2(\bA)\) directly from the definition, and explain which component of a solution is sensitive to perturbations.
Explore. Changing units. Suppose the two coordinates of \(\bx\) are measured in meters and millimeters. Introduce a diagonal scaling matrix that expresses both in meters. Derive the transformed matrix for the same linear map and compare its induced norm before and after scaling. Explain why a condition number is meaningful only after the input and output measurements have been specified.

6.2 Invertibility and the Four Fundamental Subspaces
The solvability and uniqueness of solutions are governed by four fundamental subspaces associated with \(\bA\).






NoteDefinition: The Four Fundamental Subspaces



Let \(\bA \in \fR^{m \times n}\).

The Column Space (Range) \(\mathcal{R}(\bA) = \{ \bA\bx : \bx \in \fR^n \} \subseteq \fR^m\).
The Null Space (Kernel) \(\mathcal{N}(\bA) = \{ \bx \in \fR^n : \bA\bx = \bzero \} \subseteq \fR^n\).
The Row Space \(\mathcal{R}(\bA^T) = \{ \bA^T\by : \by \in \fR^m \} \subseteq \fR^n\).
The Left Null Space \(\mathcal{N}(\bA^T) = \{ \by \in \fR^m : \bA^T\by = \bzero \} \subseteq \fR^m\).

The rank \(r = \operatorname{rank}(\bA)\) is the dimension of the column space \(\mathcal{R}(\bA)\).








NoteTheorem: The Rank-Nullity Theorem



Let \(\bA \in \fR^{m \times n}\) with rank \(r = \dim \mathcal{R}(\bA)\). Then \[
\begin{align}
\operatorname{rank}(\bA) + \dim \mathcal{N}(\bA) = n.
\end{align}
\]








TipProof





Let \(k = \dim \mathcal{N}(\bA)\), and choose a basis \(\{\bv_1, ..., \bv_k\}\) for the null space. Extend this list to a basis \(\{\bv_1, ..., \bv_n\}\) of \(\fR^n\). For any \(\bx \in \fR^n\), expand \(\bx = \sum_{i=1}^n c_i \bv_i\). Linearity gives \[
\begin{align}
\bA\bx = \sum_{i=1}^k c_i \bA\bv_i + \sum_{i=k+1}^n c_i \bA\bv_i = \bzero + \sum_{i=k+1}^n c_i \bA\bv_i = \sum_{i=k+1}^n c_i \bA\bv_i.
\end{align}
\] Thus the vectors \(\{\bA\bv_{k+1}, ..., \bA\bv_n\}\) span \(\mathcal{R}(\bA)\). To establish linear independence, suppose \(\sum_{i=k+1}^n d_i \bA\bv_i = \bzero\). Linearity yields \[
\begin{align}
\bA\left(\sum_{i=k+1}^n d_i \bv_i\right) = \bzero \quad \Longrightarrow \quad \bw = \sum_{i=k+1}^n d_i \bv_i \in \mathcal{N}(\bA).
\end{align}
\] Because \(\{\bv_1, ..., \bv_k\}\) is a basis for \(\mathcal{N}(\bA)\), there exist coefficients \(a_1, ..., a_k\) such that \(\bw = \sum_{j=1}^k a_j \bv_j\). Equating the two expressions gives \[
\begin{align}
\sum_{j=1}^k a_j \bv_j - \sum_{i=k+1}^n d_i \bv_i = \bzero.
\end{align}
\] Since the full set \(\{\bv_1, ..., \bv_n\}\) is linearly independent, every coefficient vanishes: \[
\begin{align}
a_1 = ... = a_k = d_{k+1} = ... = d_n = 0.
\end{align}
\] Therefore \(\{\bA\bv_{k+1}, ..., \bA\bv_n\}\) is a basis for \(\mathcal{R}(\bA)\), containing exactly \(n - k\) vectors. Hence \[
\begin{align}
r = n - k = n - \dim\mathcal{N}(\bA) \quad \Longrightarrow \quad \operatorname{rank}(\bA) + \dim \mathcal{N}(\bA) = n.
\end{align}
\]









NoteTheorem: Fundamental Theorem of Linear Algebra and Orthogonal Decompositions



For any matrix \(\bA \in \fR^{m \times n}\):

The row space and null space are orthogonal complements in \(\fR^n\): \[
\begin{align}
\mathcal{R}(\bA^T) \perp \mathcal{N}(\bA) \qquad \text{and} \qquad \fR^n = \mathcal{R}(\bA^T) \oplus \mathcal{N}(\bA).
\end{align}
\]
The column space and left null space are orthogonal complements in \(\fR^m\): \[
\begin{align}
\mathcal{R}(\bA) \perp \mathcal{N}(\bA^T) \qquad \text{and} \qquad \fR^m = \mathcal{R}(\bA) \oplus \mathcal{N}(\bA^T).
\end{align}
\]

Consequently, \(\dim \mathcal{R}(\bA^T) = \dim \mathcal{R}(\bA) = \operatorname{rank}(\bA)\).








TipProof





For any \(\bx\in\fR^n\), the condition \(\bA\bx=\bzero\) says that every row of \(\bA\) has zero inner product with \(\bx\). Since those rows span \(\mathcal{R}(\bA^T)\), \[
\begin{align}
\bx\in\mathcal{N}(\bA)
    \quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
    \bx\in\mathcal{R}(\bA^T)^\perp.
\end{align}
\] Taking orthogonal complements in finite-dimensional space gives \[
\begin{align}
\mathcal{R}(\bA^T)=\mathcal{N}(\bA)^\perp.
\end{align}
\] The identical argument with \(\bA^T\) gives \(\mathcal{R}(\bA)=\mathcal{N}(\bA^T)^\perp\). Each space therefore decomposes into one of these subspaces and its orthogonal complement. Finally, rank–nullity gives \[
\begin{align}
\dim\mathcal{R}(\bA^T)
    =n-\dim\mathcal{N}(\bA)
    =\operatorname{rank}(\bA),
\end{align}
\] so row rank equals column rank.









NoteTheorem: Solvability and the Left Null Space



The linear system \(\bA\bx = \bb\) has a solution if and only if \(\bb \in \mathcal{R}(\bA)\). Equivalently, \(\bA\bx = \bb\) is solvable if and only if \(\bb\) is orthogonal to every vector in the left null space of \(\bA\): \[
\begin{align}
\bA\bx = \bb \text{ has a solution} \iff \by^T\bb = 0 \quad \text{for all } \by \text{ such that } \bA^T\by = \bzero.
\end{align}
\] If a solution exists and \(\bx_p\) is one particular solution, the complete set of solutions is the affine space \[
\begin{align}
\bx = \bx_p + \bz, \qquad \bz \in \mathcal{N}(\bA).
\end{align}
\]








TipProof





By the referenced result, \(\fR^m = \mathcal{R}(\bA) \oplus \mathcal{N}(\bA^T)\). Therefore \(\bb \in \mathcal{R}(\bA)\) if and only if \(\bb \perp \mathcal{N}(\bA^T)\), which means \[
\begin{align}
\langle \by, \bb \rangle = \by^T\bb = 0 \qquad \text{for all } \by \in \mathcal{N}(\bA^T).
\end{align}
\] If \(\bA\bx_p = \bb\), then for any \(\bz \in \mathcal{N}(\bA)\), \[
\begin{align}
\bA(\bx_p + \bz) = \bA\bx_p + \bA\bz = \bb + \bzero = \bb.
\end{align}
\] Conversely, if \(\bA\bx = \bb\), then \[
\begin{align}
\bA(\bx - \bx_p) = \bA\bx - \bA\bx_p = \bb - \bb = \bzero,
\end{align}
\] which implies \(\bx - \bx_p \in \mathcal{N}(\bA)\), so \(\bx = \bx_p + \bz\) with \(\bz \in \mathcal{N}(\bA)\).









TipRemark



This compatibility condition is often called the finite-dimensional Fredholm alternative. Each left-null vector describes one obstruction that a solvable right-hand side must avoid.

NoteDefinition: Forward Error, Residual, and Backward Error



For a computed approximation \(\hat{\bx}\):

The forward error is the vector difference \(\Delta\bx = \hat{\bx} - \bx^*\), with relative measure \(\frac{\|\hat{\bx} - \bx^*\|}{\|\bx^*\|}\).
The residual is the vector defect \(\br = \bb - \bA\hat{\bx}\), with relative measure \(\frac{\|\br\|}{\|\bA\|\|\hat{\bx}\| + \|\bb\|}\).
The backward error is the smallest perturbation \((\delta\bA, \delta\bb)\) such that \(\hat{\bx}\) is the exact solution to the perturbed system \((\bA + \delta\bA)\hat{\bx} = \bb + \delta\bb\).









NoteTheorem: The Fundamental Residual-Error Bound



Let \(\bA \in \fR^{n \times n}\) be nonsingular, let \(\bb \neq \bzero\), and let \(\bx^*\) satisfy \(\bA\bx^* = \bb\). For any vector \(\hat{\bx}\) with residual \(\br = \bb - \bA\hat{\bx}\), the relative forward error satisfies \[
\begin{align}
\frac{1}{\kappa(\bA)}\frac{\|\br\|}{\|\bb\|}
    \leq
    \frac{\|\hat{\bx} - \bx^*\|}{\|\bx^*\|}
    \leq
    \kappa(\bA) \frac{\|\br\|}{\|\bb\|},
\end{align}
\] where \(\kappa(\bA) = \|\bA\|\|\bA^{-1}\|\) is the condition number of \(\bA\).








TipProof





Because \(\bb = \bA\bx^*\), the residual can be written in terms of the forward error as \(\br = \bb - \bA\hat{\bx} = \bA(\bx^* - \hat{\bx})\). Multiplying both sides by \(\bA^{-1}\) yields \(\bx^* - \hat{\bx} = \bA^{-1}\br\). Taking vector norms and applying the submultiplicative property of induced matrix norms gives \[
\begin{align}
\|\hat{\bx} - \bx^*\| \leq \|\bA^{-1}\| \|\br\|.
\end{align}
\] Furthermore, from \(\bb = \bA\bx^*\), we obtain \(\|\bb\| \leq \|\bA\| \|\bx^*\|\), which rearranges to \(\frac{1}{\|\bx^*\|} \leq \frac{\|\bA\|}{\|\bb\|}\). Multiplying these two inequalities produces \[
\begin{align}
\frac{\|\hat{\bx} - \bx^*\|}{\|\bx^*\|} \leq (\|\bA\| \|\bA^{-1}\|) \frac{\|\br\|}{\|\bb\|} = \kappa(\bA) \frac{\|\br\|}{\|\bb\|}.
\end{align}
\] For the lower bound, \(\br=\bA(\bx^*-\hat{\bx})\) gives \(\|\br\|\leq\|\bA\|\|\bx^*-\hat{\bx}\|\), while \(\bx^*=\bA^{-1}\bb\) gives \(\|\bx^*\|\leq\|\bA^{-1}\|\|\bb\|\). Combining these inequalities yields \[
\begin{align}
\frac{\|\hat{\bx}-\bx^*\|}{\|\bx^*\|}
    \geq
    \frac{1}{\|\bA\|\|\bA^{-1}\|}\frac{\|\br\|}{\|\bb\|}.
\end{align}
\]









NoteExample



(A small residual does not guarantee a small error) Consider the diagonal system \[
\begin{align}
\bA = \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 0 & 10^{-8} \end{pmatrix}, \qquad \bb = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 10^{-8} \end{pmatrix}.
\end{align}
\] The exact solution is \(\bx^* = (1, 1)^T\). Suppose an algorithm produces the approximation \(\hat{\bx} = (1, 0)^T\). The residual evaluates to \[
\begin{align}
\br = \bb - \bA\hat{\bx} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 10^{-8} \end{pmatrix} - \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 10^{-8} \end{pmatrix},
\end{align}
\] which satisfies \(\|\br\|_2 / \|\bb\|_2 \approx 10^{-8}\). However, the forward error is \(\hat{\bx} - \bx^* = (0, -1)^T\), yielding a relative forward error of \(\|\hat{\bx} - \bx^*\|_2 / \|\bx^*\|_2 = 1/\sqrt{2} \approx 0.707\) (a seventy percent error). The matrix has condition number \(\kappa_2(\bA) = 10^8\), which amplifies the small residual into an \(O(1)\) error in the solution.

7.2 Sensitivity to Perturbations in the Data
the referenced result measures the error of a given approximation through its residual. A related question asks how far the exact solution moves when the data \(\bA\) and \(\bb\) are perturbed. The answer is again governed by the condition number \(\kappa(\bA)=\|\bA\|\|\bA^{-1}\|\) of the referenced result. Perturbing \(\bA\) requires one preliminary fact, namely that a sufficiently small perturbation of a nonsingular matrix remains nonsingular.






NoteLemma: Perturbation of the Identity



Let \(\|\cdot\|\) be an induced matrix norm and let \(\bM\) be a square matrix with \(\|\bM\|&lt;1\). Then \(\bI+\bM\) is nonsingular and \[
\begin{align}
\|(\bI+\bM)^{-1}\|\leq\frac{1}{1-\|\bM\|}.
\end{align}
\]








TipProof





If \((\bI+\bM)\bz=\bzero\), then \(\|\bz\|=\|\bM\bz\|\leq\|\bM\|\|\bz\|\), which forces \(\bz=\bzero\) because \(\|\bM\|&lt;1\). Hence \(\bI+\bM\) is nonsingular. For any vector \(\by\), let \(\bz=(\bI+\bM)^{-1}\by\). Then \(\bz=\by-\bM\bz\), so \(\|\bz\|\leq\|\by\|+\|\bM\|\|\bz\|\). Rearranging gives \(\|\bz\|\leq\|\by\|/(1-\|\bM\|)\), and taking the maximum over unit vectors \(\by\) proves the bound.









NoteTheorem: Perturbation Bound for Linear Systems



Let \(\bA\) be nonsingular, let \(\bb\neq\bzero\), and let \(\bA\bx=\bb\). Suppose the perturbations \(\delta\bA\) and \(\delta\bb\) satisfy \(\kappa(\bA)\|\delta\bA\|/\|\bA\|&lt;1\). Then \(\bA+\delta\bA\) is nonsingular, and the solution of \((\bA+\delta\bA)(\bx+\delta\bx)=\bb+\delta\bb\) satisfies \[
\begin{align}
\frac{\|\delta\bx\|}{\|\bx\|}
    \leq
    \frac{\kappa(\bA)}{1-\kappa(\bA)\|\delta\bA\|/\|\bA\|}
    \left(\frac{\|\delta\bA\|}{\|\bA\|}+\frac{\|\delta\bb\|}{\|\bb\|}\right).
\end{align}
\]








TipProof





Factor \(\bA+\delta\bA=\bA(\bI+\bA^{-1}\delta\bA)\). Since \(\|\bA^{-1}\delta\bA\|\leq\|\bA^{-1}\|\|\delta\bA\|=\kappa(\bA)\|\delta\bA\|/\|\bA\|&lt;1\), the referenced result shows that \(\bA+\delta\bA\) is nonsingular. Subtracting \(\bA\bx=\bb\) from the perturbed equation gives \[
\begin{align}
(\bA+\delta\bA)\,\delta\bx=\delta\bb-\delta\bA\,\bx,
    \qquad\text{so}\qquad
    \delta\bx=(\bI+\bA^{-1}\delta\bA)^{-1}\bA^{-1}(\delta\bb-\delta\bA\,\bx).
\end{align}
\] the referenced result then gives \[
\begin{align}
\|\delta\bx\|\leq\frac{\|\bA^{-1}\|\big(\|\delta\bb\|+\|\delta\bA\|\|\bx\|\big)}{1-\|\bA^{-1}\|\|\delta\bA\|}.
\end{align}
\] Dividing by \(\|\bx\|\) and using \(\|\bb\|\leq\|\bA\|\|\bx\|\) to bound \(\|\delta\bb\|/\|\bx\|\) by \(\|\bA\|\|\delta\bb\|/\|\bb\|\) proves the stated inequality.



To first order, relative perturbations of size \(\varepsilon\) in \(\bA\) and in \(\bb\) change the solution by a relative amount of at most about \(2\kappa(\bA)\varepsilon\). The hypothesis \(\kappa(\bA)\|\delta\bA\|/\|\bA\|&lt;1\) cannot be dropped, because a larger perturbation may make \(\bA+\delta\bA\) singular. In the Euclidean norm, the smallest perturbation that does so has \(\|\delta\bA\|_2=\sigma_{\min}(\bA)=\|\bA\|_2/\kappa_2(\bA)\) by the referenced result. The reciprocal \(1/\kappa_2(\bA)\) is therefore the relative distance from \(\bA\) to the nearest singular matrix.

NoteDefinition: LU Factorization



Let \(\bA \in \fR^{n \times n}\). An LU factorization of \(\bA\) is a decomposition \[
\begin{align}
\bA = \bL\bU,
\end{align}
\] where \(\bL \in \fR^{n \times n}\) is unit lower triangular (\(\ell_{ii} = 1\) and \(\ell_{ij} = 0\) for \(j &gt; i\)) and \(\bU \in \fR^{n \times n}\) is upper triangular (\(u_{ij} = 0\) for \(i &gt; j\)).








NoteLemma: Algebra of Unit Lower Triangular Matrices



The set of \(n \times n\) unit lower triangular matrices forms a group under matrix multiplication. In particular:

The product of two unit lower triangular matrices is unit lower triangular.
The inverse of a unit lower triangular matrix is unit lower triangular.
The determinant of every unit lower triangular matrix equals one.









TipProof





Let \(\bL_1, \bL_2\) be unit lower triangular matrices. Entry \((i,j)\) of their product is \[
\begin{align}
(\bL_1 \bL_2)_{ij} = \sum_{k=1}^n (\bL_1)_{ik} (\bL_2)_{kj} = \sum_{k=j}^i (\bL_1)_{ik} (\bL_2)_{kj}.
\end{align}
\] If \(i &lt; j\), the summation range is empty, so \((\bL_1 \bL_2)_{ij} = 0\). For the diagonal entries where \(i = j\), the only surviving term is \(k = i\), yielding \((\bL_1 \bL_2)_{ii} = (\bL_1)_{ii} (\bL_2)_{ii} = 1 \cdot 1 = 1\). This proves that the product is unit lower triangular. To prove invertibility, let \(\bL\) be unit lower triangular. Because its determinant is the product of its diagonal entries, \(\det(\bL) = \prod_{i=1}^n 1 = 1 \neq 0\), so \(\bL^{-1}\) exists. Solving \(\bL \bX = \bI\) column by column using forward substitution reveals that each column \(\bx_j\) has zeros above row \(j\) and \(x_{jj} = 1\), which establishes that \(\bL^{-1}\) is unit lower triangular.









NoteTheorem: Elimination Matrices and the Structure of L



Let \(\bA^{(0)} = \bA\). At step \(k\) of Gaussian elimination, assuming the pivot entry \(a_{kk}^{(k-1)} \neq 0\), the subdiagonal entries in column \(k\) are annihilated by applying the elementary elimination matrix \[
\begin{align}
\bM_k = \bI - \boldsymbol{\ell}_k \be_k^T,
\end{align}
\] where \(\boldsymbol{\ell}_k = (0, ..., 0, \ell_{k+1,k}, ..., \ell_{n,k})^T\) contains the multipliers \(\ell_{ik} = a_{ik}^{(k-1)} / a_{kk}^{(k-1)}\). After \(n-1\) elimination steps, the upper triangular factor is \(\bU = \bM_{n-1} ... \bM_1 \bA\). The lower triangular factor is the inverse of the accumulated elimination operations: \[
\begin{align}
\bL = \bM_1^{-1} \bM_2^{-1} ... \bM_{n-1}^{-1} = \bI + \sum_{k=1}^{n-1} \boldsymbol{\ell}_k \be_k^T.
\end{align}
\] Consequently, the entries of \(\bL\) strictly below the main diagonal are precisely the multipliers computed during Gaussian elimination.








TipProof





The inverse of an elementary elimination matrix \(\bM_k = \bI - \boldsymbol{\ell}_k \be_k^T\) is \(\bM_k^{-1} = \bI + \boldsymbol{\ell}_k \be_k^T\). This identity follows from the fact that the first \(k\) entries of \(\boldsymbol{\ell}_k\) are zero, which implies \(\be_k^T \boldsymbol{\ell}_k = 0\) and therefore \[
\begin{align}
(\bI - \boldsymbol{\ell}_k \be_k^T)(\bI + \boldsymbol{\ell}_k \be_k^T) = \bI - \boldsymbol{\ell}_k \be_k^T + \boldsymbol{\ell}_k \be_k^T - \boldsymbol{\ell}_k (\be_k^T \boldsymbol{\ell}_k) \be_k^T = \bI.
\end{align}
\] Next, consider the product of two successive inverse matrices \(\bM_j^{-1} \bM_k^{-1}\) with \(j &lt; k\). Because \(\be_j^T \boldsymbol{\ell}_k = 0\), the cross term vanishes: \[
\begin{align}
(\bI + \boldsymbol{\ell}_j \be_j^T)(\bI + \boldsymbol{\ell}_k \be_k^T) = \bI + \boldsymbol{\ell}_j \be_j^T + \boldsymbol{\ell}_k \be_k^T + \boldsymbol{\ell}_j (\be_j^T \boldsymbol{\ell}_k) \be_k^T = \bI + \boldsymbol{\ell}_j \be_j^T + \boldsymbol{\ell}_k \be_k^T.
\end{align}
\] By induction, the product \(\bM_1^{-1} ... \bM_{n-1}^{-1}\) simply places each column of multipliers into column \(k\) of the identity matrix without modifying any previously placed multipliers. Multiplying \(\bU = \bM_{n-1} ... \bM_1 \bA\) on the left by \(\bL\) yields \(\bA = \bL\bU\).









NoteTheorem: Existence and Uniqueness of the LU Factorization



A matrix \(\bA \in \fR^{n \times n}\) has a unique LU factorization with unit lower triangular \(\bL\) and nonsingular upper triangular \(\bU\) if and only if every leading principal submatrix \(\bA_k = \bA[1:k, 1:k]\) is nonsingular for each \(k = 1, ..., n\).








TipProof





We proceed by induction on the matrix dimension \(n\). For \(n = 1\), \(\bA = (a_{11})\). Because \(\det(\bA_1) = a_{11} \neq 0\), the unique factorization is \(\bL = (1)\) and \(\bU = (a_{11})\). Assume the result holds for matrices of dimension \(n-1\). Partition the \(n \times n\) matrix \(\bA\) into blocks: \[
\begin{align}
\bA = \begin{pmatrix} \bA_{n-1} & \bc \\ \bd^T & a_{nn} \end{pmatrix},
\end{align}
\] where \(\bA_{n-1} \in \fR^{(n-1) \times (n-1)}\), \(\bc, \bd \in \fR^{n-1}\), and \(a_{nn} \in \fR\). By the induction hypothesis, \(\bA_{n-1}\) has a unique factorization \(\bA_{n-1} = \bL_{n-1} \bU_{n-1}\) with \(\bL_{n-1}\) unit lower triangular and \(\bU_{n-1}\) nonsingular. We seek block factors \[
\begin{align}
\bL = \begin{pmatrix} \bL_{n-1} & \bzero \\ \boldsymbol{\ell}^T & 1 \end{pmatrix}, \qquad
    \bU = \begin{pmatrix} \bU_{n-1} & \bu \\ \bzero^T & u_{nn} \end{pmatrix}.
\end{align}
\] Multiplying \(\bL\) and \(\bU\) and equating block by block gives: \[
\begin{align}
\bL_{n-1}\bu = \bc, \qquad \boldsymbol{\ell}^T \bU_{n-1} = \bd^T, \qquad \boldsymbol{\ell}^T\bu + u_{nn} = a_{nn}.
\end{align}
\] Because \(\bL_{n-1}\) is unit lower triangular (hence invertible), \(\bu = \bL_{n-1}^{-1}\bc\) is uniquely determined. Because \(\bU_{n-1}\) is nonsingular, \(\boldsymbol{\ell}^T = \bd^T \bU_{n-1}^{-1}\) is uniquely determined. Finally, the scalar diagonal entry is uniquely determined by \(u_{nn} = a_{nn} - \boldsymbol{\ell}^T\bu\). Taking determinants of the block decomposition gives \[
\begin{align}
\det(\bA) = \det(\bL)\det(\bU) = (1 \cdot \det(\bL_{n-1})) (\det(\bU_{n-1}) \cdot u_{nn}) = \det(\bA_{n-1}) u_{nn}.
\end{align}
\] Because \(\det(\bA) \neq 0\) and \(\det(\bA_{n-1}) \neq 0\), we have \(u_{nn} = \det(\bA)/\det(\bA_{n-1}) \neq 0\), which ensures that \(\bU\) is nonsingular. This completes the induction.

9.2 Cholesky Factorization






NoteTheorem: Cholesky Factorization



Every SPD matrix \(\bA\in\fR^{n\times n}\) has a unique factorization \[
\begin{align}
\bA=\bL\bL^T,
\end{align}
\] where \(\bL\) is lower triangular with positive diagonal entries.








TipProof





We proceed by induction on \(n\). For \(n=1\), \(\bA = (\alpha)\) with \(\alpha &gt; 0\), so \(\bL = (\sqrt{\alpha})\) is the unique positive square root. For the induction step, partition \(\bA \in \fR^{n \times n}\) as \[
\begin{align}
\bA = \begin{pmatrix} \alpha & \br^T \\ \br & \bB \end{pmatrix}.
\end{align}
\] Since \(\bA\) is SPD, \(\alpha = \be_1^T\bA\be_1 &gt; 0\). Set \(\ell_{11} = \sqrt{\alpha}\) and \(\boldsymbol{\ell} = \br / \ell_{11}\). Form the Schur complement \[
\begin{align}
\bS = \bB - \boldsymbol{\ell}\boldsymbol{\ell}^T = \bB - \frac{\br\br^T}{\alpha}.
\end{align}
\] For any nonzero \(\by \in \fR^{n-1}\), choose \(\bx = (-\boldsymbol{\ell}^T\by / \ell_{11}, \by^T)^T \in \fR^n\). Expanding the quadratic form: \[
\begin{align}
\bx^T\bA\bx
    &= \begin{pmatrix} -\frac{\boldsymbol{\ell}^T\by}{\ell_{11}} \\ \by \end{pmatrix}^T
       \begin{pmatrix} \alpha & \br^T \\ \br & \bB \end{pmatrix}
       \begin{pmatrix} -\frac{\boldsymbol{\ell}^T\by}{\ell_{11}} \\ \by \end{pmatrix} \\
    &= \alpha\left(\frac{\boldsymbol{\ell}^T\by}{\ell_{11}}\right)^2 - 2\frac{(\boldsymbol{\ell}^T\by)(\br^T\by)}{\ell_{11}} + \by^T\bB\by \\
    &= (\boldsymbol{\ell}^T\by)^2 - 2(\boldsymbol{\ell}^T\by)^2 + \by^T\bB\by \\
    &= \by^T(\bB - \boldsymbol{\ell}\boldsymbol{\ell}^T)\by = \by^T\bS\by.
\end{align}
\] Since \(\by \neq \bzero\), the vector \(\bx\) is nonzero, so \(\by^T\bS\by = \bx^T\bA\bx &gt; 0\). Hence \(\bS\) is SPD. By induction, \(\bS = \bK\bK^T\) for a unique lower triangular \(\bK\) with positive diagonal entries. Block multiplication gives \[
\begin{align}
\bA = \begin{pmatrix} \ell_{11} & \bzero^T \\ \boldsymbol{\ell} & \bK \end{pmatrix}
          \begin{pmatrix} \ell_{11} & \boldsymbol{\ell}^T \\ \bzero & \bK^T \end{pmatrix}
        = \begin{pmatrix} \ell_{11}^2 & \ell_{11}\boldsymbol{\ell}^T \\ \ell_{11}\boldsymbol{\ell} & \boldsymbol{\ell}\boldsymbol{\ell}^T + \bK\bK^T \end{pmatrix}
        = \begin{pmatrix} \alpha & \br^T \\ \br & \bB \end{pmatrix}.
\end{align}
\] The positivity of the diagonal requires \(\ell_{11} = \sqrt{\alpha}\), which uniquely fixes \(\boldsymbol{\ell} = \br / \ell_{11}\), and uniqueness of \(\bK\) makes the entire factor \(\bL\) unique.









NoteTheorem: Equivalent Characterizations of Positive Definiteness



For a real symmetric matrix \(\bA\), the following statements are equivalent.

\(\bx^T\bA\bx&gt;0\) for every nonzero \(\bx\).
\(\bA=\bL\bL^T\) for a lower triangular \(\bL\) with positive diagonal.
\(\bA=\bW^T\bW\) for a nonsingular matrix \(\bW\).
Every leading principal minor is positive.









TipProof





The implication from (1) to (2) is the referenced result. Statement (2) implies (3) by taking \(\bW=\bL^T\). If (3) holds, then \[
\begin{align}
\bx^T\bA\bx=\|\bW\bx\|_2^2&gt;0
\end{align}
\] for every nonzero \(\bx\), so (3) implies (1).
If (2) holds, the leading \(k\) by \(k\) block satisfies \(\bA_k=\bL_k\bL_k^T\). Hence \[
\begin{align}
\det(\bA_k)=\det(\bL_k)^2
    =\prod_{j=1}^k\ell_{jj}^2&gt;0,
\end{align}
\] which proves (4). Conversely, suppose every leading principal minor is positive. Apply the Cholesky formulas in order. At step \(k\), the next squared pivot \(v_k\) satisfies \[
\begin{align}
\det(\bA_k)=\left(\prod_{j=1}^{k-1}\ell_{jj}^2\right)v_k.
\end{align}
\] The determinant on the left and the preceding factors are positive, so \(v_k&gt;0\). Thus every square root exists and the algorithm produces (2).









TipRemark



After the spectral theorem is available, one more characterization follows directly. A real symmetric matrix is SPD exactly when all its eigenvalues are positive. The Cholesky argument does not need this fact, so we postpone it and keep the present argument self-contained.

9.3 The Cholesky Algorithm






NoteTheorem: The Cholesky Algorithm



The entries of the factor in \(\bA=\bL\bL^T\) are computed column by column: \[
\begin{align}
\ell_{jj}
    &=\sqrt{a_{jj}-\sum_{k=1}^{j-1}\ell_{jk}^2},\\
    \ell_{ij}
    &=\frac{a_{ij}-\sum_{k=1}^{j-1}\ell_{ik}\ell_{jk}}{\ell_{jj}},
    \qquad i=j+1,...,n.
\end{align}
\] For an SPD matrix every expression under a square root is positive. A nonpositive pivot signals that the input is not positive definite, apart from the effects of rounding.








TipProof





For \(i\geq j\), equating entry \((i,j)\) in \(\bA\) and \(\bL\bL^T\) gives \[
\begin{align}
a_{ij}=\sum_{k=1}^{j}\ell_{ik}\ell_{jk}.
\end{align}
\] When \(i=j\), solve this identity for the positive diagonal entry. When \(i&gt;j\), solve it for \(\ell_{ij}\). The order of computation ensures that all earlier terms are already known.









NoteExample



For \[
\begin{align}
\bA=\begin{pmatrix}4&2&-2\\2&10&2\\-2&2&6\end{pmatrix},
\end{align}
\] the algorithm gives \[
\begin{align}
\ell_{11}=2,&\quad \ell_{21}=1,\quad \ell_{31}=-1,\\
    \ell_{22}=3,&\quad \ell_{32}=1,\quad \ell_{33}=2.
\end{align}
\] Thus \[
\begin{align}
\bL=\begin{pmatrix}2&0&0\\1&3&0\\-1&1&2\end{pmatrix},
    \qquad \bL\bL^T=\bA.
\end{align}
\] To solve \(\bA\bx=\bb\), first solve \(\bL\by=\bb\) and then \(\bL^T\bx=\by\).

NoteTheorem: Sherman–Morrison Formula



Let \(\bA\in\fR^{n\times n}\) be nonsingular, and let \(\bu,\bv\in\fR^{n}\). The updated matrix \(\bA+\bu\bv^T\) is nonsingular if and only if \[
\begin{align}
1+\bv^T\bA^{-1}\bu\neq 0.
\end{align}
\] When this condition holds, \[
\begin{align}
(\bA+\bu\bv^T)^{-1}
    =\bA^{-1}-\frac{\bA^{-1}\bu\bv^T\bA^{-1}}
    {1+\bv^T\bA^{-1}\bu}.
\end{align}
\]








TipProof





Let \(\bz=\bA^{-1}\bu\) and \(\alpha=1+\bv^T\bz\). If \(\alpha=0\), then \[
\begin{align}
(\bA+\bu\bv^T)\bz
    =\bu+\bu\bv^T\bz
    =\alpha\bu
    =\bzero.
\end{align}
\] The vector \(\bz\) is nonzero because \(\alpha=0\) implies \(\bu\neq\bzero\). Thus the updated matrix is singular.
Now suppose \(\alpha\neq0\). Direct multiplication gives \[
\begin{align}
(\bA+\bu\bv^T)
  \left(\bA^{-1}-\frac{\bz\bv^T\bA^{-1}}{\alpha}\right)
  &=\bI+\bu\bv^T\bA^{-1}
    -\frac{\bu\bv^T\bA^{-1}}{\alpha}
    -\frac{\bu(\bv^T\bz)\bv^T\bA^{-1}}{\alpha} \\
  &=\bI+\bu\bv^T\bA^{-1}
    -\frac{1+\bv^T\bz}{\alpha}\bu\bv^T\bA^{-1}
   =\bI.
\end{align}
\] The displayed matrix is therefore the inverse of \(\bA+\bu\bv^T\).









NoteDefinition: Sherman–Morrison Solve



To solve \((\bA+\bu\bv^T)\bx=\bb\) from stored factors of \(\bA\):

Solve \(\bA\bz=\bu\) once for the update.
Compute \(\alpha=1+\bv^T\bz\). Refactor the updated matrix if \(\alpha\) is zero or so small that division by it would magnify roundoff.
For each right-hand side, solve \(\bA\by=\bb\).
Compute \[
\begin{align}
\bx=\by-\bz\frac{\bv^T\by}{\alpha}.
\end{align}
\]

The algorithm never forms \(\bA^{-1}\).








NoteExample



Let \[
\begin{align}
\bA=\begin{pmatrix}2&0\\0&3\end{pmatrix},\qquad
    \bu=\begin{pmatrix}1\\1\end{pmatrix},\qquad
    \bv=\begin{pmatrix}1\\-1\end{pmatrix},\qquad
    \bb=\begin{pmatrix}1\\0\end{pmatrix}.
\end{align}
\] The two solves with \(\bA\) give \(\bz=(1/2,1/3)^T\) and \(\by=(1/2,0)^T\). Since \(\alpha=1+1/2-1/3=7/6\), \[
\begin{align}
\bx=\by-\bz\frac{1/2}{7/6}
       =\begin{pmatrix}2/7\\-1/7\end{pmatrix}.
\end{align}
\] Multiplication by \(\bA+\bu\bv^T=\begin{pmatrix}3&-1\\1&2\end{pmatrix}\) verifies the solution.








TipRemark



(Cost of a rank-one change) With a dense LU factorization, preparing the correction requires one \(O(n^2)\) solve. Each new right-hand side requires one further solve and \(O(n)\) correction work. A fresh \(O(n^3)\) factorization is avoided. For a sparse factorization, the same comparison uses the measured cost of a triangular solve rather than the dense estimate.

10.5 The Symmetric Positive Definite Case
When the block-tridiagonal matrix is symmetric positive definite, block Cholesky removes the need for pivoting and stores only one off-diagonal side. The key fact is that every diagonal Schur complement remains positive definite.






NoteTheorem: Positive Definite Schur Complement



Let \[
\begin{align}
\bA=\begin{pmatrix}\bD&\bB^T\\\bB&\bC\end{pmatrix}
\end{align}
\] be symmetric positive definite. Then \(\bD\) is positive definite, and the Schur complement \[
\begin{align}
\bC-\bB\bD^{-1}\bB^T
\end{align}
\] is positive definite.








TipProof





For every nonzero \(\bx\), testing with \((\bx,\bzero)^T\) gives \[
\begin{align}
\begin{pmatrix}\bx\\\bzero\end{pmatrix}^T \bA \begin{pmatrix}\bx\\\bzero\end{pmatrix} = \bx^T\bD\bx &gt; 0,
\end{align}
\] so \(\bD\) is positive definite and \(\bD^{-1}\) exists. Now let \(\by \neq \bzero\), and choose \(\bx = -\bD^{-1}\bB^T\by\). Expanding the block quadratic form: \[
\begin{align}
\begin{pmatrix}\bx\\\by\end{pmatrix}^T
    \begin{pmatrix}\bD&\bB^T\\\bB&\bC\end{pmatrix}
    \begin{pmatrix}\bx\\\by\end{pmatrix}
    &= \bx^T\bD\bx + \bx^T\bB^T\by + \by^T\bB\bx + \by^T\bC\by \\
    &= (-\bD^{-1}\bB^T\by)^T\bD(-\bD^{-1}\bB^T\by) + 2(-\bD^{-1}\bB^T\by)^T\bB^T\by + \by^T\bC\by \\
    &= \by^T\bB\bD^{-1}\bB^T\by - 2\by^T\bB\bD^{-1}\bB^T\by + \by^T\bC\by \\
    &= \by^T(\bC - \bB\bD^{-1}\bB^T)\by.
\end{align}
\] Because \(\by \neq \bzero\), the compound vector \((\bx,\by)^T\) is nonzero. The positive definiteness of \(\bA\) implies that this quadratic form is strictly positive: \[
\begin{align}
\by^T(\bC - \bB\bD^{-1}\bB^T)\by &gt; 0.
\end{align}
\] The Schur complement \(\bC - \bB\bD^{-1}\bB^T\) is therefore positive definite.



Block Cholesky follows the same order as block LU. Factor \(\bS_k=\bF_k\bF_k^T\), solve \[
\begin{align}
\bC_k\bF_k^T=\bL_k,
\end{align}
\] and update \[
\begin{align}
\bS_{k+1}=\bD_{k+1}-\bC_k\bC_k^T.
\end{align}
\] The theorem above shows that every \(\bS_k\) remains positive definite. The stored factors form a block lower-bidiagonal Cholesky factor of \(\bA\).

11.3 Householder Triangularization
The standard method for computing the QR factorization of a dense matrix is the Householder reflection technique. At each step, a Householder reflector is constructed to zero out all subdiagonal entries of a column simultaneously (Householder 1958).






NoteDefinition: Householder Reflector



Let \(\bv \in \fR^m\) be a nonzero vector. The Householder reflector associated with \(\bv\) is the matrix \[
\begin{align}
\bH = \bI - 2 \frac{\bv\bv^T}{\bv^T\bv}.
\end{align}
\] Geometrically, \(\bH\) reflects any vector across the hyperplane orthogonal to \(\bv\). Every Householder reflector is symmetric (\(\bH^T = \bH\)) and orthogonal (\(\bH^T\bH = \bH^2 = \bI\)).








NoteTheorem: Householder Vector Construction



Given a vector \(\bx \in \fR^m\), we wish to find a reflector \(\bH\) that aligns \(\bx\) with the first canonical coordinate axis: \(\bH\bx = \pm \|\bx\|_2 \be_1\). The vector \(\bv\) is chosen as \[
\begin{align}
\bv = \bx + \operatorname{sign}(x_1) \|\bx\|_2 \be_1,
\end{align}
\] where \(\operatorname{sign}(x_1) = 1\) if \(x_1 \geq 0\) and \(-1\) if \(x_1 &lt; 0\). Choosing the sign of the first component ensures that the two added terms have identical signs and avoids cancellation when the first component of \(\bv\) is formed.








TipProof





Let \(\bv = \bx - \alpha \be_1\). Because \(\bH\) preserves vector lengths, we must have \(\|\bH\bx\|_2 = \|\bx\|_2\), which requires \(\alpha = \pm \|\bx\|_2\). Computing \(\bv^T\bv\) gives \[
\begin{align}
\bv^T\bv = (\bx - \alpha \be_1)^T(\bx - \alpha \be_1) = \|\bx\|_2^2 - 2\alpha x_1 + \alpha^2 = 2 \|\bx\|_2^2 - 2\alpha x_1 = 2 \bv^T\bx.
\end{align}
\] Applying \(\bH\) to \(\bx\) yields \[
\begin{align}
\bH\bx = \bx - 2 \frac{\bv^T\bx}{\bv^T\bv} \bv = \bx - \bv = \alpha \be_1 = \pm \|\bx\|_2 \be_1.
\end{align}
\] To ensure numerical stability in floating-point arithmetic, we choose \(\alpha = -\operatorname{sign}(x_1)\|\bx\|_2\), so that \(v_1 = x_1 + \operatorname{sign}(x_1)\|\bx\|_2\) avoids subtracting nearly equal quantities.









TipRemark



(Matrix-free application and complexity) In numerical algorithms, a Householder matrix \(\bH\) is usually stored through its defining vector rather than as an explicit \(m \times m\) array. To apply \(\bH\) to a vector \(\by\), we compute \[
\begin{align}
\bH\by = \by - \left( \frac{2\bv^T\by}{\bv^T\bv} \right) \bv,
\end{align}
\] which requires only an inner product and a vector update costing \(O(m)\) flops. For a matrix \(\bA \in \fR^{m \times n}\), Householder QR requires \(2mn^2 - \frac{2}{3}n^3\) flops.

NoteDefinition: Linear Least-Squares Problem



For \(\bA\in\fR^{m\times n}\) and \(\bb\in\fR^m\), the linear least-squares problem is \[
\begin{align}
\min_{\bx\in\fR^n}\|\bA\bx-\bb\|_2^2
    =
    \min_{\bx\in\fR^n}
    \sum_{i=1}^m
    \left(\sum_{j=1}^n a_{ij}x_j-b_i\right)^2.
\end{align}
\] The residual associated with \(\bx\) is \(\br(\bx)=\bb-\bA\bx\).








NoteTheorem: Orthogonal Characterization and Normal Equations



A vector \(\widehat{\bx}\) is a least-squares minimizer if and only if its residual is orthogonal to every column of \(\bA\): \[
\begin{align}
\br=\bb-\bA\widehat{\bx}
    \perp\mathcal{R}(\bA).
\end{align}
\] Equivalently, \[
\begin{align}
\bA^T\br=\bzero,
    \qquad
    \bA^T\bA\widehat{\bx}=\bA^T\bb.
\end{align}
\] These are the normal equations. A minimizer is unique exactly when the columns of \(\bA\) are linearly independent.








TipProof





Decompose \(\bb-\bA\bx\) by inserting the candidate fitted vector \(\bA\widehat{\bx}\): \[
\begin{align}
\bb-\bA\bx
    &= (\bb-\bA\widehat{\bx}) + \bA(\widehat{\bx}-\bx).
\end{align}
\] If \(\bb-\bA\widehat{\bx}\perp\mathcal{R}(\bA)\), then since \(\bA(\widehat{\bx}-\bx)\in\mathcal{R}(\bA)\), the two summands are orthogonal: \[
\begin{align}
(\bb-\bA\widehat{\bx})^T\bA(\widehat{\bx}-\bx) = 0.
\end{align}
\] The Pythagorean identity therefore yields \[
\begin{align}
\|\bb-\bA\bx\|_2^2
    &= \|(\bb-\bA\widehat{\bx}) + \bA(\widehat{\bx}-\bx)\|_2^2 \\
    &= \|\bb-\bA\widehat{\bx}\|_2^2 + \|\bA(\widehat{\bx}-\bx)\|_2^2
    \ge \|\bb-\bA\widehat{\bx}\|_2^2.
\end{align}
\] Equality holds if and only if \(\|\bA(\widehat{\bx}-\bx)\|_2^2=0\), meaning \(\bA(\widehat{\bx}-\bx)=\bzero\). Thus \(\widehat{\bx}\) minimizes the residual norm.
Conversely, if \(\widehat{\bx}\) is a minimizer, define for any \(\bv\in\fR^n\) and \(t\in\fR\) the scalar function \[
\begin{align}
\phi(t)
    &= \|\bb-\bA(\widehat{\bx}+t\bv)\|_2^2 \\
    &= \|\br - t\bA\bv\|_2^2 \\
    &= \|\br\|_2^2 - 2t\,\bv^T\bA^T\br + t^2\|\bA\bv\|_2^2,
\end{align}
\] where \(\br=\bb-\bA\widehat{\bx}\). Because \(\phi\) has a global minimum at \(t=0\), \[
\begin{align}
\phi'(0) = -2\bv^T\bA^T\br = 0
    \quad\text{for all }\bv\in\fR^n.
\end{align}
\] Choosing \(\bv = \be_j\) for \(j=1,...,n\) gives \(\bA^T\br=\bzero\), which means \(\br\perp\mathcal{R}(\bA)\). Expanding \(\bA^T(\bb-\bA\widehat{\bx})=\bzero\) yields the normal equations \[
\begin{align}
\bA^T\bA\widehat{\bx}=\bA^T\bb.
\end{align}
\]
For uniqueness, suppose \(\widehat{\bx}_1\) and \(\widehat{\bx}_2\) are both minimizers. Then \(\bA(\widehat{\bx}_1-\widehat{\bx}_2)=\bzero\), so \(\widehat{\bx}_1-\widehat{\bx}_2\in\mathcal{N}(\bA)\). When \(\bA\) has full column rank, \(\mathcal{N}(\bA)=\{\bzero\}\), forcing \(\widehat{\bx}_1=\widehat{\bx}_2\). If \(\bz\in\mathcal{N}(\bA)\setminus\{\bzero\}\), then \(\bA(\widehat{\bx}+\bz)=\bA\widehat{\bx}\), so \(\widehat{\bx}+\bz\) is also a minimizer.









TipRemark



(Normal Equations via Differentiation) For \(f(\bx)=\frac12\|\bA\bx-\bb\|_2^2\), the gradient is \(\nabla f(\bx)=\bA^T(\bA\bx-\bb)\). Setting \(\nabla f(\bx)=\bzero\) recovers \(\bA^T\bA\bx=\bA^T\bb\). The Pythagorean argument reveals why the condition is sufficient and geometrically necessary.








TipRemark



(Conditioning of Normal Equations) When \(\bA\) has full column rank, \(\bA^T\bA\) is SPD because \[
\begin{align}
\bx^T\bA^T\bA\bx=\|\bA\bx\|_2^2&gt;0
\end{align}
\] for nonzero \(\bx\). This proves that the normal equations have a unique solution.









NoteTheorem: Least-Squares Solution by QR



Let \(\bA\in\fR^{m\times n}\) have full column rank and thin QR factorization \(\bA=\bQ_1\bR_1\). Then the unique least-squares solution is obtained from \[
\begin{align}
\bR_1\widehat{\bx}=\bQ_1^T\bb.
\end{align}
\] The fitted vector and residual are \[
\begin{align}
\widehat{\bb}=\bQ_1\bQ_1^T\bb,
    \qquad
    \br=(\bI-\bQ_1\bQ_1^T)\bb.
\end{align}
\]








TipProof





Let \(\bQ = (\bQ_1  \bQ_2)\in\fR^{m\times m}\) be orthogonal, where \(\bQ_1\in\fR^{m\times n}\) and \(\bQ_2\in\fR^{m\times (m-n)}\). Since \(\bQ^T\bQ=\bI\), the Euclidean norm is orthogonally invariant: \[
\begin{align}
\|\bA\bx-\bb\|_2^2
    &= \|\bQ^T(\bA\bx-\bb)\|_2^2 \\
    &= \left\|
    \begin{pmatrix}\bQ_1^T\\\bQ_2^T\end{pmatrix}(\bQ_1\bR_1\bx - \bb)
    \right\|_2^2 \\
    &= \left\|
    \begin{pmatrix}\bR_1\bx - \bQ_1^T\bb\\ -\bQ_2^T\bb\end{pmatrix}
    \right\|_2^2 \\
    &= \|\bR_1\bx-\bQ_1^T\bb\|_2^2 + \|\bQ_2^T\bb\|_2^2.
\end{align}
\] The second term \(\|\bQ_2^T\bb\|_2^2\) is fixed and independent of \(\bx\). Because \(\bA\) has full column rank, \(\bR_1\) is non-singular, and setting \(\bR_1\widehat{\bx}=\bQ_1^T\bb\) reduces the first term to zero, attaining the minimum value \(\|\bQ_2^T\bb\|_2^2\). The fitted vector and residual follow: \[
\begin{align}
\widehat{\bb} &= \bA\widehat{\bx} = \bQ_1\bR_1(\bR_1^{-1}\bQ_1^T\bb) = \bQ_1\bQ_1^T\bb, \\
    \br &= \bb - \widehat{\bb} = (\bI - \bQ_1\bQ_1^T)\bb.
\end{align}
\]









TipRemark



(Geometry vs. Conditioning in Householder QR) For a matrix with full column rank, the referenced result proves \(\kappa_2(\bA^T\bA)=\kappa_2(\bA)^2\). Householder QR avoids forming \(\bA^T\bA\) and is the safer dense default. The normal equations remain useful when special structure, cost, or repeated updates make the trade worthwhile.








NoteExample



Fitting \(y=c_0+c_1t\) to \((0,1)\), \((1,2)\), and \((2,4)\) gives \[
\begin{align}
\bA=\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\1&1\\1&2\end{pmatrix},
    \qquad
    \bb=\begin{pmatrix}1\\2\\4\end{pmatrix}.
\end{align}
\] The solution is \(c_0=5/6\) and \(c_1=3/2\). The residual is orthogonal to both the constant column and the time column. Hence its entries sum to zero and have zero first moment with respect to the sample times.

12.2 Rank-Deficient Problems






NoteTheorem: All Minimizers and the Minimum-Norm Choice



Let \(\widehat{\bx}\) be any least-squares minimizer. Then every minimizer has the form \[
\begin{align}
\widehat{\bx}+\bz,
    \qquad \bz\in\mathcal{N}(\bA).
\end{align}
\] Among them there is exactly one vector of smallest Euclidean norm. It is characterized by \[
\begin{align}
\widehat{\bx}_{\min}\perp\mathcal{N}(\bA),
    \qquad\text{equivalently}\qquad
    \widehat{\bx}_{\min}\in\mathcal{R}(\bA^T).
\end{align}
\]








TipProof





Let \(\widehat{\bx}\) be any minimizer. For any \(\bz\in\mathcal{N}(\bA)\), \(\bA(\widehat{\bx}+\bz)=\bA\widehat{\bx}\), so \(\widehat{\bx}+\bz\) is also a minimizer. Conversely, if \(\bx'\) is any minimizer, then \(\bA\bx'=\bA\widehat{\bx}\) by uniqueness of the orthogonal projection \(\widehat{\bb}\), which gives \(\bA(\bx'-\widehat{\bx})=\bzero\), so \(\bx'-\widehat{\bx}\in\mathcal{N}(\bA)\).
Every vector decomposes uniquely as \(\bx = \bx_{\perp} + \bx_0\) with \(\bx_0\in\mathcal{N}(\bA)\) and \(\bx_{\perp}\in\mathcal{N}(\bA)^\perp\). For any minimizer \(\widehat{\bx} + \bz\), we have \(\bz\in\mathcal{N}(\bA)\), so \[
\begin{align}
\|\widehat{\bx}+\bz\|_2^2
    &= \|(\widehat{\bx}_{\perp} + \widehat{\bx}_0) + \bz\|_2^2 \\
    &= \|\widehat{\bx}_{\perp} + (\widehat{\bx}_0 + \bz)\|_2^2 \\
    &= \|\widehat{\bx}_{\perp}\|_2^2 + \|\widehat{\bx}_0 + \bz\|_2^2
    \ge \|\widehat{\bx}_{\perp}\|_2^2.
\end{align}
\] The minimum is attained uniquely when \(\bz = -\widehat{\bx}_0\), which yields \(\widehat{\bx}_{\min} = \widehat{\bx}_{\perp}\in\mathcal{N}(\bA)^\perp\). By the fundamental theorem of linear algebra, \(\mathcal{N}(\bA)^\perp = \mathcal{R}(\bA^T)\).









NoteDefinition: Tikhonov Regularization



For \(\lambda&gt;0\), Tikhonov or ridge regularization solves \[
\begin{align}
\min_{\bx}
    \left(\|\bA\bx-\bb\|_2^2+\lambda\|\bx\|_2^2\right).
\end{align}
\] It trades residual fit against parameter size. The problem is equivalently the augmented least-squares problem \[
\begin{align}
\min_{\bx}
    \left\|
    \begin{pmatrix}\bA\\\sqrt{\lambda}\bI\end{pmatrix}\bx
    -
    \begin{pmatrix}\bb\\\bzero\end{pmatrix}
    \right\|_2^2,
\end{align}
\] and its normal equations are \[
\begin{align}
(\bA^T\bA+\lambda\bI)\bx=\bA^T\bb.
\end{align}
\]








TipRemark



(Interpretation of Regularization) Regularization also adds a modeling assumption. Among models with similar fit, it prefers smaller parameter vectors. Whether that assumption is scientifically appropriate depends on the meaning and scaling of the parameters. the referenced result identifies the directions that regularization damps and the amount by which each is damped.

13.2 Multiplicity and Schur Form






NoteDefinition: Algebraic and Geometric Multiplicity



Let \(\lambda\) be an eigenvalue of \(\bA\).

The algebraic multiplicity \(\mu_a(\lambda)\) is the multiplicity of \(\lambda\) as a root of \(\det(\bA - z\bI) = 0\).
The geometric multiplicity \(\mu_g(\lambda) = \dim \mathcal{N}(\bA - \lambda\bI)\) is the dimension of the corresponding eigenspace.

Every eigenvalue satisfies \(1 \leq \mu_g(\lambda) \leq \mu_a(\lambda)\). When \(\mu_g(\lambda) &lt; \mu_a(\lambda)\) for any eigenvalue, \(\bA\) is defective and does not possess a complete basis of eigenvectors.








NoteTheorem: Schur Triangularization Decomposition



Every square matrix \(\bA \in \fC^{n \times n}\) is unitarily similar to an upper triangular matrix: \[
\begin{align}
\bA = \bQ\bT\bQ^*,
\end{align}
\] where \(\bQ \in \fC^{n \times n}\) is unitary (\(\bQ^*\bQ = \bI\)) and \(\bT \in \fC^{n \times n}\) is upper triangular. The diagonal entries of \(\bT\) are the eigenvalues of \(\bA\).








TipProof





We proceed by induction on the matrix dimension \(n\). For \(n = 1\), the statement holds trivially with \(\bQ = [1]\) and \(\bT = \bA\).
Assume the theorem holds for all square matrices of order \(n - 1\). By the Fundamental Theorem of Algebra, the characteristic polynomial \(\det(\bA - z\bI) = 0\) possesses at least one complex root \(\lambda_1\). Let \(\bv_1 \in \fC^n\) be an eigenvector associated with \(\lambda_1\), normalized so that \(\|\bv_1\|_2 = 1\). Using the Gram–Schmidt process or Householder reflectors, construct an orthonormal basis for \(\fC^n\) whose first column is \(\bv_1\), forming a unitary matrix \(\bU_1 = [\bv_1, \bW] \in \fC^{n \times n}\) with \(\bW \in \fC^{n \times (n-1)}\).
Applying this unitary similarity transformation to \(\bA\) gives \[
\begin{align}
\bU_1^* \bA \bU_1
    =
    \begin{pmatrix} \bv_1^* \\ \bW^* \end{pmatrix} \bA \begin{pmatrix} \bv_1 & \bW \end{pmatrix}
    =
    \begin{pmatrix} \bv_1^* \bA \bv_1 & \bv_1^* \bA \bW \\ \bW^* \bA \bv_1 & \bW^* \bA \bW \end{pmatrix}.
\end{align}
\] Because \(\bA\bv_1 = \lambda_1 \bv_1\), the leading entry is \(\bv_1^*(\lambda_1\bv_1) = \lambda_1 \|\bv_1\|_2^2 = \lambda_1\). The lower-left block is \(\bW^*(\lambda_1\bv_1) = \lambda_1(\bW^*\bv_1) = \bzero\), because the columns of \(\bW\) are orthogonal to \(\bv_1\). Thus \[
\begin{align}
\bU_1^* \bA \bU_1 = \begin{pmatrix} \lambda_1 & \bw^* \\ \bzero & \bA_1 \end{pmatrix},
\end{align}
\] where \(\bA_1 = \bW^* \bA \bW \in \fC^{(n-1) \times (n-1)}\) and \(\bw^* = \bv_1^* \bA \bW\).
By the induction hypothesis, there exists a unitary matrix \(\bQ_1 \in \fC^{(n-1) \times (n-1)}\) such that \(\bQ_1^* \bA_1 \bQ_1 = \bT_1\) is upper triangular. Define the \(n \times n\) block-diagonal unitary matrix \[
\begin{align}
\bV = \begin{pmatrix} 1 & \bzero \\ \bzero & \bQ_1 \end{pmatrix}.
\end{align}
\] Setting \(\bQ = \bU_1 \bV\), which is unitary as the product of unitary matrices, we obtain \[
\begin{align}
\bQ^* \bA \bQ
    =
    \bV^* (\bU_1^* \bA \bU_1) \bV
    =
    \begin{pmatrix} 1 & \bzero \\ \bzero & \bQ_1^* \end{pmatrix}
    \begin{pmatrix} \lambda_1 & \bw^* \\ \bzero & \bA_1 \end{pmatrix}
    \begin{pmatrix} 1 & \bzero \\ \bzero & \bQ_1 \end{pmatrix}
    =
    \begin{pmatrix} \lambda_1 & \bw^* \bQ_1 \\ \bzero & \bQ_1^* \bA_1 \bQ_1 \end{pmatrix}
    =
    \begin{pmatrix} \lambda_1 & \bw^* \bQ_1 \\ \bzero & \bT_1 \end{pmatrix}.
\end{align}
\] Because \(\bT_1\) is upper triangular, the entire block matrix \(\bT = \bQ^*\bA\bQ\) is upper triangular. The diagonal entries of \(\bT\) are \(\lambda_1\) and the eigenvalues of \(\bA_1\), which collectively form the eigenvalues of \(\bA\).









NoteTheorem: Spectral Theorem for Real Symmetric Matrices



If \(\bA \in \fR^{n \times n}\) is real symmetric (\(\bA = \bA^T\)), then all eigenvalues of \(\bA\) are real, and \(\bA\) is orthogonally diagonalizable: \[
\begin{align}
\bA = \bQ \boldsymbol{\Lambda} \bQ^T = \sum_{i=1}^n \lambda_i \bq_i \bq_i^T,
\end{align}
\] where \(\bQ \in \fR^{n \times n}\) is real orthogonal (\(\bQ^T\bQ = \bI\)) and \(\boldsymbol{\Lambda} = \operatorname{diag}(\lambda_1, ..., \lambda_n)\) is real diagonal.








TipProof





First, let \(\lambda \in \fC\) and \(\bv \in \fC^n \setminus \{\bzero\}\) satisfy \(\bA\bv = \lambda\bv\). Taking the inner product with \(\bv\), \[
\begin{align}
\bv^*\bA\bv = \bv^*(\lambda\bv) = \lambda\|\bv\|_2^2.
\end{align}
\] Taking the conjugate transpose of this scalar and using \(\bA = \bA^T = \bA^*\), \[
\begin{align}
\bv^*\bA\bv = (\bv^*\bA\bv)^* = (\lambda\|\bv\|_2^2)^* = \bar{\lambda}\|\bv\|_2^2.
\end{align}
\] Subtracting these two scalar expressions gives \[
\begin{align}
(\lambda - \bar{\lambda})\|\bv\|_2^2 = 0.
\end{align}
\] Because \(\bv \neq \bzero\), \(\|\bv\|_2^2 &gt; 0\), forcing \(\lambda = \bar{\lambda} \in \fR\). Thus all eigenvalues are real.
Because \(\lambda_1 \in \fR\) and \(\bA\) is real, writing \(\bv = \bx + i\by\) gives \(\bA\bx = \lambda_1\bx\) and \(\bA\by = \lambda_1\by\). At least one of \(\bx,\by\) is nonzero, yielding a real unit eigenvector \(\bq_1\). Extend \(\bq_1\) to an orthogonal matrix \(\bQ_1 = [\bq_1, \bW] \in \fR^{n \times n}\) with \(\bW \in \fR^{n \times (n-1)}\). Because \(\bA\bq_1 = \lambda_1\bq_1\) and \(\bW^T\bq_1 = \bzero\): \[
\begin{align}
\bQ_1^T\bA\bQ_1
    =
    \begin{pmatrix} \bq_1^T \\ \bW^T \end{pmatrix} \bA \begin{pmatrix} \bq_1 & \bW \end{pmatrix}
    =
    \begin{pmatrix} \bq_1^T\bA\bq_1 & \bq_1^T\bA\bW \\ \bW^T\bA\bq_1 & \bW^T\bA\bW \end{pmatrix}
    =
    \begin{pmatrix} \lambda_1 & (\bW^T\bA\bq_1)^T \\ \bzero & \bA_1 \end{pmatrix}
    =
    \begin{pmatrix} \lambda_1 & \bzero^T \\ \bzero & \bA_1 \end{pmatrix},
\end{align}
\] where \(\bA_1 = \bW^T\bA\bW \in \fR^{(n-1)\times(n-1)}\) is symmetric. By induction on dimension, there exists an orthogonal \(\bQ_2 \in \fR^{(n-1)\times(n-1)}\) such that \(\bQ_2^T\bA_1\bQ_2 = \operatorname{diag}(\lambda_2, ..., \lambda_n)\). The matrix \[
\begin{align}
\bQ = \bQ_1 \begin{pmatrix} 1 & \bzero \\ \bzero & \bQ_2 \end{pmatrix}
\end{align}
\] is orthogonal and satisfies \(\bQ^T\bA\bQ = \boldsymbol{\Lambda}\), so \(\bA = \bQ\boldsymbol{\Lambda}\bQ^T\).









NoteTheorem: Courant–Fischer Minimax Theorem



Let \(\bA \in \fR^{n \times n}\) be real symmetric with eigenvalues ordered as \(\lambda_1 \leq \lambda_2 \leq ... \leq \lambda_n\). Then for each \(k \in \{1, ..., n\}\), \[
\begin{align}
\lambda_k = \min_{\dim(S) = k} \max_{\bx \in S,\, \bx \neq \bzero} \frac{\bx^T \bA \bx}{\bx^T \bx}
    =
    \max_{\dim(S) = n - k + 1} \min_{\bx \in S,\, \bx \neq \bzero} \frac{\bx^T \bA \bx}{\bx^T \bx}.
\end{align}
\]








TipProof





Let \(\bq_1, ..., \bq_n\) be an orthonormal basis of eigenvectors of \(\bA\) with \(\bA\bq_i = \lambda_i \bq_i\). For any nonzero \(\bx = \sum_{i=1}^n c_i \bq_i\), the Rayleigh quotient satisfies \[
\begin{align}
R(\bx) = \frac{\bx^T\bA\bx}{\bx^T\bx} = \frac{\sum_{i=1}^n \lambda_i c_i^2}{\sum_{i=1}^n c_i^2}.
\end{align}
\] Define the subspace \(V_k = \operatorname{span}\{\bq_1, ..., \bq_k\}\), which has dimension \(k\). For any nonzero \(\bx \in V_k\), \(c_i = 0\) for all \(i &gt; k\), so \[
\begin{align}
R(\bx) = \frac{\sum_{i=1}^k \lambda_i c_i^2}{\sum_{i=1}^k c_i^2} \leq \lambda_k \frac{\sum_{i=1}^k c_i^2}{\sum_{i=1}^k c_i^2} = \lambda_k.
\end{align}
\] Because \(\bq_k \in V_k\) achieves \(R(\bq_k) = \lambda_k\), we have \(\max_{\bx \in V_k,\, \bx \neq \bzero} R(\bx) = \lambda_k\).
Now let \(S\) be any arbitrary subspace of \(\fR^n\) with \(\dim(S) = k\). Define \(W_{k-1} = \operatorname{span}\{\bq_k, ..., \bq_n\}\), which has dimension \(n - k + 1\). By the dimension formula, \[
\begin{align}
\dim(S \cap W_{k-1}) = \dim(S) + \dim(W_{k-1}) - \dim(S + W_{k-1}) \geq k + (n - k + 1) - n = 1.
\end{align}
\] Therefore, there exists a nonzero vector \(\by \in S \cap W_{k-1}\). Because \(\by \in W_{k-1}\), its expansion \(\by = \sum_{i=k}^n d_i \bq_i\) contains only modes \(i \geq k\), so \[
\begin{align}
R(\by) = \frac{\sum_{i=k}^n \lambda_i d_i^2}{\sum_{i=k}^n d_i^2} \geq \lambda_k \frac{\sum_{i=k}^n d_i^2}{\sum_{i=k}^n d_i^2} = \lambda_k.
\end{align}
\] Because \(\by \in S\), the maximum over \(S\) satisfies \(\max_{\bx \in S,\, \bx \neq \bzero} R(\bx) \geq R(\by) \geq \lambda_k\). Taking the minimum over all \(k\)-dimensional subspaces \(S\) proves the first equality. The dual maximum-minimum characterization follows similarly by replacing \(\bA\) with \(-\bA\).









NoteTheorem: Cauchy Interlacing Theorem



Let \(\bA\in\fR^{n\times n}\) be symmetric, and let \(\bB\in\fR^{(n-1)\times(n-1)}\) be a principal submatrix. If \[
\begin{align}
\lambda_1\leq\cdots\leq\lambda_n,
    \qquad
    \mu_1\leq\cdots\leq\mu_{n-1}
\end{align}
\] are the eigenvalues of \(\bA\) and \(\bB\), respectively, then \[
\begin{align}
\lambda_k\leq\mu_k\leq\lambda_{k+1},
    \qquad k=1,...,n-1.
\end{align}
\]








TipProof





After a simultaneous permutation of rows and columns, we may assume that \(\bB\) is obtained by deleting the last row and column. Identify \(\fR^{n-1}\) with the coordinate hyperplane \[
\begin{align}
H=\{\bx\in\fR^n:x_n=0\}.
\end{align}
\] For every nonzero \(\bx\in H\), the Rayleigh quotients of \(\bA\) and \(\bB\) agree.
Apply the first Courant–Fischer characterization from the referenced result. The minimization defining \(\mu_k\) ranges only over \(k\)-dimensional subspaces of \(H\), whereas the minimization defining \(\lambda_k\) ranges over all \(k\)-dimensional subspaces of \(\fR^n\). Restricting the admissible set cannot decrease the minimum, so \(\lambda_k\leq\mu_k\).
For the other inequality, use the dual characterization. Since \(\dim(H)=n-1\), \[
\begin{align}
\mu_k
    &=\max_{\substack{S\subset H\\ \dim(S)=n-k}}
      \min_{\bx\in S,\,\bx\neq\bzero}R_{\bA}(\bx)
    \leq\max_{\substack{S\subset\fR^n\\ \dim(S)=n-k}}
      \min_{\bx\in S,\,\bx\neq\bzero}R_{\bA}(\bx)
      =\lambda_{k+1}.
\end{align}
\] Together the two bounds give the interlacing inequalities.

NoteDefinition: Power Iteration



Let \(\bA\) be real symmetric. Given a unit vector \(\bv^{(0)}\), power iteration constructs \[
\begin{align}
\bw^{(k)} = \bA \bv^{(k-1)}, \qquad \bv^{(k)} = \frac{\bw^{(k)}}{\|\bw^{(k)}\|_2}, \qquad \lambda^{(k)} = (\bv^{(k)})^T \bA \bv^{(k)}.
\end{align}
\] Order the eigenvalues by magnitude. If \(|\lambda_1|&gt;|\lambda_2|\geq\cdots\geq|\lambda_n|\) and \(\bq_1^T\bv^{(0)}\neq0\), the direction of the iterate converges linearly: \[
\begin{align}
\|\bv^{(k)} - (\pm \bq_1)\|_2 = O\left( \left| \frac{\lambda_2}{\lambda_1} \right|^k \right).
\end{align}
\] For a general nonnormal matrix, an eigenvector basis may be poorly conditioned or may not exist, and this simple orthogonal expansion no longer justifies the claim.








NoteDefinition: Shifted Inverse Iteration



For a real symmetric matrix, an eigenvalue near a target \(\sigma\) can be isolated by applying power iteration implicitly to \((\bA-\sigma\bI)^{-1}\): \[
\begin{align}
(\bA - \sigma \bI) \bw^{(k)} = \bv^{(k-1)}, \qquad \bv^{(k)} = \frac{\bw^{(k)}}{\|\bw^{(k)}\|_2}.
\end{align}
\] The eigenvalues of \((\bA - \sigma \bI)^{-1}\) are \((\lambda_i - \sigma)^{-1}\). Provided the shift is not itself an eigenvalue and the starting vector contains the desired component, the mode closest to \(\sigma\) dominates. The matrix inverse is never formed. Each step solves a shifted linear system instead.








NoteDefinition: Rayleigh Quotient Iteration (RQI)



For symmetric matrices, updating the shift dynamically to the current Rayleigh estimate \(\sigma_k = (\bv^{(k)})^T \bA \bv^{(k)}\) defines Rayleigh Quotient Iteration: \[
\begin{align}
(\bA - \sigma_k \bI) \bw^{(k+1)} = \bv^{(k)}, \qquad \bv^{(k+1)} = \frac{\bw^{(k+1)}}{\|\bw^{(k+1)}\|_2}, \qquad \sigma_{k+1} = (\bv^{(k+1)})^T \bA \bv^{(k+1)}.
\end{align}
\]








NoteTheorem: Local Cubic Convergence of Rayleigh Quotient Iteration



Let \(\bA\) be real symmetric, and let \(\lambda_*\) be a simple eigenvalue with unit eigenvector \(\bq_*\). If a unit RQI iterate \(\bv^{(k)}\) is sufficiently close to \(\bq_*\), then \[
\begin{align}
\sin\angle(\bv^{(k+1)},\bq_*)
    \leq C\sin^3\angle(\bv^{(k)},\bq_*).
\end{align}
\] If \(\lambda_*\) is an extremal eigenvalue, the Rayleigh shifts also satisfy \[
\begin{align}
|\sigma_{k+1}-\lambda_*|
    \leq C'|\sigma_k-\lambda_*|^3
\end{align}
\] for iterates sufficiently close to \(\bq_*\).








TipProof





Write the current unit vector as \[
\begin{align}
\bv=\cos\theta\,\bq_*+\sin\theta\,\bz,
    \qquad
    \bz\perp\bq_*,
    \qquad
    \|\bz\|_2=1.
\end{align}
\] Its Rayleigh quotient \(\sigma=\bv^T\bA\bv\) obeys \[
\begin{align}
\sigma
    &= (\cos\theta\,\bq_*+\sin\theta\,\bz)^T\bA(\cos\theta\,\bq_*+\sin\theta\,\bz) \\
    &= \cos^2\theta\,\lambda_* + 2\cos\theta\sin\theta\,\bq_*^T\bA\bz + \sin^2\theta\,\bz^T\bA\bz.
\end{align}
\] Because \(\bA\bq_* = \lambda_*\bq_*\) and \(\bq_*^T\bz = 0\), the cross-term vanishes: \(\bq_*^T\bA\bz = \lambda_*\bq_*^T\bz = 0\). Substituting \(\cos^2\theta = 1-\sin^2\theta\), \[
\begin{align}
\sigma-\lambda_*
    = (1-\sin^2\theta)\lambda_* + \sin^2\theta\,\bz^T\bA\bz - \lambda_*
    = \sin^2\theta\,(\bz^T\bA\bz-\lambda_*),
\end{align}
\] so \(|\sigma-\lambda_*|\leq C_0\sin^2\theta\).
Let \(\gamma=\min_{\lambda_j\neq\lambda_*}|\lambda_j-\lambda_*|&gt;0\). Once \(\theta\) is small enough, \(|\lambda_j-\sigma|\geq\gamma/2\) for every other eigenvalue. Expanding the inverse-iteration vector \(\bw=(\bA-\sigma\bI)^{-1}\bv\) in an orthonormal eigenbasis, its component along \(\bq_*\) has magnitude \[
\begin{align}
\frac{|\cos\theta|}{|\lambda_*-\sigma|},
\end{align}
\] whereas the norm of its orthogonal component is at most \(2|\sin\theta|/\gamma\). Normalization does not change their ratio. Therefore, \[
\begin{align}
\tan\angle(\bv^+,\bq_*)
    \leq
    \frac{2|\lambda_*-\sigma|}{\gamma|\cos\theta|}|\sin\theta|
    \leq C_1|\sin\theta|^3.
\end{align}
\] For small angles, \(\sin\theta\) and \(\tan\theta\) are comparable, which proves cubic convergence of the eigenvector direction.
If \(\lambda_*\) is extremal, the spectral gap also gives two-sided local bounds \(c\sin^2\theta\leq|\sigma-\lambda_*|\leq C\sin^2\theta\). Applying the Rayleigh-quotient identity to the next iterate and using the cubic angle bound yields \[
\begin{align}
|\sigma^+-\lambda_*|
    \leq C_2\sin^2\angle(\bv^+,\bq_*)
    \leq C_3\sin^6\theta
    \leq C_4|\sigma-\lambda_*|^3.
\end{align}
\]

14.3 The Jacobi Eigenvalue Algorithm
For a symmetric matrix, an alternative to the QR algorithm drives the off-diagonal entries to zero directly. Each step applies an orthogonal similarity by a plane rotation chosen to annihilate one off-diagonal entry. The method, due to Jacobi in 1846, is slower than shifted QR for a general dense matrix. It remains useful because it can compute the small eigenvalues of graded positive definite matrices to high relative accuracy (Demmel 1997), and because rotations acting on disjoint pairs of indices can be applied in parallel.






NoteDefinition: Givens Plane Rotation Matrix



The matrix \(\bG(p, q, \theta) \in \fR^{n \times n}\) is an identity matrix except for the four entries at the intersections of rows and columns \(p\) and \(q\): \[
\begin{align}
g_{pp} = c, \qquad g_{pq} = s, \qquad g_{qp} = -s, \qquad g_{qq} = c,
\end{align}
\] where \(c = \cos\theta\) and \(s = \sin\theta\). The transformation \(\bA' = \bG^T \bA \bG\) modifies only rows and columns \(p\) and \(q\) of \(\bA\).








NoteTheorem: Derivation of the Optimal Rotation Angle



To zero the transformed off-diagonal entry \(a'_{pq} = 0\), we consider the \(2 \times 2\) orthogonal similarity transformation on the submatrix at indices \(\{p, q\}\): \[
\begin{align}
\begin{pmatrix} a'_{pp} & a'_{pq} \\ a'_{pq} & a'_{qq} \end{pmatrix}
    &=
    \begin{pmatrix} c & -s \\ s & c \end{pmatrix}
    \begin{pmatrix} a_{pp} & a_{pq} \\ a_{pq} & a_{qq} \end{pmatrix}
    \begin{pmatrix} c & s \\ -s & c \end{pmatrix} \\
    &=
    \begin{pmatrix}
        c^2 a_{pp} - 2cs a_{pq} + s^2 a_{qq} & (c^2 - s^2) a_{pq} + cs (a_{pp} - a_{qq}) \\
        (c^2 - s^2) a_{pq} + cs (a_{pp} - a_{qq}) & s^2 a_{pp} + 2cs a_{pq} + c^2 a_{qq}
    \end{pmatrix}.
\end{align}
\] Setting \(a'_{pq} = 0\) yields the trigonometric condition \[
\begin{align}
(c^2 - s^2) a_{pq} + cs (a_{pp} - a_{qq}) = 0
    \implies
    \frac{\cos(2\theta)}{\sin(2\theta)} = \frac{a_{qq} - a_{pp}}{2a_{pq}}.
\end{align}
\] Defining \(\tau = \cot(2\theta) = \frac{a_{qq} - a_{pp}}{2a_{pq}}\) and using \(\cot(2\theta) = \frac{1 - \tan^2\theta}{2\tan\theta}\), setting \(t = \tan\theta\) produces \[
\begin{align}
t^2 + 2\tau t - 1 = 0.
\end{align}
\] To minimize the perturbation to \(\bA\) and avoid cancellation, we select the root of smaller absolute value: \[
\begin{align}
t = \frac{\operatorname{sign}(\tau)}{|\tau| + \sqrt{1 + \tau^2}}, \qquad c = \frac{1}{\sqrt{1 + t^2}}, \qquad s = ct.
\end{align}
\]








TipProof





The quadratic formula yields roots \(t = -\tau \pm \sqrt{1 + \tau^2}\). Multiplying the root of smaller magnitude by its algebraic conjugate yields \[
\begin{align}
t = \frac{(-\tau \pm \sqrt{1 + \tau^2})(\tau \pm \sqrt{1 + \tau^2})}{\tau \pm \sqrt{1 + \tau^2}} = \frac{1}{\tau + \operatorname{sign}(\tau)\sqrt{1 + \tau^2}} = \frac{\operatorname{sign}(\tau)}{|\tau| + \sqrt{1 + \tau^2}}.
\end{align}
\] This formula satisfies \(|t| \leq 1\), corresponding to a rotation angle \(|\theta| \leq \pi/4\), and avoids subtracting nearly equal floating-point numbers.



A rotation that annihilates \(a_{pq}\) may make a previously annihilated entry nonzero again, so the method does not terminate after \(n(n-1)/2\) steps. Convergence follows instead from a quantity that decreases at every step.






NoteTheorem: Monotone Decrease of the Off-Diagonal Frobenius Norm



Let the off-diagonal squared norm be defined by \[
\begin{align}
\operatorname{off}(\bA)^2 = \sum_{i \neq j} a_{ij}^2 = \|\bA\|_F^2 - \sum_{i=1}^n a_{ii}^2.
\end{align}
\] Applying the Jacobi rotation \(\bA' = \bG(p, q, \theta)^T \bA \bG(p, q, \theta)\) with the optimal angle strictly reduces the off-diagonal norm: \[
\begin{align}
\operatorname{off}(\bA')^2 = \operatorname{off}(\bA)^2 - 2a_{pq}^2.
\end{align}
\]








TipProof





Because \(\bG\) is orthogonal, the total Frobenius norm is preserved: \(\|\bA'\|_F^2 = \|\bA\|_F^2\). The rotation affects only entries in rows and columns \(p\) and \(q\). For the \(2 \times 2\) submatrix, the sum of squares is invariant under orthogonal similarity: \[
\begin{align}
(a'_{pp})^2 + (a'_{qq})^2 + 2(a'_{pq})^2 = a_{pp}^2 + a_{qq}^2 + 2a_{pq}^2.
\end{align}
\] Because the rotation enforces \(a'_{pq} = 0\), the sum of squared diagonal entries increases: \[
\begin{align}
(a'_{pp})^2 + (a'_{qq})^2 = a_{pp}^2 + a_{qq}^2 + 2a_{pq}^2.
\end{align}
\] All other diagonal entries \(a_{ii}\) (\(i \neq p, q\)) are untouched. Substituting this into \(\operatorname{off}(\bA')^2 = \|\bA'\|_F^2 - \sum (a'_{ii})^2\) proves that \(\operatorname{off}(\bA')^2 = \operatorname{off}(\bA)^2 - 2a_{pq}^2\).









NoteDefinition: Cyclic Jacobi Algorithm



Let \(\bA^{(0)}=\bA\) and \(\bV=\bI_n\). While \(\operatorname{off}(\bA)&gt;`tol`\,\|\bA^{(0)}\|_F\), sweep through the pairs \(1\leq p&lt;q\leq n\) in a fixed order. For each pair, compute \(c\) and \(s\) from the referenced result, replace \(\bA\) by \(\bG(p,q,\theta)^T\bA\bG(p,q,\theta)\), and replace \(\bV\) by \(\bV\bG(p,q,\theta)\). On termination, the diagonal of \(\bA\) approximates the eigenvalues and the columns of \(\bV\) the eigenvectors.


the referenced result shows that \(\operatorname{off}(\bA)^2\) decreases by \(2a_{pq}^2\) at every rotation. Choosing the largest off-diagonal entry at each step gives linear convergence, as the exercises show. The cyclic order avoids that search, and once the off-diagonal entries are small compared with the gaps between the eigenvalues, cyclic Jacobi converges quadratically, measured sweep by sweep (Golub and Van Loan 2013). Each sweep costs \(O(n^3)\) flops.

15.3 Low-Rank Matrix Approximation






NoteTheorem: Eckart–Young–Mirsky Low-Rank Approximation Theorem



Let \(\bA \in \fR^{m \times n}\) have singular value decomposition \(\bA = \sum_{i=1}^r \sigma_i \bu_i \bv_i^T\). For any \(k &lt; r\), let the rank-\(k\) truncated SVD be \[
\begin{align}
\bA_k = \sum_{i=1}^k \sigma_i \bu_i \bv_i^T.
\end{align}
\] Then \(\bA_k\) is the best rank-\(k\) approximation to \(\bA\) in the spectral norm: \[
\begin{align}
\min_{\operatorname{rank}(\bB) \leq k} \|\bA - \bB\|_2 = \|\bA - \bA_k\|_2 = \sigma_{k+1},
\end{align}
\] and in the Frobenius norm: \[
\begin{align}
\min_{\operatorname{rank}(\bB) \leq k} \|\bA - \bB\|_F = \|\bA - \bA_k\|_F = \sqrt{\sum_{j=k+1}^r \sigma_j^2}.
\end{align}
\]








TipProof





First, evaluating the spectral norm of \(\bA - \bA_k\): \[
\begin{align}
\bA - \bA_k = \sum_{i=k+1}^r \sigma_i \bu_i \bv_i^T = \bU \operatorname{diag}(0, ..., 0, \sigma_{k+1}, ..., \sigma_r, 0, ..., 0) \bV^T.
\end{align}
\] Because the 2-norm is unitarily invariant, \(\|\bA - \bA_k\|_2 = \sigma_{k+1}\).
Now let \(\bB \in \fR^{m \times n}\) be any arbitrary matrix with \(\operatorname{rank}(\bB) \leq k\). By the Rank-Nullity Theorem, \(\dim \mathcal{N}(\bB) = n - \operatorname{rank}(\bB) \geq n - k\). Define the \((k+1)\)-dimensional subspace \(W_{k+1} = \operatorname{span}\{\bv_1, ..., \bv_{k+1}\} \subset \fR^n\). By the dimension formula, \[
\begin{align}
\dim(\mathcal{N}(\bB) \cap W_{k+1}) = \dim \mathcal{N}(\bB) + \dim W_{k+1} - \dim(\mathcal{N}(\bB) + W_{k+1}) \geq (n - k) + (k + 1) - n = 1.
\end{align}
\] Therefore, there exists a nonzero vector \(\bw \in \mathcal{N}(\bB) \cap W_{k+1}\), normalized so that \(\|\bw\|_2 = 1\).
Because \(\bw \in \mathcal{N}(\bB)\), \(\bB\bw = \bzero\). Expanding \(\bw = \sum_{j=1}^{k+1} c_j \bv_j\) with \(\sum_{j=1}^{k+1} c_j^2 = 1\), \[
\begin{align}
\|(\bA - \bB)\bw\|_2^2
    &= \|\bA\bw - \bB\bw\|_2^2
    = \|\bA\bw\|_2^2 \\
    &= \left\| \bA \sum_{j=1}^{k+1} c_j \bv_j \right\|_2^2
    = \left\| \sum_{j=1}^{k+1} c_j \sigma_j \bu_j \right\|_2^2 \\
    &= \sum_{j=1}^{k+1} c_j^2 \sigma_j^2
    \geq \sigma_{k+1}^2 \sum_{j=1}^{k+1} c_j^2
    = \sigma_{k+1}^2.
\end{align}
\] Consequently, the operator norm satisfies \[
\begin{align}
\|\bA - \bB\|_2 = \max_{\|\bx\|_2 = 1} \|(\bA - \bB)\bx\|_2 \geq \|(\bA - \bB)\bw\|_2 \geq \sigma_{k+1}.
\end{align}
\] This proves that \(\|\bA - \bB\|_2 \geq \sigma_{k+1}\) for every matrix \(\bB\) of rank at most \(k\).
For the Frobenius norm, let \(\bP\) be the orthogonal projector onto \(\mathcal{N}(\bB)\). Since \(\operatorname{rank}(\bB)\leq k\), this null space has dimension at least \(n-k\). Hence \[
\begin{align}
\|\bA-\bB\|_F^2
    \geq \|(\bA-\bB)\bP\|_F^2
    =\|\bA\bP\|_F^2.
\end{align}
\] Choose an \((n-k)\)-dimensional subspace of \(\mathcal{N}(\bB)\) if the null space is larger, and let \(\bP\) project onto that subspace. Expanding in the right singular-vector basis gives \[
\begin{align}
\|\bA\bP\|_F^2
    &= \operatorname{tr}(\bP^T\bA^T\bA\bP)
    = \operatorname{tr}\left( \bP \left(\sum_{i=1}^n \sigma_i^2 \bv_i\bv_i^T\right) \bP \right) \\
    &= \sum_{i=1}^n \sigma_i^2 \operatorname{tr}(\bP\bv_i\bv_i^T\bP)
    = \sum_{i=1}^n \sigma_i^2 \|\bP\bv_i\|_2^2.
\end{align}
\] The weights \(d_i=\|\bP\bv_i\|_2^2\) satisfy \(0\leq d_i\leq1\) and \(\sum_i d_i=\operatorname{tr}(\bP)=n-k\). The weighted sum is minimized by placing unit weight on the \(n-k\) smallest singular values. Therefore \[
\begin{align}
\|\bA-\bB\|_F^2\geq\sum_{i=k+1}^r\sigma_i^2.
\end{align}
\] The truncated SVD attains this bound, completing the proof.









TipRemark



(Numerical rank and noise filtering) In measured or discretized data, singular values rarely vanish exactly. Given a tolerance \(\tau&gt;0\), define the numerical rank by \[
\begin{align}
r_\tau = \#\{i:\sigma_i&gt;\tau\}.
\end{align}
\] A common floating-point default is \(\tau=\varepsilon_{\text{mach}}\max(m,n)\sigma_1\), but a scientifically meaningful threshold should reflect the noise and discretization scales. Truncation removes components below the chosen threshold. Calling those components noise requires a model of the data.

18.1 Existence, Uniqueness, and the Vandermonde Matrix
Interpolation is the process of constructing a continuous function that passes exactly through a discrete collection of given data points. Given \(n+1\) distinct abscissas (or nodes) \(x_0, x_1, ..., x_n \in \fR\) and corresponding values \(y_0, y_1, ..., y_n \in \fR\), polynomial interpolation seeks a polynomial \(P_n\) of degree at most \(n\) such that \[
\begin{align}
P_n(x_i) = y_i, \qquad i = 0, 1, ..., n.
\end{align}
\] Polynomials are useful local and global approximants because finite polynomial representations can be evaluated, differentiated, and integrated by finite algorithms.

NoteTheorem: Existence and Uniqueness of the Interpolating Polynomial



Let \(x_0, x_1, ..., x_n\) be \(n+1\) pairwise distinct real numbers, and let \(y_0, y_1, ..., y_n\) be arbitrary real numbers. There exists a unique polynomial \(P_n \in \mathcal{P}_n\) of degree at most \(n\) such that \(P_n(x_i) = y_i\) for all \(i = 0, 1, ..., n\).








TipProof





We establish existence and uniqueness using the fundamental properties of polynomials.

Uniqueness: Suppose there exist two polynomials \(P_n, Q_n \in \mathcal{P}_n\) that satisfy the interpolation conditions, so \(P_n(x_i) = Q_n(x_i) = y_i\) for \(i = 0, ..., n\). Define the difference polynomial \(R(x) = P_n(x) - Q_n(x)\). Because \(\mathcal{P}_n\) is a vector space, \(R\) has degree at most \(n\). Furthermore, \[
\begin{align}
R(x_i) = P_n(x_i) - Q_n(x_i) = y_i - y_i = 0, \qquad i = 0, ..., n.
\end{align}
\] Thus \(R(x)\) has at least \(n+1\) distinct real roots. By the Fundamental Theorem of Algebra, a non-zero polynomial of degree at most \(n\) can have at most \(n\) roots. Therefore, \(R(x)\) must be the zero polynomial, which implies \(P_n(x) \equiv Q_n(x)\).
Existence via Linear Algebra: Express \(P_n(x)\) in the standard monomial basis: \[
\begin{align}
P_n(x) = c_0 + c_1 x + c_2 x^2 + ... + c_n x^n = \sum_{j=0}^n c_j x^j.
\end{align}
\] The interpolation conditions \(P_n(x_i) = y_i\) form a square \((n+1) \times (n+1)\) linear system \(\bV \bc = \by\): \[
\begin{align}
\begin{pmatrix}
             1 & x_0 & x_0^2 & \cdots & x_0^n \\
             1 & x_1 & x_1^2 & \cdots & x_1^n \\
             \vdots & \vdots & \vdots & \ddots & \vdots \\
             1 & x_n & x_n^2 & \cdots & x_n^n
         \end{pmatrix}
         \begin{pmatrix}
             c_0 \\ c_1 \\ \vdots \\ c_n
         \end{pmatrix}
         =
         \begin{pmatrix}
             y_0 \\ y_1 \\ \vdots \\ y_n
         \end{pmatrix}.
\end{align}
\] The matrix \(\bV\) is the Vandermonde matrix. Its determinant evaluates to \[
\begin{align}
\det(\bV) = \prod_{0 \leq i &lt; j \leq n} (x_j - x_i).
\end{align}
\] Because the nodes \(x_i\) are pairwise distinct, \(x_j - x_i \neq 0\) for all \(j &gt; i\), so \(\det(\bV) \neq 0\). Consequently, \(\bV\) is invertible, and a unique coefficient vector \(\bc = \bV^{-1} \by\) exists for any data vector \(\by\).










TipRemark



Although the Vandermonde matrix provides a constructive proof of existence, solving \(\bV\bc=\by\) in the monomial basis is often a poor numerical representation. Its condition number depends on the nodes and their scaling and can grow rapidly with degree. A large coefficient error does not imply that the interpolation problem itself is ill-posed. It may instead reflect an ill-conditioned choice of basis. The Lagrange and barycentric forms avoid solving for monomial coefficients.

18.2 The Lagrange Form
The Lagrange formulation circumvents the ill-conditioned Vandermonde linear system by constructing a basis of polynomials tailored to the specific nodes \(x_i\).






NoteDefinition: Lagrange Basis Polynomials



Given \(n+1\) distinct nodes \(x_0, x_1, ..., x_n\), the \(i\)-th Lagrange basis polynomial \(L_i \in \mathcal{P}_n\) is defined by \[
\begin{align}
L_i(x) = \prod_{\substack{j=0 \\ j \neq i}}^n \frac{x - x_j}{x_i - x_j} = \frac{(x - x_0)\cdots(x - x_{i-1})(x - x_{i+1})\cdots(x - x_n)}{(x_i - x_0)\cdots(x_i - x_{i-1})(x_i - x_{i+1})\cdots(x_i - x_n)}.
\end{align}
\] The Lagrange basis polynomials satisfy the cardinal Kronecker delta property: \[
\begin{align}
L_i(x_j) = \delta_{ij} =
    \begin{cases}
        1, & \text{if } i = j, \\
        0, & \text{if } i \neq j.
    \end{cases}
\end{align}
\] The interpolating polynomial in Lagrange form is \[
\begin{align}
P_n(x) = \sum_{i=0}^n y_i L_i(x).
\end{align}
\]








NoteExample



To interpolate the three data points \((-1, 1)\), \((0, 0)\), and \((1, 1)\), we construct the degree-2 Lagrange basis polynomials on \(x_0 = -1, x_1 = 0, x_2 = 1\): \[
\begin{align}
L_0(x) &= \frac{(x - 0)(x - 1)}{(-1 - 0)(-1 - 1)} = \frac{x(x - 1)}{2} = \frac{x^2 - x}{2}, \\
    L_1(x) &= \frac{(x - (-1))(x - 1)}{(0 - (-1))(0 - 1)} = \frac{(x + 1)(x - 1)}{-1} = 1 - x^2, \\
    L_2(x) &= \frac{(x - (-1))(x - 0)}{(1 - (-1))(1 - 0)} = \frac{x(x + 1)}{2} = \frac{x^2 + x}{2}.
\end{align}
\] Multiplying by the data values \(y_0 = 1, y_1 = 0, y_2 = 1\) gives \[
\begin{align}
P_2(x) = 1 \cdot \left(\frac{x^2 - x}{2}\right) + 0 \cdot (1 - x^2) + 1 \cdot \left(\frac{x^2 + x}{2}\right) = \frac{x^2 - x + x^2 + x}{2} = x^2.
\end{align}
\]








TipRemark



The standard Lagrange form has two practical shortcomings. First, evaluating \(P_n(x)\) at a single point \(x\) requires \(O(n^2)\) floating-point operations. Second, adding a new node \((x_{n+1}, y_{n+1})\) alters every single basis polynomial \(L_i(x)\), requiring a complete recomputation from scratch.

18.3 The Barycentric Interpolation Formula
Barycentric interpolation reorganizes the Lagrange formula into a stable, computationally optimal form requiring only \(O(n)\) operations per evaluation.






NoteTheorem: Barycentric Interpolation Formulas



Let \(\ell(x) = \prod_{j=0}^n (x - x_j)\) denote the nodal polynomial. For each node \(x_i\), define the barycentric weights \[
\begin{align}
w_i = \frac{1}{\prod_{j \neq i} (x_i - x_j)} = \frac{1}{\ell'(x_i)}, \qquad i = 0, 1, ..., n.
\end{align}
\] Then the interpolating polynomial satisfies:

First Barycentric Form: \[
\begin{align}
P_n(x) = \ell(x) \sum_{i=0}^n \frac{w_i}{x - x_i} y_i.
\end{align}
\]
Second (True) Barycentric Form: \[
\begin{align}
P_n(x) = \frac{\sum_{i=0}^n \frac{w_i}{x - x_i} y_i}{\sum_{i=0}^n \frac{w_i}{x - x_i}}.
\end{align}
\]

If \(x = x_j\) for some node index \(j\), the formula evaluates to \(P_n(x_j) = y_j\).








TipProof





We express the Lagrange basis polynomials in terms of \(\ell(x)\) and \(w_i\). By definition, \[
\begin{align}
L_i(x) = \prod_{j \neq i} \frac{x - x_j}{x_i - x_j} = \left( \prod_{j \neq i} (x - x_j) \right) \left( \frac{1}{\prod_{j \neq i} (x_i - x_j)} \right) = \frac{\ell(x)}{x - x_i} w_i.
\end{align}
\] Substituting this relation into \(P_n(x) = \sum_{i=0}^n y_i L_i(x)\) yields the First Barycentric Formula: \[
\begin{align}
P_n(x) = \sum_{i=0}^n y_i \left( \frac{\ell(x)}{x - x_i} w_i \right) = \ell(x) \sum_{i=0}^n \frac{w_i}{x - x_i} y_i.
\end{align}
\] To derive the Second Barycentric Formula, consider interpolating the constant function \(f(x) \equiv 1\), where \(y_i = 1\) for all \(i\). Because the unique degree-\(n\) interpolant of a constant is the constant itself, the First Barycentric Formula gives \[
\begin{align}
1 = \ell(x) \sum_{i=0}^n \frac{w_i}{x - x_i}.
\end{align}
\] Dividing the expression for \(P_n(x)\) by this identity cancels the nodal polynomial \(\ell(x)\), yielding \[
\begin{align}
P_n(x) = \frac{P_n(x)}{1} = \frac{\ell(x) \sum_{i=0}^n \frac{w_i}{x - x_i} y_i}{\ell(x) \sum_{i=0}^n \frac{w_i}{x - x_i}} = \frac{\sum_{i=0}^n \frac{w_i}{x - x_i} y_i}{\sum_{i=0}^n \frac{w_i}{x - x_i}}.
\end{align}
\]









TipRemark



The second barycentric formula is numerically stable. Higham (2004) proved that barycentric interpolation is backward stable, meaning the computed value \(\hat{P}_n(x)\) is the exact value of a polynomial interpolating slightly perturbed data \(y_i(1 + \varepsilon_i)\). Furthermore, common multiplicative factors in the weights \(w_i\) cancel out in the numerator and denominator, allowing simple analytical expressions for standard node families (such as Chebyshev nodes).

18.4 The Interpolation Error Formula






NoteTheorem: Cauchy Remainder Error Theorem



Let \(f \in C^{n+1}[a, b]\), and let \(P_n \in \mathcal{P}_n\) interpolate \(f\) at \(n+1\) distinct nodes \(x_0, x_1, ..., x_n \in [a, b]\). For each point \(x \in [a, b]\), there exists a point \(\xi_x \in (a, b)\) such that \[
\begin{align}
f(x) - P_n(x) = \frac{f^{(n+1)}(\xi_x)}{(n+1)!} \prod_{j=0}^n (x - x_j) = \frac{f^{(n+1)}(\xi_x)}{(n+1)!} \omega_{n+1}(x),
\end{align}
\] where \(\omega_{n+1}(x) = \prod_{j=0}^n (x - x_j)\) is the nodal error polynomial.








TipProof





If \(x = x_i\) for some node \(x_i\), both sides vanish and the formula holds trivially for any \(\xi_x\). Now fix \(x \in [a, b]\) with \(x \neq x_i\) for all \(i\). Define the constant \[
\begin{align}
\lambda = \frac{f(x) - P_n(x)}{\omega_{n+1}(x)},
\end{align}
\] and consider the auxiliary function \(g : [a, b] \to \fR\) defined by \[
\begin{align}
g(t) = f(t) - P_n(t) - \lambda \omega_{n+1}(t).
\end{align}
\] Because \(f \in C^{n+1}[a, b]\) and polynomials are infinitely differentiable, \(g \in C^{n+1}[a, b]\). We evaluate \(g\) at the \(n+2\) distinct points \(x_0, x_1, ..., x_n\) and \(x\): \[
\begin{align}
g(x_i) &= f(x_i) - P_n(x_i) - \lambda \omega_{n+1}(x_i) = y_i - y_i - 0 = 0, \qquad i = 0, ..., n, \\
    g(x) &= f(x) - P_n(x) - \left( \frac{f(x) - P_n(x)}{\omega_{n+1}(x)} \right) \omega_{n+1}(x) = 0.
\end{align}
\] Thus \(g(t)\) vanishes at \(n+2\) distinct points in \([a, b]\).
By Rolle’s Theorem, between any two adjacent zeros of \(g(t)\), its derivative \(g'(t)\) has at least one zero. Therefore, \(g'(t)\) has at least \(n+1\) distinct zeros in \((a, b)\). Applying Rolle’s Theorem successively:

\(g''(t)\) has at least \(n\) distinct zeros in \((a, b)\),
\(g'''(t)\) has at least \(n-1\) distinct zeros in \((a, b)\),
Continuing inductively, \(g^{(n+1)}(t)\) has at least one zero in \((a, b)\), which we denote by \(\xi_x\).

We now differentiate \(g(t)\) exactly \(n+1\) times with respect to \(t\): \[
\begin{align}
g^{(n+1)}(t) = f^{(n+1)}(t) - P_n^{(n+1)}(t) - \lambda \frac{d^{n+1}}{dt^{n+1}} [\omega_{n+1}(t)].
\end{align}
\] Since \(P_n\) has degree at most \(n\), its \((n+1)\)-th derivative is identically zero: \(P_n^{(n+1)}(t) \equiv 0\). The nodal polynomial \(\omega_{n+1}(t) = t^{n+1} + ...\) is a monic polynomial of degree \(n+1\), so its \((n+1)\)-th derivative is \((n+1)!\). Evaluating at \(t = \xi_x\) gives \[
\begin{align}
0 = g^{(n+1)}(\xi_x) = f^{(n+1)}(\xi_x) - 0 - \lambda (n+1)!.
\end{align}
\] Solving for \(\lambda\) produces \[
\begin{align}
\lambda = \frac{f^{(n+1)}(\xi_x)}{(n+1)!}.
\end{align}
\] Equating this to our definition of \(\lambda = (f(x) - P_n(x)) / \omega_{n+1}(x)\) completes the proof.

18.6 Piecewise Polynomials: Cubic Splines
When interpolating large sets of experimental data without prior knowledge of analytical smoothness, high-degree global polynomials are abandoned in favor of piecewise low-degree polynomials. A spline is a piecewise polynomial that maintains global derivative continuity across all internal nodes.






NoteDefinition: Cubic Spline Interpolant



Given \(n+1\) distinct points \(a = x_0 &lt; x_1 &lt; ... &lt; x_n = b\) and values \(y_0, y_1, ..., y_n\), a cubic spline \(S(x)\) is a function such that:

On each subinterval \([x_i, x_{i+1}]\), \(S(x) = S_i(x)\) is a cubic polynomial: \[
\begin{align}
S_i(x) = a_i + b_i(x - x_i) + c_i(x - x_i)^2 + d_i(x - x_i)^3, \qquad i = 0, ..., n-1.
\end{align}
\]
Interpolation conditions: \(S(x_i) = y_i\) for all \(i = 0, ..., n\).
\(C^2\) continuity: \(S(x)\), \(S'(x)\), and \(S''(x)\) are continuous on the entire interval \([a, b]\).









NoteTheorem: The Tridiagonal Spline System



Let \(h_i = x_{i+1} - x_i\) and let \(M_i = S''(x_i)\) denote the second derivative of the spline at node \(x_i\). The condition that \(S'(x)\) is continuous at each interior node \(x_i\) (\(i = 1, ..., n-1\)) leads to the linear system \[
\begin{align}
h_{i-1} M_{i-1} + 2(h_{i-1} + h_i) M_i + h_i M_{i+1} = 6 \left( \frac{y_{i+1} - y_i}{h_i} - \frac{y_i - y_{i-1}}{h_{i-1}} \right).
\end{align}
\] To close the system of \(n-1\) equations in \(n+1\) unknowns, two boundary conditions are imposed:

Natural (Free) Spline: \(M_0 = M_n = 0\).
Clamped (Complete) Spline: \(S'(x_0) = f'(a)\) and \(S'(x_n) = f'(b)\).
Periodic Spline: \(S'(x_0) = S'(x_n)\) and \(M_0 = M_n\).

For natural and clamped boundary conditions, the resulting linear system is strictly diagonally dominant and symmetric positive definite. It can be solved in \(O(n)\) time using the Thomas algorithm.








TipProof





Since \(S_i(x)\) is a cubic polynomial on \([x_i, x_{i+1}]\), its second derivative \(S_i''(x)\) is a linear function interpolating \(M_i = S''(x_i)\) and \(M_{i+1} = S''(x_{i+1})\): \[
\begin{align}
S_i''(x) = M_i \frac{x_{i+1} - x}{h_i} + M_{i+1} \frac{x - x_i}{h_i}.
\end{align}
\] Integrating twice with respect to \(x\) gives \[
\begin{align}
S_i(x) = \frac{M_i (x_{i+1} - x)^3}{6 h_i} + \frac{M_{i+1} (x - x_i)^3}{6 h_i} + C_i (x_{i+1} - x) + D_i (x - x_i).
\end{align}
\] Applying the interpolation conditions \(S_i(x_i) = y_i\) and \(S_i(x_{i+1}) = y_{i+1}\) determines the constants \(C_i\) and \(D_i\): \[
\begin{align}
C_i = \frac{y_i}{h_i} - \frac{M_i h_i}{6}, \qquad D_i = \frac{y_{i+1}}{h_i} - \frac{M_{i+1} h_i}{6}.
\end{align}
\] Differentiating \(S_i(x)\) to obtain \(S_i'(x)\) and evaluating at the endpoints of \([x_i, x_{i+1}]\) yields \[
\begin{align}
S_i'(x_i) &= -\frac{M_i h_i}{3} - \frac{M_{i+1} h_i}{6} + \frac{y_{i+1} - y_i}{h_i}, \\
    S_{i-1}'(x_i) &= \frac{M_{i-1} h_{i-1}}{6} + \frac{M_i h_{i-1}}{3} + \frac{y_i - y_{i-1}}{h_{i-1}}.
\end{align}
\] Equating \(S_{i-1}'(x_i) = S_i'(x_i)\) for continuity of the first derivative and gathering terms produces the stated tridiagonal equation. Strict diagonal dominance follows from \(2(h_{i-1} + h_i) &gt; h_{i-1} + h_i\), which guarantees non-singularity and unconditional stability of Gaussian elimination without pivoting.









NoteTheorem: Minimum Curvature (Energy Minimization) Property



Let \(S(x)\) be the natural cubic spline interpolating \(f(x)\) at \(x_0, x_1, ..., x_n\). Let \(g \in C^2[a, b]\) be any other function satisfying \(g(x_i) = y_i\) for all \(i = 0, ..., n\). Then \[
\begin{align}
\int_a^b [S''(x)]^2\,dx \leq \int_a^b [g''(x)]^2\,dx.
\end{align}
\] Natural cubic splines are the smoothest possible interpolants in the sense of minimizing total bending energy.








TipProof





Let \(h(x) = g(x) - S(x)\). Since both \(g\) and \(S\) interpolate \(f\) at the knots \(x_0, x_1, ..., x_n\), we have \[
\begin{align}
h(x_i) = g(x_i) - S(x_i) = y_i - y_i = 0, \qquad i = 0, 1, ..., n.
\end{align}
\] Expanding the squared second derivative of \(g = S + h\): \[
\begin{align}
\int_a^b [g''(x)]^2\,dx
    &= \int_a^b [S''(x) + h''(x)]^2\,dx \\
    &= \int_a^b [S''(x)]^2\,dx + 2\int_a^b S''(x)h''(x)\,dx + \int_a^b [h''(x)]^2\,dx.
\end{align}
\] We evaluate the cross term by integrating by parts on each subinterval \([x_i, x_{i+1}]\): \[
\begin{align}
\int_{x_i}^{x_{i+1}} S''(x)h''(x)\,dx
    &= \left[ S''(x) h'(x) \right]_{x_i}^{x_{i+1}} - \int_{x_i}^{x_{i+1}} S'''(x) h'(x)\,dx.
\end{align}
\] Because \(S(x)\) is a cubic polynomial on \([x_i, x_{i+1}]\), its third derivative \(S_i'''(x)\) is constant on \((x_i, x_{i+1})\). Let \(S_i'''(x) = c_i\). Then \[
\begin{align}
\int_{x_i}^{x_{i+1}} S'''(x) h'(x)\,dx
    &= c_i \int_{x_i}^{x_{i+1}} h'(x)\,dx
    = c_i [h(x_{i+1}) - h(x_i)] = 0,
\end{align}
\] since \(h(x_{i+1}) = h(x_i) = 0\). Summing the boundary terms over all subintervals: \[
\begin{align}
\int_a^b S''(x)h''(x)\,dx
    &= \sum_{i=0}^{n-1} \left( S''(x_{i+1}^-) h'(x_{i+1}) - S''(x_i^+) h'(x_i) \right) \\
    &= S''(b) h'(b) - S''(a) h'(a) + \sum_{i=1}^{n-1} \left( S''(x_i^-) - S''(x_i^+) \right) h'(x_i).
\end{align}
\] Because \(S \in C^2[a, b]\), the second derivative is continuous across interior knots: \(S''(x_i^-) - S''(x_i^+) = 0\). For a natural cubic spline, \(S''(a) = S''(b) = 0\), so the boundary terms vanish as well. Therefore \[
\begin{align}
\int_a^b S''(x)h''(x)\,dx = 0.
\end{align}
\] Substituting this back yields \[
\begin{align}
\int_a^b [g''(x)]^2\,dx
    &= \int_a^b [S''(x)]^2\,dx + \int_a^b [h''(x)]^2\,dx
    \geq \int_a^b [S''(x)]^2\,dx,
\end{align}
\] with equality if and only if \(h''(x) \equiv 0\), meaning \(h\) is linear and hence identically zero because \(h(x_0) = h(x_n) = 0\).

19.2 Chebyshev Polynomials and Fundamental Identities






NoteDefinition: Chebyshev Polynomials of the First Kind



For \(x \in [-1, 1]\), the Chebyshev polynomial of degree \(k\) is defined by \[
\begin{align}
T_k(x) = \cos(k \arccos x).
\end{align}
\] Under the substitution \(x = \cos\theta\) with \(\theta \in [0, \pi]\), \[
\begin{align}
T_k(\cos\theta) = \cos(k\theta).
\end{align}
\]








NoteLemma: Chebyshev Three-Term Recurrence



The functions \(T_k(x)\) are algebraic polynomials satisfying the three-term recurrence relation \[
\begin{align}
T_0(x) = 1, \qquad T_1(x) = x, \qquad T_{k+1}(x) = 2x T_k(x) - T_{k-1}(x) \quad \text{for } k \geq 1.
\end{align}
\] Furthermore, for every \(k \geq 1\), the leading coefficient of \(T_k(x)\) is \(2^{k-1}\).








TipProof





Using the trigonometric angle sum and difference identities, \[
\begin{align}
\cos((k+1)\theta) + \cos((k-1)\theta) = 2\cos\theta \cos(k\theta).
\end{align}
\] Substituting \(\theta = \arccos x\), so that \(\cos\theta = x\), yields \[
\begin{align}
T_{k+1}(x) + T_{k-1}(x) = 2x T_k(x),
\end{align}
\] which establishes the recurrence.
For the initial conditions, \(T_0(x) = \cos(0) = 1\) and \(T_1(x) = \cos(\arccos x) = x\). Proceeding by induction, if \(T_k(x)\) has degree \(k\) with leading coefficient \(2^{k-1}\), then \(2x T_k(x)\) has degree \(k+1\) with leading coefficient \(2 \cdot 2^{k-1} = 2^k\). Because \(T_{k-1}\) has degree \(k-1 &lt; k+1\), it does not affect the leading coefficient, confirming that \(T_{k+1}(x)\) has leading coefficient \(2^k\).









NoteTheorem: Orthogonality of Chebyshev Polynomials



Chebyshev polynomials are orthogonal on \([-1, 1]\) with respect to the singular weight function \(w(x) = (1 - x^2)^{-1/2}\): \[
\begin{align}
\int_{-1}^1 T_j(x) T_k(x) \frac{dx}{\sqrt{1 - x^2}}
    =
    \begin{cases}
        0, & j \neq k, \\
        \pi, & j = k = 0, \\
        \frac{\pi}{2}, & j = k \geq 1.
    \end{cases}
\end{align}
\]








TipProof





Substitute \(x = \cos\theta\). As \(x\) ranges from \(-1\) to \(1\), \(\theta\) ranges from \(\pi\) to \(0\). The differential transforms as \(dx = -\sin\theta\, d\theta\), and the denominator becomes \(\sqrt{1 - x^2} = \sin\theta\). The integral transforms to \[
\begin{align}
\int_{-1}^1 T_j(x) T_k(x) \frac{dx}{\sqrt{1 - x^2}}
    =
    \int_\pi^0 \cos(j\theta) \cos(k\theta) \frac{-\sin\theta\, d\theta}{\sin\theta}
    =
    \int_0^\pi \cos(j\theta) \cos(k\theta)\, d\theta.
\end{align}
\] By the orthogonality of the trigonometric cosine basis on \([0, \pi]\), this integral evaluates to \(0\) when \(j \neq k\), to \(\pi\) when \(j = k = 0\), and to \(\pi/2\) when \(j = k \geq 1\).

19.4 The Minimax Property and Equioscillation






NoteTheorem: Chebyshev Minimax Theorem



Let \(\widetilde{\mathcal{P}}_n\) denote the set of all monic polynomials of degree \(n\) with real coefficients. Then \(2^{1-n}T_n\) is the unique solution to the minimax problem on \([-1, 1]\): \[
\begin{align}
\min_{q \in \widetilde{\mathcal{P}}_n} \|q\|_\infty = \|2^{1-n}T_n\|_\infty = 2^{1-n}.
\end{align}
\]








TipProof





Let \(m(x) = 2^{1-n}T_n(x) \in \widetilde{\mathcal{P}}_n\). By the referenced result, \(\|m\|_\infty = 2^{1-n}\). At the \(n+1\) extreme points \(y_j = \cos(j\pi/n)\) for \(j = 0, ..., n\), \[
\begin{align}
m(y_j) = 2^{1-n}(-1)^j.
\end{align}
\] Suppose there exists a monic polynomial \(q \in \widetilde{\mathcal{P}}_n\) such that \(\|q\|_\infty &lt; 2^{1-n}\). Consider the difference polynomial \[
\begin{align}
r(x) = m(x) - q(x).
\end{align}
\] Because both \(m\) and \(q\) are monic polynomials of degree \(n\), the leading terms \(x^n\) cancel. Thus \(r \in \mathcal{P}_{n-1}\) has degree at most \(n-1\).
Evaluating \(r(x)\) at each extreme point \(y_j\): \[
\begin{align}
r(y_j) = m(y_j) - q(y_j) = 2^{1-n}(-1)^j - q(y_j).
\end{align}
\] Because \(|q(y_j)| \leq \|q\|_\infty &lt; 2^{1-n}\), the sign of \(r(y_j)\) is dictated entirely by \(m(y_j)\):

When \(j\) is even, \(m(y_j) = 2^{1-n}\), so \(r(y_j) = 2^{1-n} - q(y_j) &gt; 0\).
When \(j\) is odd, \(m(y_j) = -2^{1-n}\), so \(r(y_j) = -2^{1-n} - q(y_j) &lt; 0\).

Thus \(r(x)\) alternates sign across the \(n+1\) ordered points \(1 = y_0 &gt; y_1 &gt; ... &gt; y_n = -1\). By the Intermediate Value Theorem, \(r(x)\) must vanish at least once in each of the \(n\) disjoint open intervals \((y_{j+1}, y_j)\) for \(j = 0, ..., n-1\).
Consequently, \(r(x)\) has at least \(n\) distinct real zeros in \((-1, 1)\). However, a nonzero polynomial of degree at most \(n-1\) cannot have \(n\) roots. Therefore, \(r(x)\) must be identically zero, which contradicts the strict inequality \(\|q\|_\infty &lt; 2^{1-n}\). This proves that \(\|q\|_\infty \geq 2^{1-n}\) for all monic \(q \in \widetilde{\mathcal{P}}_n\).
To establish uniqueness, suppose \(\|q\|_\infty=2^{1-n}\) and retain \(r=m-q\in\mathcal{P}_{n-1}\). The weak inequalities \[
\begin{align}
(-1)^j r(y_j)\geq0,
    \qquad j=0,...,n,
\end{align}
\] follow from \(m(y_j)=2^{1-n}(-1)^j\) and \(|q(y_j)|\leq2^{1-n}\). Because \(r\) has degree at most \(n-1\), its divided difference of order \(n\) at the distinct points \(y_0,...,y_n\) is zero: \[
\begin{align}
0=r[y_0,...,y_n]
    =\sum_{j=0}^n\frac{r(y_j)}{\prod_{k\neq j}(y_j-y_k)}.
\end{align}
\] Because the nodes are ordered \(1 = y_0 &gt; y_1 &gt; ... &gt; y_n = -1\), the product in the denominator has exactly \(j\) negative factors: \[
\begin{align}
\operatorname{sign}\left(\prod_{k\neq j}(y_j-y_k)\right)
    = \operatorname{sign}\left(\prod_{k&lt;j}(y_j-y_k) \prod_{k&gt;j}(y_j-y_k)\right)
    = (-1)^j.
\end{align}
\] Therefore \[
\begin{align}
\frac{r(y_j)}{\prod_{k\neq j}(y_j-y_k)}
    = \frac{(-1)^j r(y_j)}{\left|\prod_{k\neq j}(y_j-y_k)\right|} \geq 0.
\end{align}
\] A sum of non-negative terms can vanish only if each numerator \((-1)^j r(y_j)\) is zero. Thus \(r(y_j) = 0\) for all \(j = 0, ..., n\). Since \(r \in \mathcal{P}_{n-1}\) has \(n+1\) distinct roots, \(r \equiv 0\), proving \(q \equiv m\).









TipRemark



(Lebesgue constant growth) For any grid of \(n\) interpolation nodes \(X_n\), the interpolation projection \(I_n: C([-1, 1]) \to \mathcal{P}_{n-1}\) satisfies the Lebesgue inequality \(\|f - I_n f\|_\infty \leq (1 + \Lambda_n) \min_{p \in \mathcal{P}_{n-1}} \|f - p\|_\infty\), where \(\Lambda_n\) is the Lebesgue constant. For equally spaced nodes, \(\Lambda_n \sim \frac{2^n}{e n \log n}\), which diverges exponentially and induces Runge’s phenomenon. For Chebyshev nodes, \(\Lambda_n \leq \frac{2}{\pi}\log n + 1\), guaranteeing that Chebyshev interpolation converges at a rate within a logarithmic factor of the optimal polynomial approximation.

19.5 Chebyshev Series and Spectral Accuracy






NoteDefinition: Chebyshev Series Expansion



A function with a convergent Chebyshev expansion is written using the primed-sum convention \[
\begin{align}
f(x) = \sideset{}{'}\sum_{k=0}^\infty c_k T_k(x),
\end{align}
\] where the prime on the summation indicates that the initial term \(k = 0\) carries a factor of \(1/2\): \[
\begin{align}
c_k = \frac{2}{\pi} \int_{-1}^1 f(x) T_k(x) \frac{dx}{\sqrt{1 - x^2}} = \frac{2}{\pi} \int_0^\pi f(\cos\theta) \cos(k\theta)\, d\theta.
\end{align}
\]








NoteTheorem: Geometric Convergence for Analytic Functions



Let \(f\) be analytic on and inside a Bernstein ellipse whose boundary is \[
\begin{align}
\mathcal{E}_\rho = \left\{ z \in \fC : z = \frac{w + w^{-1}}{2}, \; |w| = \rho \right\}
\end{align}
\] with \(\rho&gt;1\), semi-axes \(a = \frac{\rho + \rho^{-1}}{2}\) and \(b = \frac{\rho - \rho^{-1}}{2}\). Suppose \(|f(z)|\leq M\) on this boundary. Then the Chebyshev coefficients satisfy \[
\begin{align}
|c_k| \leq \frac{2M}{\rho^k} \qquad \text{for all } k \geq 0.
\end{align}
\] Consequently, the truncated polynomial approximation \(p_n(x) = \sideset{}{'}\sum_{k=0}^n c_k T_k(x)\) converges geometrically: \[
\begin{align}
\|f - p_n\|_\infty \leq \frac{2M}{\rho - 1} \rho^{-n}.
\end{align}
\]








TipProof





Under the conformal map \(z = \frac{w + w^{-1}}{2}\), the circle \(|w| = r\) for \(1 &lt; r &lt; \rho\) maps to the ellipse \(\mathcal{E}_r\) in the complex \(z\)-plane. The transformed function \(F(w) = f\left(\frac{w + w^{-1}}{2}\right)\) is analytic in the annulus \(r^{-1} &lt; |w| &lt; r\) and satisfies \(F(w) = F(w^{-1})\).
Expanding \(F(w)\) in its Laurent series \(F(w) = \sum_{k=-\infty}^\infty a_k w^k\), the symmetry \(F(w) = F(w^{-1})\) implies \(a_k = a_{-k}\). On the unit circle \(w = e^{i\theta}\), \(F(e^{i\theta}) = f(\cos\theta) = a_0 + 2\sum_{k=1}^\infty a_k \cos(k\theta)\), so the Chebyshev coefficients are related to the Laurent coefficients by \(c_k = 2 a_k\) for \(k \geq 1\). By Cauchy’s Integral Formula, for any \(1 &lt; r &lt; \rho\), \[
\begin{align}
a_k = \frac{1}{2\pi i} \oint_{|w|=r} \frac{F(w)}{w^{k+1}}\, dw.
\end{align}
\] Taking absolute values and using \(|F(w)| \leq M\) gives \(|a_k| \leq M r^{-k}\). Taking the infimum over \(r \to \rho^-\) yields \(|c_k| \leq 2M \rho^{-k}\). Summing the tail of the series gives the uniform error bound: \[
\begin{align}
\|f - p_n\|_\infty \leq \sum_{k=n+1}^\infty |c_k| \leq 2M \sum_{k=n+1}^\infty \rho^{-k} = \frac{2M \rho^{-n}}{\rho - 1}.
\end{align}
\]

NoteDefinition: Degree of Exactness (Algebraic Precision)



A quadrature rule \(Q_n\) has degree of exactness \(m\) if \[
\begin{align}
Q_n(p) = \int_a^b p(x)\,dx \qquad \text{for all } p \in \mathcal{P}_m,
\end{align}
\] and there exists at least one polynomial \(q \in \mathcal{P}_{m+1}\) for which \(Q_n(q) \neq \int_a^b q(x)\,dx\).








NoteTheorem: Interpolatory Quadrature Rules



Let \(x_0, x_1, ..., x_n\) be \(n+1\) distinct nodes in \([a, b]\). The interpolatory quadrature rule approximates \(I(f)\) by integrating the polynomial interpolant \(P_n(x) = \sum_{i=0}^n f(x_i) L_i(x)\) in Lagrange form: \[
\begin{align}
Q_n(f) = \int_a^b P_n(x)\,dx = \sum_{i=0}^n w_i f(x_i), \qquad w_i = \int_a^b L_i(x)\,dx.
\end{align}
\] Every interpolatory quadrature rule with \(n+1\) distinct nodes has degree of exactness at least \(n\).








TipProof





Let \(p \in \mathcal{P}_n\). By the uniqueness of polynomial interpolation (the referenced result), the interpolant of \(p\) is identical to \(p\) itself: \(P_n(x) \equiv p(x)\). Integrating this identity yields \[
\begin{align}
\int_a^b p(x)\,dx = \int_a^b P_n(x)\,dx = \int_a^b \left( \sum_{i=0}^n p(x_i) L_i(x) \right) dx = \sum_{i=0}^n p(x_i) \int_a^b L_i(x)\,dx = \sum_{i=0}^n w_i p(x_i) = Q_n(p).
\end{align}
\] Therefore, \(Q_n\) integrates all polynomials of degree at most \(n\) exactly.

20.2 Newton–Cotes Formulas and Composite Rules
Newton–Cotes rules are interpolatory quadrature formulas built on equally spaced nodes. Because high-order single-panel Newton–Cotes rules suffer from negative weights and Runge’s phenomenon, practical computations use composite rules on small subintervals of uniform width \(h = (b - a)/N\).






NoteDefinition: Composite Trapezoidal and Simpson’s Rules



Dividing \([a, b]\) into \(N\) subintervals with uniform width \(h = (b - a)/N\) and grid points \(x_j = a + jh\):

The Composite Trapezoidal Rule is \[
\begin{align}
T_N(f) = h \left[ \frac{f(a) + f(b)}{2} + \sum_{j=1}^{N-1} f(x_j) \right].
\end{align}
\]
For an even number of panels \(N\), the Composite Simpson’s Rule is \[
\begin{align}
S_N(f) = \frac{h}{3} \left[ f(x_0) + 4\sum_{\substack{j=1 \\ j \text{ odd}}}^{N-1} f(x_j) + 2\sum_{\substack{j=2 \\ j \text{ even}}}^{N-2} f(x_j) + f(x_N) \right].
\end{align}
\]









NoteTheorem: Composite Error Formulas



Let \(f \in C^2[a, b]\) for the trapezoidal rule, and let \(f \in C^4[a, b]\) for Simpson’s rule.

The composite trapezoidal error satisfies \[
\begin{align}
I(f) - T_N(f) = -\frac{b - a}{12} h^2 f''(\xi_T), \qquad \xi_T \in (a, b).
\end{align}
\]
The composite Simpson error satisfies \[
\begin{align}
I(f) - S_N(f) = -\frac{b - a}{180} h^4 f^{(4)}(\xi_S), \qquad \xi_S \in (a, b).
\end{align}
\]









TipProof





For the trapezoidal rule on panel \([x_j, x_{j+1}]\), the Cauchy interpolation error formula (the referenced result) gives \[
\begin{align}
f(x) - P_1(x) = \frac{f''(\xi_j(x))}{2}(x - x_j)(x - x_{j+1}).
\end{align}
\] Because \((x - x_j)(x - x_{j+1}) \leq 0\) on this panel, the Mean Value Theorem for Integrals implies there exists \(\mu_j \in (x_j, x_{j+1})\) such that \[
\begin{align}
\int_{x_j}^{x_{j+1}} [f(x) - P_1(x)]\,dx = \frac{f''(\mu_j)}{2} \int_{x_j}^{x_{j+1}} (x - x_j)(x - x_{j+1})\,dx = -\frac{h^3}{12} f''(\mu_j).
\end{align}
\] Summing across all \(N\) panels and applying the Intermediate Value Theorem to the average \(\frac{1}{N}\sum_{j=0}^{N-1} f''(\mu_j) = f''(\xi_T)\) yields the trapezoidal error formula: \[
\begin{align}
I(f) - T_N(f) = -\frac{h^3}{12} \sum_{j=0}^{N-1} f''(\mu_j) = -\frac{N h^3}{12} f''(\xi_T) = -\frac{b - a}{12} h^2 f''(\xi_T).
\end{align}
\] For Simpson’s rule, expand \(f(x_{2j+1} + t)\) about the midpoint \(c_j = x_{2j+1}\) through fourth order for \(t \in [-h, h]\): \[
\begin{align}
f(c_j + t) = f(c_j) + t f'(c_j) + \frac{t^2}{2} f''(c_j) + \frac{t^3}{6} f'''(c_j) + \frac{t^4}{24} f^{(4)}(c_j) + O(t^5).
\end{align}
\] Integrating over \([-h, h]\), odd powers vanish by symmetry: \[
\begin{align}
\int_{c_j - h}^{c_j + h} f(x)\,dx = \int_{-h}^h f(c_j + t)\,dt = 2h f(c_j) + \frac{h^3}{3} f''(c_j) + \frac{h^5}{60} f^{(4)}(c_j) + O(h^7).
\end{align}
\] The Simpson rule on this double-panel evaluates to \[
\begin{align}
S_{\text{panel}} &= \frac{h}{3} \left[ f(c_j - h) + 4 f(c_j) + f(c_j + h) \right] \\
    &= \frac{h}{3} \left[ 6 f(c_j) + h^2 f''(c_j) + \frac{h^4}{12} f^{(4)}(c_j) + O(h^6) \right] \\
    &= 2h f(c_j) + \frac{h^3}{3} f''(c_j) + \frac{h^5}{36} f^{(4)}(c_j) + O(h^7).
\end{align}
\] Subtracting gives the single-panel error: \[
\begin{align}
\int_{c_j - h}^{c_j + h} f(x)\,dx - S_{\text{panel}} = \left( \frac{1}{60} - \frac{1}{36} \right) h^5 f^{(4)}(\mu_j) = -\frac{h^5}{90} f^{(4)}(\mu_j).
\end{align}
\] Summing over all \(N/2\) double panels and applying the Intermediate Value Theorem gives \[
\begin{align}
I(f) - S_N(f) = -\frac{h^5}{90} \sum_{j=0}^{N/2 - 1} f^{(4)}(\mu_j) = -\frac{N h^5}{180} f^{(4)}(\xi_S) = -\frac{b - a}{180} h^4 f^{(4)}(\xi_S).
\end{align}
\]

20.3 Peano Kernel Error Representation
The Peano kernel theorem provides a unified framework for representing the error of any quadrature rule with algebraic degree of exactness \(m\).






NoteTheorem: Peano Kernel Theorem



Let \(Q\) be a quadrature rule on \([a, b]\) with error functional \(E(f) = \int_a^b f(x)\,dx - Q(f)\). If \(E(p) = 0\) for all \(p \in \mathcal{P}_m\), then for every \(f \in C^{m+1}[a, b]\), \[
\begin{align}
E(f) = \int_a^b f^{(m+1)}(t) K(t)\,dt,
\end{align}
\] where the Peano kernel \(K(t)\) is defined by applying the error functional to the truncated power function: \[
\begin{align}
K(t) = \frac{1}{m!} E_x \left( (x - t)_+^m \right), \qquad (x - t)_+ = \max(0, x - t).
\end{align}
\] If \(K(t)\) does not change sign on \([a, b]\), then there exists \(\xi \in (a, b)\) such that \[
\begin{align}
E(f) = \frac{f^{(m+1)}(\xi)}{(m+1)!} E(x^{m+1}).
\end{align}
\]








TipProof





By Taylor’s theorem with integral remainder expanded about \(a\): \[
\begin{align}
f(x) = \sum_{k=0}^m \frac{f^{(k)}(a)}{k!} (x - a)^k + \frac{1}{m!} \int_a^x f^{(m+1)}(t) (x - t)^m\,dt.
\end{align}
\] Extending the upper integration limit using the truncated power notation \((x - t)_+^m\) allows writing the remainder as \(\frac{1}{m!} \int_a^b f^{(m+1)}(t) (x - t)_+^m\,dt\). Applying the linear functional \(E\) to both sides, the polynomial terms vanish because \(E(p) = 0\) for all \(p \in \mathcal{P}_m\). Exchanging the order of integration and the functional gives \[
\begin{align}
E(f) = E_x \left( \frac{1}{m!} \int_a^b f^{(m+1)}(t) (x - t)_+^m\,dt \right) = \int_a^b f^{(m+1)}(t) \left[ \frac{1}{m!} E_x\left( (x - t)_+^m \right) \right] dt = \int_a^b f^{(m+1)}(t) K(t)\,dt.
\end{align}
\] If \(K(t)\) is of constant sign, the Mean Value Theorem for Integrals implies \(E(f) = f^{(m+1)}(\xi) \int_a^b K(t)\,dt\). Testing the identity with \(f(x) = x^{m+1}\), we have \(f^{(m+1)}(x) = (m+1)!\), which yields \(E(x^{m+1}) = (m+1)! \int_a^b K(t)\,dt\). Substituting this integral proves the claim.

20.4 Gauss–Legendre Quadrature
Newton–Cotes rules fix equispaced nodes and optimize only the weights. Gaussian quadrature optimizes both the nodes \(x_1, ..., x_p\) and the weights \(w_1, ..., w_p\), providing \(2p\) degrees of freedom to maximize the degree of exactness.






NoteLemma: Roots of Orthogonal Polynomials



Let \(P_p(x)\) be the degree-\(p\) Legendre polynomial orthogonal on \([-1, 1]\) with respect to weight \(w(x) \equiv 1\): \[
\begin{align}
\int_{-1}^1 P_p(x) q(x)\,dx = 0 \qquad \text{for all } q \in \mathcal{P}_{p-1}.
\end{align}
\] Then \(P_p(x)\) has exactly \(p\) distinct, real roots, and all roots lie in the open interval \((-1, 1)\).








TipProof





Let \(x_1, ..., x_k\) denote the points in \((-1, 1)\) where \(P_p(x)\) changes sign. Because \(P_p\) is continuous and orthogonal to the constant polynomial \(q(x) = 1\), we have \(\int_{-1}^1 P_p(x)\,dx = 0\), so \(P_p\) must change sign at least once in \((-1, 1)\), meaning \(k \geq 1\). Suppose for contradiction that \(k &lt; p\). Construct the polynomial \(q(x) = \prod_{j=1}^k (x - x_j) \in \mathcal{P}_k\). By construction, \(P_p(x)\) and \(q(x)\) change sign at identical points in \((-1, 1)\), so their product \(P_p(x) q(x)\) has constant sign on \([-1, 1]\) and is not identically zero. Consequently, \[
\begin{align}
\int_{-1}^1 P_p(x) q(x)\,dx \neq 0.
\end{align}
\] However, because \(\deg(q) = k &lt; p\), orthogonality requires \(\int_{-1}^1 P_p(x) q(x)\,dx = 0\), producing a contradiction. Therefore, \(k = p\), proving that \(P_p\) has \(p\) distinct real roots in \((-1, 1)\).









NoteTheorem: Fundamental Theorem of Gaussian Quadrature



Let \(x_1, ..., x_p\) be the roots of the degree-\(p\) Legendre polynomial \(P_p(x)\) on \([-1, 1]\), and let \(w_i = \int_{-1}^1 L_i(x)\,dx\). The \(p\)-point Gauss–Legendre quadrature rule \[
\begin{align}
\int_{-1}^1 f(x)\,dx \approx \sum_{i=1}^p w_i f(x_i)
\end{align}
\] has degree of exactness \(2p - 1\). No \(p\)-point quadrature rule can achieve a degree of exactness higher than \(2p - 1\). Furthermore, all weights are strictly positive: \(w_i &gt; 0\) for all \(i = 1, ..., p\).








TipProof





Let \(f \in \mathcal{P}_{2p-1}\). Polynomial long division of \(f(x)\) by \(P_p(x)\) yields \[
\begin{align}
f(x) = q(x) P_p(x) + r(x), \qquad \deg(q) \leq p - 1, \quad \deg(r) \leq p - 1.
\end{align}
\] Integrating both sides over \([-1, 1]\): \[
\begin{align}
\int_{-1}^1 f(x)\,dx = \int_{-1}^1 q(x) P_p(x)\,dx + \int_{-1}^1 r(x)\,dx.
\end{align}
\] Because \(\deg(q) \leq p - 1\), orthogonality of \(P_p\) against \(\mathcal{P}_{p-1}\) implies \(\int_{-1}^1 q(x) P_p(x)\,dx = 0\), giving \[
\begin{align}
\int_{-1}^1 f(x)\,dx = \int_{-1}^1 r(x)\,dx.
\end{align}
\] Applying the \(p\)-point quadrature sum at the Legendre roots \(\{x_i\}_{i=1}^p\) where \(P_p(x_i) = 0\): \[
\begin{align}
\sum_{i=1}^p w_i f(x_i) &= \sum_{i=1}^p w_i \left[ q(x_i) P_p(x_i) + r(x_i) \right] \\
    &= \sum_{i=1}^p w_i \left[ 0 + r(x_i) \right] = \sum_{i=1}^p w_i r(x_i).
\end{align}
\] Because \(\deg(r) \leq p - 1\), interpolatory quadrature on \(p\) distinct nodes integrates \(r(x)\) exactly by the referenced result: \[
\begin{align}
\sum_{i=1}^p w_i r(x_i) = \int_{-1}^1 r(x)\,dx = \int_{-1}^1 f(x)\,dx.
\end{align}
\] Thus \(\sum_{i=1}^p w_i f(x_i) = \int_{-1}^1 f(x)\,dx\) for all \(f \in \mathcal{P}_{2p-1}\). To prove that \(2p - 1\) is the maximal degree, define the nodal polynomial \[
\begin{align}
g(x) = \prod_{i=1}^p (x - x_i)^2 \in \mathcal{P}_{2p}.
\end{align}
\] Because \(g(x) \geq 0\) and vanishes only at isolated points, its integral is strictly positive: \[
\begin{align}
\int_{-1}^1 g(x)\,dx &gt; 0.
\end{align}
\] However, evaluating the quadrature sum yields \[
\begin{align}
\sum_{i=1}^p w_i g(x_i) = \sum_{i=1}^p w_i \cdot 0 = 0 \neq \int_{-1}^1 g(x)\,dx.
\end{align}
\] Therefore no \(p\)-point quadrature rule can integrate all degree-\(2p\) polynomials. For weight positivity, let \(L_i \in \mathcal{P}_{p-1}\) be the \(i\)-th Lagrange basis polynomial. The square satisfies \(\deg(L_i^2) = 2p - 2 \leq 2p - 1\). The rule integrates \(L_i^2\) exactly: \[
\begin{align}
0 &lt; \int_{-1}^1 L_i^2(x)\,dx = \sum_{j=1}^p w_j L_i^2(x_j) = \sum_{j=1}^p w_j \delta_{ij} = w_i.
\end{align}
\] Every weight \(w_i\) is strictly positive.

20.5 Richardson Extrapolation and Romberg Integration
When the error of a numerical method admits an asymptotic expansion in powers of \(h\), Richardson extrapolation algebraically cancels the leading error terms.






NoteTheorem: Euler–Maclaurin Summation and Romberg Integration



Let \(f \in C^{2m+2}[a, b]\). The composite trapezoidal rule satisfies the asymptotic expansion \[
\begin{align}
T(h) = I(f) + c_2 h^2 + c_4 h^4 + c_6 h^6 + ... + c_{2m} h^{2m} + O(h^{2m+2}),
\end{align}
\] where \(c_{2k} = \frac{B_{2k}}{(2k)!}[f^{(2k-1)}(b) - f^{(2k-1)}(a)]\) depend on the Bernoulli numbers \(B_{2k}\). Romberg integration systematically eliminates powers of \(h^2\) via the tableau recurrence \[
\begin{align}
R(k, 0) = T(h_k), \qquad R(k, j) = \frac{4^j R(k, j-1) - R(k-1, j-1)}{4^j - 1} \quad (1 \leq j \leq k),
\end{align}
\] where \(h_k = (b - a)/2^k\). Column \(j\) achieves order \(O(h^{2j+2})\).








TipProof





At column 1, \(R(k, 0) = I(f) + c_2 h_k^2 + O(h_k^4)\) and \(R(k-1, 0) = I(f) + c_2 (2h_k)^2 + O(h_k^4) = I(f) + 4 c_2 h_k^2 + O(h_k^4)\). Forming the linear combination \[
\begin{align}
\frac{4 R(k, 0) - R(k-1, 0)}{3} = \frac{4(I(f) + c_2 h_k^2) - (I(f) + 4 c_2 h_k^2)}{3} + O(h_k^4) = I(f) + O(h_k^4)
\end{align}
\] cancels the \(h^2\) error term. Induction on \(j\) shows that combining step sizes differing by a factor of 2 cancels the leading \(h^{2j}\) error term with weights \(\frac{4^j}{4^j - 1}\) and \(-\frac{1}{4^j - 1}\).

20.6 Periodic Functions and Spectral Convergence






NoteTheorem: Spectral Accuracy of the Trapezoidal Rule on Periodic Domains



Let \(f\) be a smooth, \(2\pi\)-periodic function on \(\fR\). All endpoint derivatives in the Euler–Maclaurin expansion cancel identically: \[
\begin{align}
f^{(2k-1)}(2\pi) - f^{(2k-1)}(0) = 0 \qquad \text{for all } k \geq 1.
\end{align}
\] Consequently, all algebraic error coefficients \(c_2, c_4, c_6, ...\) vanish. If \(f\) is analytic in a strip \(\{z \in \fC : |\operatorname{Im}(z)| &lt; d\}\), the composite trapezoidal rule converges exponentially fast: \[
\begin{align}
|I(f) - T_N(f)| \leq C e^{-d N}.
\end{align}
\] On smooth periodic domains, the composite trapezoidal rule achieves spectral accuracy.








TipProof





Because \(f\) is periodic and smooth, \(f^{(m)}(2\pi) = f^{(m)}(0)\) for all \(m \geq 0\). Every term \(c_{2k}\) in the Euler–Maclaurin formula is proportional to \(f^{(2k-1)}(2\pi) - f^{(2k-1)}(0) = 0\), so the error decays faster than any power of \(h = 2\pi / N\). To quantify the exponential rate, expand \(f(x)\) in its Fourier series: \[
\begin{align}
f(x) = \sum_{k=-\infty}^\infty \hat{f}_k e^{ikx}, \qquad \hat{f}_k = \frac{1}{2\pi} \int_0^{2\pi} f(x) e^{-ikx}\,dx.
\end{align}
\] The exact integral is \(I(f) = \int_0^{2\pi} f(x)\,dx = 2\pi \hat{f}_0\). The composite trapezoidal rule with \(h = 2\pi / N\) and nodes \(x_j = 2\pi j / N\) for \(j = 0, ..., N - 1\) gives \[
\begin{align}
T_N(f) = \frac{2\pi}{N} \sum_{j=0}^{N-1} f(x_j) &= \frac{2\pi}{N} \sum_{j=0}^{N-1} \sum_{k=-\infty}^\infty \hat{f}_k e^{2\pi i j k / N} \\
    &= 2\pi \sum_{k=-\infty}^\infty \hat{f}_k \left( \frac{1}{N} \sum_{j=0}^{N-1} e^{2\pi i j k / N} \right).
\end{align}
\] The inner sum satisfies discrete orthogonality: \[
\begin{align}
\frac{1}{N} \sum_{j=0}^{N-1} e^{2\pi i j k / N} = \begin{cases}
        1, & k = mN \text{ for some } m \in \fZ, \\
        0, & k \not\equiv 0 \pmod N.
    \end{cases}
\end{align}
\] Substituting this indicator isolates the aliased modes: \[
\begin{align}
T_N(f) = 2\pi \sum_{m=-\infty}^\infty \hat{f}_{mN} = 2\pi \hat{f}_0 + 2\pi \sum_{m \neq 0} \hat{f}_{mN}.
\end{align}
\] The quadrature error is therefore the tail sum: \[
\begin{align}
I(f) - T_N(f) = -2\pi \sum_{m \neq 0} \hat{f}_{mN}.
\end{align}
\] Because \(f\) is analytic and bounded by \(M_0\) in the strip \(|\operatorname{Im}(z)| &lt; d\), shifting the integration path in the definition of \(\hat{f}_k\) gives the geometric decay \(|\hat{f}_k| \leq M e^{-d|k|}\). Applying this bound: \[
\begin{align}
|I(f) - T_N(f)| \leq 2\pi M \sum_{m \neq 0} e^{-d|m|N} = 4\pi M \sum_{m=1}^\infty (e^{-dN})^m = 4\pi M \frac{e^{-dN}}{1 - e^{-dN}} = O(e^{-dN}).
\end{align}
\]

NoteDefinition: Periodic Grid Signal



A discrete signal \(\bx \in \fC^n\) represents samples of a periodic function on the uniform grid \(x_j = 2\pi j / n\) for \(j = 0, 1, ..., n-1\), with the periodic index convention \[
\begin{align}
x_{j+n} = x_j \qquad \text{for all } j \in \fZ.
\end{align}
\]








NoteDefinition: DFT Matrix



Let \(\omega_n = e^{-2\pi i / n}\) denote the primitive \(n\)-th root of unity. The Discrete Fourier Transform matrix \(\bF \in \fC^{n \times n}\) has entries \[
\begin{align}
F_{jk} = \frac{1}{\sqrt{n}} \omega_n^{jk} = \frac{1}{\sqrt{n}} e^{-2\pi i j k / n}, \qquad 0 \leq j, k \leq n-1.
\end{align}
\] The forward transform maps a spatial signal \(\bx \in \fC^n\) to its frequency representation \(\hat{\bx} = \bF\bx\).








NoteLemma: Unitarity of the DFT Matrix



The normalized DFT matrix \(\bF\) is unitary: \[
\begin{align}
\bF^* \bF = \bF \bF^* = \bI_n.
\end{align}
\] Consequently, the inverse transform is given by the conjugate transpose: \(\bF^{-1} = \bF^*\).








TipProof





We evaluate the \((j, \ell)\) entry of the Gram matrix product \(\bF^* \bF\). Because \((\bF^*)_{jk} = \overline{F_{kj}} = \frac{1}{\sqrt{n}} \omega_n^{-jk}\), we have \[
\begin{align}
(\bF^* \bF)_{j\ell} = \sum_{k=0}^{n-1} (\bF^*)_{jk} F_{k\ell} = \frac{1}{n} \sum_{k=0}^{n-1} \omega_n^{-jk} \omega_n^{k\ell} = \frac{1}{n} \sum_{k=0}^{n-1} \left( \omega_n^{\ell - j} \right)^k.
\end{align}
\] If \(j = \ell\), each term in the summation equals \(\omega_n^0 = 1\), yielding \(\frac{1}{n} \sum_{k=0}^{n-1} 1 = 1\). If \(j \neq \ell\), let \(r = \omega_n^{\ell - j}\). Because \(-(n-1) \leq \ell - j \leq n-1\) and \(j \neq \ell\), the base \(r \neq 1\), while \(r^n = (\omega_n^n)^{\ell - j} = 1^{\ell - j} = 1\). The finite geometric series sums to \[
\begin{align}
\sum_{k=0}^{n-1} r^k = \frac{1 - r^n}{1 - r} = \frac{1 - 1}{1 - r} = 0.
\end{align}
\] Therefore, \((\bF^* \bF)_{j\ell} = \delta_{j\ell}\), which establishes \(\bF^* \bF = \bI_n\).









NoteTheorem: Parseval’s Identity and Isometry



The Discrete Fourier Transform is an isometry on \(\fC^n\) with respect to the Euclidean norm: \[
\begin{align}
\|\bF\bx\|_2 = \|\bx\|_2 \qquad \text{for all } \bx \in \fC^n.
\end{align}
\] The inner product is likewise preserved: \(\langle \bF\bx, \bF\by \rangle = \langle \bx, \by \rangle\).








TipProof





Using the referenced result, the Euclidean norm of the transformed vector satisfies \[
\begin{align}
\|\bF\bx\|_2^2 = (\bF\bx)^* (\bF\bx) = \bx^* \bF^* \bF \bx = \bx^* \bI \bx = \bx^* \bx = \|\bx\|_2^2.
\end{align}
\] Taking positive square roots proves the claim. For general vectors, \(\langle \bF\bx, \bF\by \rangle = (\bF\bx)^* (\bF\by) = \bx^* \bF^* \bF \by = \bx^* \by = \langle \bx, \by \rangle\).









NoteDefinition: Frequency Bins and Nyquist Limit



For a signal sampled on \(n\) points with uniform spacing \(h = 2\pi / n\), integer frequency index \(k\) corresponds to the continuous Fourier mode \(e^{ikx}\). The distinct resolvable frequencies are ordered as \[
\begin{align}
0, 1, 2, ..., \lfloor n/2 \rfloor, -\lfloor (n-1)/2 \rfloor, ..., -1.
\end{align}
\] The maximum resolvable frequency is the Nyquist frequency \(k_{\text{Nyq}} = \lfloor n/2 \rfloor\). Any continuous mode with frequency \(|k| &gt; k_{\text{Nyq}}\) aliases to a lower grid frequency satisfying \(k' \equiv k \pmod n\).








TipRemark



(Normalization convention) Numerical libraries such as NumPy’s np.fft.fft define the forward transform without the factor \(1/\sqrt{n}\), so that \(\hat{\bx}_{\text{numpy}} = \sum_{j=0}^{n-1} x_j \omega_n^{jk}\), and place the full factor \(1/n\) in the inverse transform np.fft.ifft. This choice scales the entries by \(\sqrt{n}\) relative to the unitary operator \(\bF\) defined here.

21.2 Circulant Matrices and the Convolution Theorem
A linear system or filtering operation that commutes with spatial translation is represented by a circulant matrix. The Discrete Fourier Transform diagonalizes every circulant matrix.






NoteDefinition: Circulant Matrix



Let \(\bc = (c_0, c_1, ..., c_{n-1})^T \in \fC^n\). The circulant matrix \(\mathbf{C} = \operatorname{circ}(\bc) \in \fC^{n \times n}\) has entries \(C_{jk} = c_{(j - k) \pmod n}\): \[
\begin{align}
\mathbf{C} = \begin{pmatrix}
        c_0 & c_{n-1} & c_{n-2} & ... & c_1 \\
        c_1 & c_0 & c_{n-1} & ... & c_2 \\
        c_2 & c_1 & c_0 & ... & c_3 \\
        \vdots & \vdots & \vdots & \ddots & \vdots \\
        c_{n-1} & c_{n-2} & c_{n-3} & ... & c_0
    \end{pmatrix}.
\end{align}
\]








NoteTheorem: Spectral Diagonalization of Circulant Matrices



Every circulant matrix \(\mathbf{C} = \operatorname{circ}(\bc)\) is diagonalized by the unitary DFT matrix: \[
\begin{align}
\mathbf{C} = \bF^* \boldsymbol{\Lambda} \bF,
\end{align}
\] where the diagonal eigenvalue matrix \(\boldsymbol{\Lambda} = \operatorname{diag}(\lambda_0, ..., \lambda_{n-1})\) has entries equal to the unnormalized DFT coefficients of the first column: \[
\begin{align}
\lambda_k = \sqrt{n} (\bF\bc)_k = \sum_{j=0}^{n-1} c_j \omega_n^{jk}.
\end{align}
\]








TipProof





Let \(\mathbf{S} = \operatorname{circ}(\be_2) \in \fR^{n \times n}\) be the cyclic permutation matrix that shifts coordinate indices forward by one: \(\mathbf{S}\bx = (x_{n-1}, x_0, x_1, ..., x_{n-2})^T\). Powers of \(\mathbf{S}\) shift indices by \(j\) positions, satisfying \(\mathbf{S}^n = \bI\). Every circulant matrix is a polynomial in \(\mathbf{S}\): \[
\begin{align}
\mathbf{C} = \sum_{j=0}^{n-1} c_j \mathbf{S}^j.
\end{align}
\] Let \(\bv_k\) denote the \(k\)-th column of \(\bF^*\), with entries \((\bv_k)_j = \frac{1}{\sqrt{n}} \omega_n^{-jk}\). Multiplying by the shift matrix gives \[
\begin{align}
(\mathbf{S}\bv_k)_j = (\bv_k)_{(j-1) \pmod n} = \frac{1}{\sqrt{n}} \omega_n^{-(j-1)k} = \omega_n^k \left( \frac{1}{\sqrt{n}} \omega_n^{-jk} \right) = \omega_n^k (\bv_k)_j.
\end{align}
\] Thus \(\bv_k\) is an eigenvector of \(\mathbf{S}\) with eigenvalue \(\omega_n^k\). Applying the polynomial representation of \(\mathbf{C}\) to \(\bv_k\) yields \[
\begin{align}
\mathbf{C}\bv_k = \sum_{j=0}^{n-1} c_j \mathbf{S}^j \bv_k = \sum_{j=0}^{n-1} c_j (\omega_n^k)^j \bv_k = \left( \sum_{j=0}^{n-1} c_j \omega_n^{jk} \right) \bv_k = \lambda_k \bv_k.
\end{align}
\] Collecting all \(n\) eigenvector equations in matrix form gives \(\mathbf{C}\bF^* = \bF^* \boldsymbol{\Lambda}\). Multiplying on the right by \(\bF\) yields \(\mathbf{C} = \bF^* \boldsymbol{\Lambda} \bF\).









NoteTheorem: Discrete Convolution Theorem



Let \(\bc, \bx \in \fC^n\). The circular convolution \(\bz = \bc * \bx\) has components \[
\begin{align}
z_j = \sum_{k=0}^{n-1} c_k x_{(j - k) \pmod n} = (\mathbf{C}\bx)_j, \qquad \mathbf{C} = \operatorname{circ}(\bc).
\end{align}
\] Applying the Discrete Fourier Transform converts convolution to entrywise multiplication: \[
\begin{align}
\bF(\bc * \bx) = \sqrt{n} (\bF\bc) \odot (\bF\bx),
\end{align}
\] where \(\odot\) denotes the Hadamard (entrywise) vector product.








TipProof





Because \(\bz = \mathbf{C}\bx\) and \(\mathbf{C} = \bF^* \boldsymbol{\Lambda} \bF\) by the referenced result, we have \[
\begin{align}
\bF\bz = \bF(\mathbf{C}\bx) = \bF(\bF^* \boldsymbol{\Lambda} \bF \bx) = \boldsymbol{\Lambda}(\bF\bx).
\end{align}
\] Because \(\boldsymbol{\Lambda}\) is diagonal with entries \(\lambda_k = \sqrt{n}(\bF\bc)_k\), matrix-vector multiplication by \(\boldsymbol{\Lambda}\) scales each component of \(\bF\bx\) by \(\sqrt{n}(\bF\bc)_k\): \[
\begin{align}
(\bF\bz)_k = \lambda_k (\bF\bx)_k = \sqrt{n}(\bF\bc)_k (\bF\bx)_k.
\end{align}
\] Writing this component identity in vector form yields \(\bF(\bc * \bx) = \sqrt{n} (\bF\bc) \odot (\bF\bx)\).

21.3 The Fast Fourier Transform
Computing a matrix-vector product with a general dense \(n \times n\) matrix requires \(2n^2\) flops. The Cooley–Tukey Fast Fourier Transform (FFT) factors the DFT matrix into sparse structured factors, reducing the complexity to \(O(n \log n)\) operations.






NoteTheorem: Radix-2 Cooley–Tukey Decomposition



Let \(n\) be an even integer, and let \(\bx \in \fC^n\). Partition \(\bx\) into even-indexed and odd-indexed subvectors \(\bx_E, \bx_O \in \fC^{n/2}\) defined by \((\bx_E)_j = x_{2j}\) and \((\bx_O)_j = x_{2j+1}\) for \(j = 0, ..., n/2 - 1\). The \(n\)-point unnormalized DFT \(\hat{x}_k = \sum_{j=0}^{n-1} x_j \omega_n^{jk}\) satisfies the butterfly identity \[
\begin{align}
\hat{x}_k &= (\hat{\bx}_E)_k + \omega_n^k (\hat{\bx}_O)_k, \\
    \hat{x}_{k + n/2} &= (\hat{\bx}_E)_k - \omega_n^k (\hat{\bx}_O)_k,
\end{align}
\] for each \(k = 0, 1, ..., n/2 - 1\), where \(\hat{\bx}_E, \hat{\bx}_O\) are the \((n/2)\)-point DFTs of \(\bx_E\) and \(\bx_O\). When \(n = 2^p\), applying this reduction recursively requires \(O(n \log n)\) floating-point operations.








TipProof





Splitting the DFT sum into even and odd indices gives \[
\begin{align}
\hat{x}_k = \sum_{m=0}^{n/2 - 1} x_{2m} \omega_n^{2mk} + \sum_{m=0}^{n/2 - 1} x_{2m+1} \omega_n^{(2m+1)k}.
\end{align}
\] Because \(\omega_n^2 = e^{-4\pi i / n} = e^{-2\pi i / (n/2)} = \omega_{n/2}\), the first sum is the \((n/2)\)-point transform of \(\bx_E\): \[
\begin{align}
\sum_{m=0}^{n/2 - 1} x_{2m} \omega_{n/2}^{mk} = (\hat{\bx}_E)_k.
\end{align}
\] Factoring \(\omega_n^k\) out of the second sum gives \(\omega_n^k \sum_{m=0}^{n/2 - 1} x_{2m+1} \omega_{n/2}^{mk} = \omega_n^k (\hat{\bx}_O)_k\). This establishes the first identity: \[
\begin{align}
\hat{x}_k = (\hat{\bx}_E)_k + \omega_n^k (\hat{\bx}_O)_k.
\end{align}
\] For the second half of the spectrum \(k + n/2\), the periodicity \(\omega_{n/2}^{m(k + n/2)} = \omega_{n/2}^{mk} (\omega_{n/2}^{n/2})^m = \omega_{n/2}^{mk}\) and the half-period phase shift \[
\begin{align}
\omega_n^{k + n/2} = \omega_n^k \omega_n^{n/2} = \omega_n^k e^{-\pi i} = -\omega_n^k
\end{align}
\] give the complementary butterfly equation: \[
\begin{align}
\hat{x}_{k + n/2} = (\hat{\bx}_E)_k - \omega_n^k (\hat{\bx}_O)_k.
\end{align}
\] In matrix form, introducing the diagonal twiddle matrix \(\boldsymbol{\Omega}_{n/2} = \operatorname{diag}(1, \omega_n, ..., \omega_n^{n/2-1})\) and the even–odd permutation matrix \(\bP_n\): \[
\begin{align}
\hat{\bx} = \begin{pmatrix} \bI_{n/2} & \boldsymbol{\Omega}_{n/2} \\ \bI_{n/2} & -\boldsymbol{\Omega}_{n/2} \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} \hat{\bx}_E \\ \hat{\bx}_O \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} \bI_{n/2} & \boldsymbol{\Omega}_{n/2} \\ \bI_{n/2} & -\boldsymbol{\Omega}_{n/2} \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} \mathbf{W}_{n/2} & \bzero \\ \bzero & \mathbf{W}_{n/2} \end{pmatrix} \bP_n \bx.
\end{align}
\] At each recursion stage, the butterfly recombination performs \(n/2\) complex multiplications and \(n\) additions. The arithmetic complexity satisfies the recurrence \[
\begin{align}
T(n) = 2 T(n/2) + c n.
\end{align}
\] For \(n = 2^p\), the depth of the recursion tree is \(\log_2 n\), yielding total cost \(T(n) = c n \log_2 n = O(n \log n)\).

23.2 Convergence Theory and the Spectral Radius
The convergence of a linear stationary iteration is determined entirely by the spectral properties of the iteration matrix \(\bT\).






NoteDefinition: Spectral Radius



Let \(\bT \in \fR^{n \times n}\) with spectrum \(\sigma(\bT) \subset \fC\). The spectral radius of \(\bT\) is defined as the maximum modulus among all its eigenvalues: \[
\begin{align}
\rho(\bT) = \max \{ |\lambda| : \lambda \in \sigma(\bT) \}.
\end{align}
\]








NoteTheorem: Gelfand’s Spectral Radius Theorem



For any matrix norm \(\|\cdot\|\) induced by a vector norm on \(\fC^n\) and any matrix \(\bT \in \fR^{n \times n}\), \[
\begin{align}
\rho(\bT) = \lim_{k \to \infty} \|\bT^k\|^{1/k}.
\end{align}
\] In particular, for any \(\varepsilon &gt; 0\), there exists an induced matrix norm \(\|\cdot\|_*\) such that \(\|\bT\|_* \leq \rho(\bT) + \varepsilon\).








NoteTheorem: Fundamental Convergence Criterion for Stationary Iterations



The stationary iteration \(\bx^{(k+1)} = \bT\bx^{(k)} + \bc\) converges to the unique solution \(\bx^* = \bA^{-1}\bb\) for every initial guess \(\bx^{(0)} \in \fR^n\) if and only if \[
\begin{align}
\rho(\bT) &lt; 1.
\end{align}
\]








TipProof





Let \(\be^{(k)} = \bx^{(k)} - \bx^*\) denote the error vector at step \(k\). Subtracting the fixed-point identity \(\bx^* = \bT\bx^* + \bc\) from the iteration formula \(\bx^{(k+1)} = \bT\bx^{(k)} + \bc\): \[
\begin{align}
\be^{(k+1)}
    &= \bx^{(k+1)} - \bx^* \\
    &= (\bT\bx^{(k)} + \bc) - (\bT\bx^* + \bc) \\
    &= \bT(\bx^{(k)} - \bx^*)
    = \bT \be^{(k)}.
\end{align}
\] Applying this recurrence recursively yields \[
\begin{align}
\be^{(k)} = \bT^k \be^{(0)}.
\end{align}
\] The error \(\be^{(k)} \to \bzero\) for all initial errors \(\be^{(0)} \in \fR^n\) if and only if \(\lim_{k\to\infty} \bT^k = \bzero\).
If \(\rho(\bT) &lt; 1\), choose \(\varepsilon = \frac{1 - \rho(\bT)}{2} &gt; 0\). By Gelfand’s theorem (the referenced result), there exists an induced matrix norm \(\|\cdot\|_*\) such that \[
\begin{align}
\|\bT\|_* \leq \rho(\bT) + \varepsilon = \frac{1 + \rho(\bT)}{2} &lt; 1.
\end{align}
\] Then \(\|\bT^k\|_* \leq \|\bT\|_*^k \to 0\) as \(k \to \infty\), so \(\bT^k \to \bzero\) and \(\|\be^{(k)}\|_* \leq \|\bT\|_*^k \|\be^{(0)}\|_* \to 0\).
Conversely, suppose \(\bT^k \to \bzero\). If \(\lambda \in \sigma(\bT)\) with eigenvector \(\bv \neq \bzero\), then \(\bT^k \bv = \lambda^k \bv\). For this sequence to converge to \(\bzero\), we must have \(|\lambda|^k \to 0\), forcing \(|\lambda| &lt; 1\). Because this holds for all eigenvalues, \(\rho(\bT) &lt; 1\).









TipRemark



(Asymptotic convergence rate and iteration count) The quantity \(-\log_{10} \rho(\bT)\) represents the asymptotic rate of convergence, measuring the number of decimal digits of accuracy gained per iteration. To reduce the initial error by a relative tolerance factor \(\varepsilon\), the required number of iterations satisfies \[
\begin{align}
k \approx \frac{\ln(1/\varepsilon)}{-\ln \rho(\bT)}.
\end{align}
\] If \(\rho(\bT) = 0.99\), we have \(-\ln(0.99) \approx 0.01005\), which requires approximately \(230\) iterations to reduce the error by a factor of ten. As \(\rho(\bT) \to 1\), convergence slows drastically.

NoteDefinition: Jacobi Iteration



Let \(\bA \in \fR^{n \times n}\) with nonzero diagonal entries \(a_{ii} \neq 0\). The Jacobi method corresponds to the splitting \(\bM = \bD\) and \(\mathbf{N} = \bL_A + \bU_A\). In component form, the update is \[
\begin{align}
x_i^{(k+1)} = \frac{1}{a_{ii}} \left( b_i - \sum_{j \neq i} a_{ij} x_j^{(k)} \right), \qquad i = 1, 2, ..., n.
\end{align}
\] In matrix notation, the iteration is given by \[
\begin{align}
\bx^{(k+1)} = \bT_J \bx^{(k)} + \bc_J,
\end{align}
\] where \(\bT_J = \bD^{-1}(\bL_A + \bU_A) = \bI - \bD^{-1}\bA\) is the Jacobi iteration matrix and \(\bc_J = \bD^{-1}\bb\).








TipRemark



(Parallel execution and memory) Because each component \(x_i^{(k+1)}\) depends only on values from the previous iteration vector \(\bx^{(k)}\), all \(n\) components can be computed concurrently. This fine-grained parallelism makes Jacobi attractive on vector and GPU hardware. However, it requires double buffering in memory to store \(\bx^{(k)}\) and \(\bx^{(k+1)}\) simultaneously.

24.2 The Gauss–Seidel Method
The Gauss–Seidel method improves upon Jacobi by using updated variable values as soon as they become available within the current sweep.






NoteDefinition: Gauss–Seidel Iteration



Let \(\bA \in \fR^{n \times n}\) with nonzero diagonal entries. The Gauss–Seidel method corresponds to the triangular splitting \(\bM = \bD - \bL_A\) and \(\mathbf{N} = \bU_A\). In component form, the update is \[
\begin{align}
x_i^{(k+1)} = \frac{1}{a_{ii}} \left( b_i - \sum_{j &lt; i} a_{ij} x_j^{(k+1)} - \sum_{j &gt; i} a_{ij} x_j^{(k)} \right), \qquad i = 1, 2, ..., n.
\end{align}
\] In matrix notation, the iteration is given by \[
\begin{align}
(\bD - \bL_A) \bx^{(k+1)} = \bU_A \bx^{(k)} + \bb \iff \bx^{(k+1)} = \bT_{GS} \bx^{(k)} + \bc_{GS},
\end{align}
\] where \(\bT_{GS} = (\bD - \bL_A)^{-1}\bU_A\) is the Gauss–Seidel iteration matrix and \(\bc_{GS} = (\bD - \bL_A)^{-1}\bb\).








TipRemark



(Sequential dependencies and in-place updates) Because the calculation of \(x_i^{(k+1)}\) requires the newly computed values \(x_1^{(k+1)}, ..., x_{i-1}^{(k+1)}\), the standard Gauss–Seidel sweep is inherently sequential. However, it permits in-place updates, in which new values immediately overwrite old values in a single vector. For many classes of matrices, Gauss–Seidel converges approximately twice as fast as Jacobi.








NoteExample



(Comparison of one Jacobi and Gauss–Seidel step) Consider the linear system \[
\begin{align}
\begin{pmatrix} 4 & 1 & 0 \\ 1 & 4 & 1 \\ 0 & 1 & 4 \end{pmatrix}
    \begin{pmatrix} x_1 \\ x_2 \\ x_3 \end{pmatrix}
    =
    \begin{pmatrix} 5 \\ 6 \\ 5 \end{pmatrix},
\end{align}
\] whose exact solution is \(\bx^* = (1, 1, 1)^T\). Let the initial guess be \(\bx^{(0)} = (0, 0, 0)^T\).

Jacobi Step: \[
  \begin{align}
  x_1^{(1)} &= \frac{1}{4}(5 - 0 - 0) = 1.25, \\
          x_2^{(1)} &= \frac{1}{4}(6 - 0 - 0) = 1.50, \\
          x_3^{(1)} &= \frac{1}{4}(5 - 0 - 0) = 1.25, \qquad \implies \bx_J^{(1)} = \begin{pmatrix} 1.25 \\ 1.50 \\ 1.25 \end{pmatrix}.
  \end{align}
  \]
Gauss–Seidel Step: \[
  \begin{align}
  x_1^{(1)} &= \frac{1}{4}(5 - 0 - 0) = 1.25, \\
          x_2^{(1)} &= \frac{1}{4}(6 - (1.25) - 0) = \frac{4.75}{4} = 1.1875, \\
          x_3^{(1)} &= \frac{1}{4}(5 - (1.1875) - 0) = \frac{3.8125}{4} \approx 0.9531, \qquad \implies \bx_{GS}^{(1)} \approx \begin{pmatrix} 1.2500 \\ 1.1875 \\ 0.9531 \end{pmatrix}.
  \end{align}
  \]

The Gauss–Seidel iterate is closer to the true solution \((1, 1, 1)^T\) because the update for \(x_2\) immediately used the new value of \(x_1\), and the update for \(x_3\) used the new value of \(x_2\).

24.3 Convergence Theorems






NoteTheorem: Convergence Under Strict Diagonal Dominance



A matrix \(\bA \in \fR^{n \times n}\) is strictly diagonally dominant if \[
\begin{align}
|a_{ii}| &gt; \sum_{j \neq i} |a_{ij}| \qquad \text{for all } i = 1, 2, ..., n.
\end{align}
\] If \(\bA\) is strictly diagonally dominant, then both the Jacobi iteration and the Gauss–Seidel iteration converge for any initial guess \(\bx^{(0)} \in \fR^n\).








TipProof





We show that the matrix \(\infty\)-norm of the iteration matrix \(\bT_J = \bD^{-1}(\bL_A + \bU_A)\) is strictly bounded by one. The entries of \(\bT_J\) are \[
\begin{align}
(\bT_J)_{ij} = \begin{cases}
        -\frac{a_{ij}}{a_{ii}}, & i \neq j, \\
        0, & i = j.
    \end{cases}
\end{align}
\] The induced \(\infty\)-norm is the maximum absolute row sum: \[
\begin{align}
\|\bT_J\|_\infty
    = \max_{1 \leq i \leq n} \sum_{j=1}^n |(\bT_J)_{ij}|
    = \max_{1 \leq i \leq n} \sum_{j \neq i} \frac{|a_{ij}|}{|a_{ii}|}.
\end{align}
\] Because \(\bA\) is strictly diagonally dominant, \(|a_{ii}| &gt; \sum_{j \neq i} |a_{ij}|\) for each \(i \in \{1, ..., n\}\), so \[
\begin{align}
\sum_{j \neq i} \frac{|a_{ij}|}{|a_{ii}|} &lt; 1
    \implies
    \|\bT_J\|_\infty &lt; 1.
\end{align}
\] By Gelfand’s spectral radius theorem, \(\rho(\bT_J) \leq \|\bT_J\|_\infty &lt; 1\), which establishes convergence of the Jacobi iteration.
For Gauss–Seidel, let \(\lambda\) be an eigenvalue of \(\bT_{GS} = (\bD - \bL_A)^{-1}\bU_A\) with eigenvector \(\bv \neq \bzero\), normalized so that \(\|\bv\|_\infty = |v_p| = 1\). From \(\bU_A \bv = \lambda(\bD - \bL_A)\bv\), row \(p\) reads \[
\begin{align}
-\sum_{j &gt; p} a_{pj} v_j = \lambda a_{pp} v_p + \lambda \sum_{j &lt; p} a_{pj} v_j.
\end{align}
\] Rearranging and applying the triangle inequality with \(|v_j| \leq 1\) yields \[
\begin{align}
|\lambda| |a_{pp}|
    \leq \sum_{j &gt; p} |a_{pj}| + |\lambda| \sum_{j &lt; p} |a_{pj}|.
\end{align}
\] If \(|\lambda| \ge 1\), then \[
\begin{align}
|a_{pp}| \leq \frac{1}{|\lambda|} \sum_{j &gt; p} |a_{pj}| + \sum_{j &lt; p} |a_{pj}| \leq \sum_{j \neq p} |a_{pj}|,
\end{align}
\] which contradicts strict diagonal dominance. Hence \(|\lambda| &lt; 1\), proving \(\rho(\bT_{GS}) &lt; 1\).









NoteTheorem: Convergence of Gauss–Seidel for Symmetric Positive Definite Systems



If \(\bA \in \fR^{n \times n}\) is symmetric positive definite, then the Gauss–Seidel method converges for every initial vector \(\bx^{(0)} \in \fR^n\). The Jacobi method, however, is not guaranteed to converge for arbitrary SPD matrices.

NoteTheorem: Variational Equivalence for SPD Systems



Let \(\bA \in \fR^{n \times n}\) be a symmetric positive definite matrix and let \(\bb \in \fR^n\). Define the quadratic functional \(\phi: \fR^n \to \fR\) by \[
\begin{align}
\phi(\bx) = \frac{1}{2} \bx^T\bA\bx - \bb^T\bx.
\end{align}
\] The gradient of \(\phi\) is \(\nabla \phi(\bx) = \bA\bx - \bb = -\br(\bx)\), where \(\br(\bx) = \bb - \bA\bx\) is the residual. Because \(\bA\) is positive definite, the Hessian matrix \(\nabla^2 \phi(\bx) = \bA\) is strictly positive definite everywhere, and \(\phi\) has a unique global minimizer \(\bx^*\) satisfying \[
\begin{align}
\nabla \phi(\bx^*) = \bzero \iff \bA\bx^* = \bb.
\end{align}
\] Furthermore, the functional error is directly proportional to the squared \(\bA\)-norm of the solution error: \[
\begin{align}
\phi(\bx) - \phi(\bx^*) = \frac{1}{2} \|\bx - \bx^*\|_\bA^2.
\end{align}
\]








TipProof





Taylor expanding \(\phi(\bx)\) about the exact solution \(\bx^* = \bA^{-1}\bb\) gives \[
\begin{align}
\phi(\bx) &= \phi(\bx^*) + \nabla \phi(\bx^*)^T(\bx - \bx^*) + \frac{1}{2} (\bx - \bx^*)^T \bA (\bx - \bx^*) \\
    &= \phi(\bx^*) + \bzero + \frac{1}{2} \|\bx - \bx^*\|_\bA^2.
\end{align}
\] Since \(\bA\) is positive definite, \(\|\bx - \bx^*\|_\bA^2 &gt; 0\) for all \(\bx \neq \bx^*\). Thus, minimizing \(\phi(\bx)\) over any subspace is strictly equivalent to minimizing the energy norm of the error \(\|\bx - \bx^*\|_\bA\).









TipRemark



(The limitation of steepest descent) The method of steepest descent chooses the negative gradient \(-\nabla \phi(\bx^{(k)}) = \br^{(k)}\) as its search direction at each step, computing \(\bx^{(k+1)} = \bx^{(k)} + \alpha_k \br^{(k)}\) where \(\alpha_k\) is the exact line minimizer. Because successive gradients are mutually orthogonal in the Euclidean metric (\(\br^{(k+1)T}\br^{(k)} = 0\)), the iterates oscillate back and forth along narrow, elongated energy valleys. The convergence rate of steepest descent depends on \((\kappa - 1)/(\kappa + 1)\), leading to severe slowdown when \(\kappa_2(\bA) \gg 1\).

25.2 Conjugate Directions and Subspace Decoupling
To eliminate the inefficient zig-zagging of steepest descent, we replace orthogonal search directions with directions that are orthogonal with respect to the \(\bA\)-inner product.






NoteDefinition: A-Conjugate Vectors



Let \(\bA \in \fR^{n \times n}\) be symmetric positive definite. A set of nonzero vectors \(\{\bp_0, \bp_1, ..., \bp_{k-1}\} \subset \fR^n\) is termed \(\bA\)-conjugate (or \(\bA\)-orthogonal) if \[
\begin{align}
\bp_i^T \bA \bp_j = 0 \qquad \text{for all } i \neq j.
\end{align}
\] Because \(\bA\) is positive definite, any set of mutually conjugate nonzero vectors is linearly independent.








NoteTheorem: Subspace Decoupling and Exact Minimization



Let \(\{\bp_0, ..., \bp_{n-1}\}\) be an \(\bA\)-conjugate basis for \(\fR^n\). The exact solution \(\bx^* = \bx^{(0)} + \sum_{i=0}^{n-1} \alpha_i \bp_i\) can be computed by performing \(n\) independent one-dimensional line minimizations. The optimal step lengths are given by \[
\begin{align}
\alpha_k = \frac{\bp_k^T \br^{(0)}}{\bp_k^T \bA \bp_k} = \frac{\bp_k^T \br^{(k)}}{\bp_k^T \bA \bp_k}.
\end{align}
\] Furthermore, for any \(k \leq n\), the partial sum \(\bx^{(k)} = \bx^{(0)} + \sum_{i=0}^{k-1} \alpha_i \bp_i\) is the unique vector that minimizes \(\|\bx - \bx^*\|_\bA\) over the affine subspace \(\bx^{(0)} + \operatorname{span}\{\bp_0, ..., \bp_{k-1}\}\).








TipProof





Express the error in the conjugate basis: \[
\begin{align}
\bx - \bx^* = \sum_{i=0}^{n-1} c_i \bp_i.
\end{align}
\] Because the directions are \(\bA\)-conjugate, \(\bp_i^T\bA\bp_j = 0\) for \(i \neq j\). The squared \(\bA\)-norm decouples into a sum of scalar terms: \[
\begin{align}
\|\bx - \bx^*\|_\bA^2
    &= \left( \sum_{i=0}^{n-1} c_i \bp_i \right)^T \bA \left( \sum_{j=0}^{n-1} c_j \bp_j \right) \\
    &= \sum_{i=0}^{n-1} \sum_{j=0}^{n-1} c_i c_j \bp_i^T \bA \bp_j
    = \sum_{i=0}^{n-1} c_i^2 \bp_i^T \bA \bp_i.
\end{align}
\] Minimizing this sum over the first \(k\) coordinates is equivalent to minimizing each term \(c_i^2 \bp_i^T \bA \bp_i\) independently.
For the initial guess \(\bx^{(0)}\), write \(\bx^{(0)} - \bx^* = -\sum_{i=0}^{n-1} \alpha_i \bp_i\). Taking the \(\bA\)-inner product with \(\bp_k\): \[
\begin{align}
\bp_k^T \bA (\bx^{(0)} - \bx^*)
    &= -\sum_{i=0}^{n-1} \alpha_i \bp_k^T \bA \bp_i
    = -\alpha_k \bp_k^T \bA \bp_k.
\end{align}
\] Since \(\bA\bx^* = \bb\), the left-hand side is \(\bp_k^T(\bA\bx^{(0)} - \bb) = -\bp_k^T \br^{(0)}\). Solving for \(\alpha_k\): \[
\begin{align}
\alpha_k = \frac{\bp_k^T \br^{(0)}}{\bp_k^T \bA \bp_k}.
\end{align}
\] Because \(\br^{(k)} = \br^{(0)} - \sum_{i=0}^{k-1} \alpha_i \bA\bp_i\) and \(\bp_k^T\bA\bp_i = 0\) for \(i &lt; k\), we have \(\bp_k^T\br^{(k)} = \bp_k^T\br^{(0)}\), giving \(\alpha_k = \frac{\bp_k^T \br^{(k)}}{\bp_k^T \bA \bp_k}\).

25.3 The Conjugate Gradient Algorithm
The Conjugate Gradient algorithm generates the conjugate directions \(\{\bp_k\}\) dynamically from the sequence of residuals \(\{\br^{(k)}\}\) via a Gram–Schmidt process. Because \(\bA\) is symmetric, all Gram–Schmidt orthogonalization coefficients against previous directions vanish except for the immediately preceding one, yielding a simple two-term recurrence.






NoteTheorem: The Conjugate Gradient Algorithm



Let \(\bA \in \fR^{n \times n}\) be symmetric positive definite and let \(\bb \in \fR^n\). Given an initial estimate \(\bx^{(0)}\), compute \(\br^{(0)} = \bb - \bA\bx^{(0)}\) and set \(\bp^{(0)} = \br^{(0)}\). For \(k = 0, 1, 2, ...\):

Compute the matrix–vector product \(\bw^{(k)} = \bA \bp^{(k)}\).
Compute the step length: \[
\begin{align}
\alpha_k = \frac{\br^{(k)T}\br^{(k)}}{\bp^{(k)T}\bw^{(k)}}.
\end{align}
\]
Update the solution iterate: \[
\begin{align}
\bx^{(k+1)} = \bx^{(k)} + \alpha_k \bp^{(k)}.
\end{align}
\]
Update the residual vector: \[
\begin{align}
\br^{(k+1)} = \br^{(k)} - \alpha_k \bw^{(k)}.
\end{align}
\]
Check termination: if \(\|\br^{(k+1)}\|_2 / \|\bb\|_2 \leq `tol`\), terminate.
Compute the orthogonalization coefficient: \[
\begin{align}
\beta_k = \frac{\br^{(k+1)T}\br^{(k+1)}}{\br^{(k)T}\br^{(k)}}.
\end{align}
\]
Compute the next search direction: \[
\begin{align}
\bp^{(k+1)} = \br^{(k+1)} + \beta_k \bp^{(k)}.
\end{align}
\]









NoteTheorem: Orthogonality Properties of CG



In exact arithmetic, the sequences generated by the Conjugate Gradient algorithm satisfy the following orthogonality relations for all \(i \neq j\): \[
\begin{align}
\br^{(i)T}\br^{(j)} = 0 \qquad \text{and} \qquad \bp^{(i)T}\bA\bp^{(j)} = 0.
\end{align}
\] Moreover, the residual vectors and search directions span the Krylov subspace: \[
\begin{align}
\operatorname{span}\{\br^{(0)}, \br^{(1)}, ..., \br^{(k)}\} = \operatorname{span}\{\bp^{(0)}, \bp^{(1)}, ..., \bp^{(k)}\} = \mathcal{K}_{k+1}(\bA, \br^{(0)}).
\end{align}
\] Consequently, the method is guaranteed to reach the exact solution in at most \(n\) iterations in exact arithmetic.








TipRemark



(Connection with Lanczos) By the referenced result, the residuals \(\br^{(0)},...,\br^{(k-1)}\) are mutually orthogonal and the first \(j\) of them span \(\mathcal{K}_j(\bA,\br^{(0)})\). The Lanczos iteration of the referenced result, started from \(\bv_1=\br^{(0)}/\|\br^{(0)}\|_2\), produces an orthonormal basis with the same nested spans. Such a basis is unique up to the signs of its vectors, and the signs work out to \[
\begin{align}
\bv_{j+1}=(-1)^j\frac{\br^{(j)}}{\|\br^{(j)}\|_2},\qquad j=0,...,k-1.
\end{align}
\] The CG coefficients also determine the Lanczos matrix \(\bT_k\) of the referenced result. Its entries are \[
\begin{align}
(\bT_k)_{11}=\frac{1}{\alpha_0},\qquad
    (\bT_k)_{j+1,j+1}=\frac{1}{\alpha_j}+\frac{\beta_{j-1}}{\alpha_{j-1}},\qquad
    (\bT_k)_{j+1,j}=(\bT_k)_{j,j+1}=\frac{\sqrt{\beta_{j-1}}}{\alpha_{j-1}},
\end{align}
\] where \(\alpha_j\) and \(\beta_j\) are the CG step length and direction coefficient. Conjugate gradients may therefore be read as the Lanczos algorithm applied to a linear system.
The correspondence also describes the CG iterate directly. Write \(\bx^{(k)}=\bx^{(0)}+\bV_k\by_k\). Since \(\br^{(k)}\) is orthogonal to \(\mathcal{K}_k\), and since \(\bV_k^T\br^{(0)}=\|\br^{(0)}\|_2\be_1\) and \(\bV_k^T\bA\bV_k=\bT_k\), we obtain \[
\begin{align}
\bzero=\bV_k^T\br^{(k)}=\bV_k^T(\br^{(0)}-\bA\bV_k\by_k)=\|\br^{(0)}\|_2\be_1-\bT_k\by_k.
\end{align}
\] The CG iterate is thus obtained by solving the projected system \(\bT_k\by_k=\|\br^{(0)}\|_2\be_1\). In addition, the extreme eigenvalues of \(\bT_k\) approach \(\lambda_{\min}\) and \(\lambda_{\max}\) by the referenced result, so the CG coefficients give a running estimate of \(\kappa_2(\bA)\) at no extra cost in products with \(\bA\).








NoteExample



(Complete hand trace of a \(2 \times 2\) CG solve) Consider solving \(\bA\bx = \bb\) with \[
\begin{align}
\bA = \begin{pmatrix} 2 & 1 \\ 1 & 2 \end{pmatrix}, \qquad \bb = \begin{pmatrix} 4 \\ 5 \end{pmatrix}, \qquad \bx^{(0)} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}.
\end{align}
\]

Initialization (\(k=0\)): \[
\begin{align}
\br^{(0)} = \bb - \bA\bx^{(0)} = \begin{pmatrix} 4 \\ 5 \end{pmatrix}, \qquad \bp^{(0)} = \br^{(0)} = \begin{pmatrix} 4 \\ 5 \end{pmatrix}.
\end{align}
\]
First Iteration (\(k=0\)): \[
\begin{align}
\bA\bp^{(0)} &= \begin{pmatrix} 2 & 1 \\ 1 & 2 \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} 4 \\ 5 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 13 \\ 14 \end{pmatrix}, \\
         \br^{(0)T}\br^{(0)} &= 4^2 + 5^2 = 41, \\
         \bp^{(0)T}\bA\bp^{(0)} &= 4(13) + 5(14) = 52 + 70 = 122, \\
         \alpha_0 &= \frac{41}{122} \approx 0.3361, \\
         \bx^{(1)} &= \bx^{(0)} + \alpha_0 \bp^{(0)} = \frac{41}{122} \begin{pmatrix} 4 \\ 5 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 164/122 \\ 205/122 \end{pmatrix} \approx \begin{pmatrix} 1.3443 \\ 1.6803 \end{pmatrix}, \\
         \br^{(1)} &= \br^{(0)} - \alpha_0 \bA\bp^{(0)} = \begin{pmatrix} 4 \\ 5 \end{pmatrix} - \frac{41}{122}\begin{pmatrix} 13 \\ 14 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} -45/122 \\ 36/122 \end{pmatrix} \approx \begin{pmatrix} -0.3689 \\ 0.2951 \end{pmatrix}.
\end{align}
\] The inner product \(\br^{(1)T}\br^{(0)} = (-45)(4) + (36)(5) = -180 + 180 = 0\) confirms residual orthogonality.
Direction Update (\(k=0 \to 1\)): \[
\begin{align}
\br^{(1)T}\br^{(1)} &= \frac{(-45)^2 + 36^2}{122^2} = \frac{2025 + 1296}{14884} = \frac{3321}{14884}, \\
         \beta_0 &= \frac{\br^{(1)T}\br^{(1)}}{\br^{(0)T}\br^{(0)}} = \frac{3321/14884}{41} = \frac{81}{14884}, \\
         \bp^{(1)} &= \br^{(1)} + \beta_0 \bp^{(0)}
         = \frac{1}{14884}\begin{pmatrix} -5166 \\ 4797 \end{pmatrix}.
\end{align}
\] Checking conjugacy with \(\bA\bp^{(0)}=(13,14)^T\) gives \[
\begin{align}
\bp^{(1)T}\bA\bp^{(0)}
         =\frac{-5166(13)+4797(14)}{14884}=0.
\end{align}
\]
Second Iteration (\(k=1\)): Computing step \(\alpha_1\) and updating \(\bx^{(2)}\) arrives at the exact solution \(\bx^{(2)} = (1, 2)^T\) with residual \(\br^{(2)} = \bzero\).

NoteTheorem: The GMRES Projected Least-Squares Problem



Let any vector in the affine space \(\bx^{(0)} + \mathcal{K}_k(\bA, \br^{(0)})\) be parameterized as \(\bx = \bx^{(0)} + \bV_k \by\) for some vector \(\by \in \fR^k\). The residual vector satisfies \[
\begin{align}
\br(\bx) = \bb - \bA(\bx^{(0)} + \bV_k \by) = \br^{(0)} - \bA \bV_k \by = \beta \bv_1 - \bV_{k+1} \bar{\bH}_k \by = \bV_{k+1} (\beta \be_1 - \bar{\bH}_k \by).
\end{align}
\] Because the columns of \(\bV_{k+1}\) are orthonormal, \(\bV_{k+1}\) is an isometry in the Euclidean norm: \[
\begin{align}
\|\bb - \bA\bx\|_2 = \|\beta \be_1 - \bar{\bH}_k \by\|_2,
\end{align}
\] where \(\be_1 = (1, 0, ..., 0)^T \in \fR^{k+1}\). The GMRES iterate is \(\bx^{(k)} = \bx^{(0)} + \bV_k \by_k\), where \(\by_k\) uniquely solves the \((k+1) \times k\) upper Hessenberg least-squares problem \[
\begin{align}
\by_k = \arg\min_{\by \in \fR^k} \|\beta \be_1 - \bar{\bH}_k \by\|_2.
\end{align}
\]








TipProof





Express any \(\bx \in \bx^{(0)} + \mathcal{K}_k(\bA, \br^{(0)})\) as \(\bx = \bx^{(0)} + \bV_k \by\) for \(\by \in \fR^k\). The residual vector is \[
\begin{align}
\br(\bx)
    &= \bb - \bA(\bx^{(0)} + \bV_k \by) \\
    &= (\bb - \bA\bx^{(0)}) - \bA \bV_k \by \\
    &= \br^{(0)} - \bV_{k+1} \bar{\bH}_k \by.
\end{align}
\] Since \(\bv_1 = \br^{(0)} / \beta\), we have \(\br^{(0)} = \beta \bv_1 = \bV_{k+1}(\beta \be_1)\), where \(\be_1 = (1, 0, ..., 0)^T \in \fR^{k+1}\). Factoring \(\bV_{k+1}\) gives \[
\begin{align}
\br(\bx) = \bV_{k+1} (\beta \be_1 - \bar{\bH}_k \by).
\end{align}
\] Because the columns of \(\bV_{k+1}\) are orthonormal, \(\bV_{k+1}^T \bV_{k+1} = \bI_{k+1}\), and the Euclidean norm satisfies \[
\begin{align}
\|\br(\bx)\|_2^2
    &= (\beta \be_1 - \bar{\bH}_k \by)^T \bV_{k+1}^T \bV_{k+1} (\beta \be_1 - \bar{\bH}_k \by) \\
    &= \|\beta \be_1 - \bar{\bH}_k \by\|_2^2.
\end{align}
\] Thus \(\|\br(\bx)\|_2 = \|\beta \be_1 - \bar{\bH}_k \by\|_2\), reducing the \(n\)-dimensional optimization to the stated \((k+1) \times k\) Hessenberg least-squares problem.









NoteTheorem: Polynomial Residual Characterization and Spectral Bound



The \(k\)-th GMRES residual satisfies the polynomial minimization property \[
\begin{align}
\|\br^{(k)}\|_2 = \min_{p \in \mathcal{P}_k,\, p(0)=1} \|p(\bA)\br^{(0)}\|_2.
\end{align}
\] If \(\bA\) is diagonalizable with \(\bA = \bX \boldsymbol{\Lambda} \bX^{-1}\), where \(\boldsymbol{\Lambda} = \operatorname{diag}(\lambda_1, ..., \lambda_n)\), then the relative residual is bounded by \[
\begin{align}
\frac{\|\br^{(k)}\|_2}{\|\br^{(0)}\|_2} \leq \kappa_2(\bX) \min_{p \in \mathcal{P}_k,\, p(0)=1} \max_{\lambda \in \sigma(\bA)} |p(\lambda)|.
\end{align}
\]








TipProof





Any vector \(\bx \in \bx^{(0)} + \mathcal{K}_k(\bA, \br^{(0)})\) takes the form \(\bx = \bx^{(0)} + q(\bA)\br^{(0)}\) for some polynomial \(q \in \mathcal{P}_{k-1}\). The resulting residual is \[
\begin{align}
\br(\bx) = \bb - \bA\bx = \br^{(0)} - \bA q(\bA)\br^{(0)} = (\bI - \bA q(\bA))\br^{(0)}.
\end{align}
\] Define \(p(z) = 1 - z q(z)\). Then \(p \in \mathcal{P}_k\) and \(p(0) = 1\). Conversely, any polynomial \(p \in \mathcal{P}_k\) satisfying \(p(0) = 1\) can be written as \(p(z) = 1 - z q(z)\) for some \(q \in \mathcal{P}_{k-1}\). Because GMRES minimizes \(\|\br(\bx)\|_2\) over all such vectors, the first equality holds.
If \(\bA = \bX \boldsymbol{\Lambda} \bX^{-1}\), then \(p(\bA) = \bX p(\boldsymbol{\Lambda}) \bX^{-1}\). Taking norms gives \[
\begin{align}
\|p(\bA)\br^{(0)}\|_2 \leq \|\bX\|_2 \|p(\boldsymbol{\Lambda})\|_2 \|\bX^{-1}\|_2 \|\br^{(0)}\|_2 = \kappa_2(\bX) \left(\max_{\lambda \in \sigma(\bA)} |p(\lambda)|\right) \|\br^{(0)}\|_2,
\end{align}
\] which yields the spectral bound upon taking the minimum over all normalized polynomials \(p \in \mathcal{P}_k\).

NoteDefinition: Initial Value Problem



An initial value problem (IVP) for a system of ordinary differential equations has the form \[
\begin{align}
\by'(t) = \boldsymbol{f}(t, \by(t)), \qquad \by(t_0) = \by_0,
\end{align}
\] where \(t \in [t_0, T]\) and \(\by(t) \in \fR^m\). The function \(\boldsymbol{f}: [t_0, T] \times \fR^m \to \fR^m\) is the vector field.








NoteTheorem: Picard–Lindelöf Existence and Uniqueness



Let \(\boldsymbol{f}(t, \by)\) be continuous in \(t\) and uniformly Lipschitz continuous in \(\by\) on the domain \([t_0, T] \times \fR^m\): \[
\begin{align}
\|\boldsymbol{f}(t, \bu) - \boldsymbol{f}(t, \bv)\| \leq L \|\bu - \bv\| \qquad \text{for all } \bu, \bv \in \fR^m,
\end{align}
\] where \(L &lt; \infty\) is the Lipschitz constant. Then the IVP has a unique continuous solution \(\by(t)\) on \([t_0, T]\).








TipProof





The differential equation \(\by' = \boldsymbol{f}(t, \by)\) with initial condition \(\by(t_0) = \by_0\) is equivalent to the Picard integral equation: \[
\begin{align}
\by(t) = \by_0 + \int_{t_0}^t \boldsymbol{f}(s, \by(s))\,ds.
\end{align}
\] Assume first that \(L&gt;0\). Define the Picard operator \(\mathcal{T}\) on the Banach space \(C([t_0, T]; \fR^m)\) equipped with the weighted norm \(\|\by\|_* = \max_{t \in [t_0, T]} e^{-2L(t-t_0)} \|\by(t)\|\). For any two functions \(\bu, \bv\): \[
\begin{align}
\|(\mathcal{T}\bu)(t) - (\mathcal{T}\bv)(t)\| &\leq \int_{t_0}^t \|\boldsymbol{f}(s, \bu(s)) - \boldsymbol{f}(s, \bv(s))\|\,ds \\
    &\leq L \int_{t_0}^t \|\bu(s) - \bv(s)\|\,ds \\
    &= L \int_{t_0}^t e^{2L(s-t_0)} \left( e^{-2L(s-t_0)} \|\bu(s) - \bv(s)\| \right) ds \\
    &\leq L \|\bu - \bv\|_* \int_{t_0}^t e^{2L(s-t_0)}\,ds \\
    &= \frac{1}{2} \left(e^{2L(t-t_0)}-1\right)\|\bu-\bv\|_*.
\end{align}
\] Multiplying through by \(e^{-2L(t-t_0)}\) yields \(\|\mathcal{T}\bu - \mathcal{T}\bv\|_* \leq \frac{1}{2} \|\bu - \bv\|_*\). Because the operator \(\mathcal{T}\) is a strict contraction with modulus \(1/2\) on a complete metric space, the Banach Fixed-Point Theorem guarantees the existence of a unique fixed point \(\by^* = \mathcal{T}\by^*\), which is the unique solution to the IVP. If \(L=0\), then \(\boldsymbol f(t,\bu)=\boldsymbol f(t,\bv)\) for every \(\bu,\bv\), so \(\mathcal{T}\) has contraction modulus zero in the ordinary supremum norm.

29.2 Consistency, Stability, and the Discrete Gronwall Lemma
A numerical time-stepping method is convergent if the computed trajectory approaches the exact trajectory as the step size \(h \to 0\). We quantify error at each step and track its accumulation over a finite time interval.






NoteDefinition: Local Truncation Error and One-Step Defect



Let \(\by_{n+1} = \Phi_h(t_n, \by_n)\) denote an autonomous or non-autonomous one-step method. The local truncation error is the defect of the exact solution: \[
\begin{align}
\boldsymbol{\tau}_{n+1} = \frac{\by(t_{n+1}) - \Phi_h(t_n, \by(t_n))}{h}.
\end{align}
\] The method is consistent of order \(p\) if \(\|\boldsymbol{\tau}_{n+1}\| = O(h^p)\) as \(h \to 0\) for all sufficiently smooth solutions.








NoteLemma: Discrete Gronwall Lemma



Let \(\{e_n\}_{n=0}^N\) be a sequence of non-negative real numbers satisfying the linear recurrence \[
\begin{align}
e_{n+1} \leq (1 + \alpha) e_n + \beta, \qquad n = 0, 1, ..., N-1,
\end{align}
\] where \(\alpha, \beta \geq 0\). For \(\alpha&gt;0\), \[
\begin{align}
e_n \leq e^{n \alpha} e_0 + \beta \frac{e^{n \alpha} - 1}{\alpha}.
\end{align}
\] For \(\alpha=0\), the corresponding bound is \(e_n\leq e_0+n\beta\).








TipProof





Expanding the recurrence by induction gives \[
\begin{align}
e_n \leq (1 + \alpha)^n e_0 + \beta \sum_{k=0}^{n-1} (1 + \alpha)^k = (1 + \alpha)^n e_0 + \beta \frac{(1 + \alpha)^n - 1}{\alpha}.
\end{align}
\] Using the standard inequality \(1 + \alpha \leq e^\alpha\) for all \(\alpha \geq 0\), we have \((1 + \alpha)^n \leq (e^\alpha)^n = e^{n \alpha}\), which completes the proof.









NoteTheorem: Fundamental Theorem of One-Step Convergence



Let \(\Phi_h\) be a consistent one-step method of order \(p\) applied to an IVP whose vector field \(\boldsymbol{f}\) is Lipschitz continuous with constant \(L\). Suppose the numerical increment function satisfies \(\|\Phi_h(t, \bu) - \Phi_h(t, \bv)\| \leq (1 + \Lambda h) \|\bu - \bv\|\) for some constant \(\Lambda \geq 0\). Then on the fixed interval \([t_0, T]\) with \(Nh = T - t_0\), the global error \(\be_n = \by(t_n) - \by_n\) satisfies \[
\begin{align}
\max_{0 \leq n \leq N} \|\be_n\| \leq e^{\Lambda(T - t_0)} \|\be_0\| + \frac{e^{\Lambda(T - t_0)} - 1}{\Lambda} \max_k \|\boldsymbol{\tau}_k\|,
\end{align}
\] where the quotient is interpreted as \(T-t_0\) when \(\Lambda=0\). Consequently, when the numerical and exact solutions use the same initial value, a local error of order \(O(h^{p+1})\) yields a global error of order \(O(h^p)\).








TipProof





Express the error at step \(n+1\) by subtracting the numerical update from the exact solution: \[
\begin{align}
\be_{n+1} &= \by(t_{n+1}) - \by_{n+1} \\
    &= \left[ \Phi_h(t_n, \by(t_n)) + h \boldsymbol{\tau}_{n+1} \right] - \Phi_h(t_n, \by_n) \\
    &= \left[ \Phi_h(t_n, \by(t_n)) - \Phi_h(t_n, \by_n) \right] + h \boldsymbol{\tau}_{n+1}.
\end{align}
\] Taking norms and applying the triangle inequality yields \[
\begin{align}
\|\be_{n+1}\| \leq (1 + \Lambda h) \|\be_n\| + h \max_k \|\boldsymbol{\tau}_k\|.
\end{align}
\] Applying the Discrete Gronwall Lemma (the referenced result) with \(\alpha = \Lambda h\) and \(\beta = h \max_k \|\boldsymbol{\tau}_k\|\) gives \[
\begin{align}
\|\be_n\| \leq e^{n \Lambda h} \|\be_0\| + h \max_k \|\boldsymbol{\tau}_k\| \frac{e^{n \Lambda h} - 1}{\Lambda h} = e^{\Lambda(t_n - t_0)} \|\be_0\| + \frac{e^{\Lambda(t_n - t_0)} - 1}{\Lambda} \max_k \|\boldsymbol{\tau}_k\|.
\end{align}
\] Since \(t_n - t_0 \leq T - t_0\) and \(\max_k \|\boldsymbol{\tau}_k\| = O(h^p)\), the global error is bounded by \(O(h^p)\) when initial data are exact (\(\be_0 = \bzero\)).

29.5 Symplectic Integrators and Geometric Mechanics
Hamiltonian systems preserve geometric phase-space volume and energy. Standard Runge–Kutta methods dissipate or amplify energy over long integration horizons. Symplectic integrators preserve the underlying canonical symplectic 2-form.






NoteDefinition: Velocity Verlet Algorithm



For a separable Hamiltonian \(H(\bq, \bv) = \frac{1}{2}\|\bv\|_2^2 + V(\bq)\) with equations of motion \(\bq' = \bv\) and \(\bv' = \bF(\bq) = -\nabla V(\bq)\), the Velocity Verlet method is: \[
\begin{align}
\bv_{n+1/2} &= \bv_n + \frac{h}{2} \bF(\bq_n), \\
    \bq_{n+1} &= \bq_n + h \bv_{n+1/2}, \\
    \bv_{n+1} &= \bv_{n+1/2} + \frac{h}{2} \bF(\bq_{n+1}).
\end{align}
\]








NoteTheorem: Symplecticity and Area Preservation of Velocity Verlet



The one-step Velocity Verlet map \(\Psi_h: (\bq_n, \bv_n) \mapsto (\bq_{n+1}, \bv_{n+1})\) preserves the canonical symplectic structure. In particular, the Jacobian matrix \(\bJ = \frac{\partial(\bq_{n+1}, \bv_{n+1})}{\partial(\bq_n, \bv_n)}\) satisfies \[
\begin{align}
\det(\bJ) = 1.
\end{align}
\] Equivalently, its Jacobian satisfies \(\bJ^T\boldsymbol{\Omega}\bJ=\boldsymbol{\Omega}\) for the canonical symplectic matrix \(\boldsymbol{\Omega}\). Symplecticity implies \(\det(\bJ)=1\), so Velocity Verlet also preserves phase-space volume.








TipProof





Decompose the Velocity Verlet step into the composition of three elementary shears: \[
\begin{align}
\Psi_h = \psi_3 \circ \psi_2 \circ \psi_1,
\end{align}
\] where: \[
\begin{align}
\psi_1(\bq, \bv) &= \left( \bq,\, \bv + \frac{h}{2}\bF(\bq) \right), \\
    \psi_2(\bq, \bv) &= (\bq + h\bv,\, \bv), \\
    \psi_3(\bq, \bv) &= \left( \bq,\, \bv + \frac{h}{2}\bF(\bq) \right).
\end{align}
\] Evaluating the Jacobian of each shear map: \[
\begin{align}
\bJ_1 = \begin{pmatrix} \bI & \bzero \\ \frac{h}{2}\nabla\bF(\bq) & \bI \end{pmatrix}, \qquad
    \bJ_2 = \begin{pmatrix} \bI & h\bI \\ \bzero & \bI \end{pmatrix}, \qquad
    \bJ_3 = \begin{pmatrix} \bI & \bzero \\ \frac{h}{2}\nabla\bF(\bq_{n+1}) & \bI \end{pmatrix}.
\end{align}
\] Because \(\bF=-\nabla V\), the Hessian block \(\bS = \frac{h}{2}\nabla\bF = -\frac{h}{2}\nabla^2V\) is symmetric (\(\bS^T = \bS\)). With canonical symplectic matrix \(\boldsymbol{\Omega} = \begin{pmatrix} \bzero & \bI \\ -\bI & \bzero \end{pmatrix}\), evaluating the quadratic form for \(\bJ_1\): \[
\begin{align}
\bJ_1^T \boldsymbol{\Omega} \bJ_1 &= \begin{pmatrix} \bI & \bS \\ \bzero & \bI \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} \bzero & \bI \\ -\bI & \bzero \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} \bI & \bzero \\ \bS & \bI \end{pmatrix} \\
    &= \begin{pmatrix} -\bS & \bI \\ -\bI & \bzero \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} \bI & \bzero \\ \bS & \bI \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} -\bS + \bS & \bI \\ -\bI & \bzero \end{pmatrix} = \boldsymbol{\Omega}.
\end{align}
\] Similarly, for the translation shear \(\bJ_2\): \[
\begin{align}
\bJ_2^T \boldsymbol{\Omega} \bJ_2 &= \begin{pmatrix} \bI & \bzero \\ h\bI & \bI \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} \bzero & \bI \\ -\bI & \bzero \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} \bI & h\bI \\ \bzero & \bI \end{pmatrix} \\
    &= \begin{pmatrix} \bzero & \bI \\ -\bI & h\bI \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} \bI & h\bI \\ \bzero & \bI \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} \bzero & \bI \\ -\bI & -h\bI + h\bI \end{pmatrix} = \boldsymbol{\Omega}.
\end{align}
\] The third shear \(\bJ_3\) has the same form as \(\bJ_1\) and satisfies \(\bJ_3^T \boldsymbol{\Omega} \bJ_3 = \boldsymbol{\Omega}\). By the chain rule, \(\bJ = \bJ_3 \bJ_2 \bJ_1\), so \[
\begin{align}
\bJ^T \boldsymbol{\Omega} \bJ = \bJ_1^T \bJ_2^T \left( \bJ_3^T \boldsymbol{\Omega} \bJ_3 \right) \bJ_2 \bJ_1 = \bJ_1^T \left( \bJ_2^T \boldsymbol{\Omega} \bJ_2 \right) \bJ_1 = \bJ_1^T \boldsymbol{\Omega} \bJ_1 = \boldsymbol{\Omega}.
\end{align}
\] Each shear has unit determinant: \(\det(\bJ_1) = \det(\bJ_2) = \det(\bJ_3) = 1\). Therefore \[
\begin{align}
\det(\bJ) = \det(\bJ_3)\det(\bJ_2)\det(\bJ_1) = 1 \cdot 1 \cdot 1 = 1.
\end{align}
\]

31.3 Diffusion Maps and Nonlinear Manifolds






TipRemark



(From graphs to manifolds) When data points \(\bx_1, ..., \bx_N \in \fR^d\) lie on a low-dimensional manifold embedded in high-dimensional space, ambient Euclidean distances can obscure intrinsic connectivity or create shortcuts across a curved manifold. Diffusion maps define a random walk on a graph constructed over the point cloud and, under suitable sampling and bandwidth limits, approximate diffusion generated by a Laplace–Beltrami operator.








NoteDefinition: Diffusion Operator and Random Walk



Given point cloud \(\{\bx_i\}_{i=1}^N \subset \fR^d\), construct a pairwise affinity matrix \(\bK \in \fR^{N \times N}\) using a Gaussian kernel with bandwidth \(\delta &gt; 0\): \[
\begin{align}
K_{ij} = \exp\left(-\frac{\|\bx_i - \bx_j\|^2}{2\delta^2}\right).
\end{align}
\] Let \(d_i = \sum_{j=1}^N K_{ij}\) denote the row sums, and define \(\bD = \operatorname{diag}(d_1, ..., d_N)\). The row-stochastic diffusion matrix is \[
\begin{align}
\mathbf{M} = \bD^{-1} \bK.
\end{align}
\] The entry \(M_{ij} = K_{ij}/d_i\) defines the transition probability from point \(\bx_i\) to point \(\bx_j\) in a single diffusion step.








NoteTheorem: Spectral Decomposition of the Diffusion Operator



The matrix \(\mathbf{M} = \bD^{-1}\bK\) is similar to the real symmetric matrix \[
\begin{align}
\bS = \bD^{-1/2} \bK \bD^{-1/2} = \bD^{1/2} \mathbf{M} \bD^{-1/2}.
\end{align}
\] Let \(\bS = \bV \boldsymbol{\Lambda} \bV^T\) be the eigendecomposition of \(\bS\) with orthonormal eigenvectors \(\bV = [\bv_0, \bv_1, ..., \bv_{N-1}]\) and eigenvalues \[
\begin{align}
1 = \lambda_0 &gt; \lambda_1 \geq \lambda_2 \geq ... \geq \lambda_{N-1} \geq 0.
\end{align}
\] Then \(\mathbf{M}\) has real eigenvalues \(\lambda_k\) with right eigenvectors \(\boldsymbol{\psi}_k = \bD^{-1/2}\bv_k\) and left eigenvectors \(\boldsymbol{\phi}_k = \bD^{1/2}\bv_k\). The right eigenvectors satisfy \(\mathbf{M} \boldsymbol{\psi}_k = \lambda_k \boldsymbol{\psi}_k\), with \(\boldsymbol{\psi}_0 = \mathbf{1}\).








TipProof





Because \(\bK\) is symmetric and strictly positive, \(\bS = \bD^{-1/2}\bK\bD^{-1/2}\) is real symmetric. By the spectral theorem (the referenced result), \(\bS = \bV\boldsymbol{\Lambda}\bV^T\) with orthonormal eigenvectors \(\bV^T\bV = \bI\). Rewriting the similarity relation: \[
\begin{align}
\mathbf{M} = \bD^{-1/2} \bS \bD^{1/2}.
\end{align}
\] Multiplying by \(\boldsymbol{\psi}_k = \bD^{-1/2}\bv_k\): \[
\begin{align}
\mathbf{M}\boldsymbol{\psi}_k = \left( \bD^{-1/2}\bS\bD^{1/2} \right) \left( \bD^{-1/2}\bv_k \right) = \bD^{-1/2}\bS\bv_k = \lambda_k \bD^{-1/2}\bv_k = \lambda_k \boldsymbol{\psi}_k.
\end{align}
\] Similarly, defining left eigenvectors \(\boldsymbol{\phi}_k = \bD^{1/2}\bv_k\): \[
\begin{align}
\boldsymbol{\phi}_k^T\mathbf{M} = \left(\bD^{1/2}\bv_k\right)^T \left(\bD^{-1/2}\bS\bD^{1/2}\right) = \bv_k^T\bS\bD^{1/2} = \lambda_k \bv_k^T\bD^{1/2} = \lambda_k \boldsymbol{\phi}_k^T.
\end{align}
\] Biorthogonality follows directly: \[
\begin{align}
\boldsymbol{\phi}_j^T \boldsymbol{\psi}_k = \left(\bD^{1/2}\bv_j\right)^T \left(\bD^{-1/2}\bv_k\right) = \bv_j^T\bv_k = \delta_{jk}.
\end{align}
\] Because \(\mathbf{M}\) is row-stochastic, \(\mathbf{M}\mathbf{1} = \mathbf{1}\), so \(\lambda_0 = 1\) with right eigenvector \(\boldsymbol{\psi}_0 = \mathbf{1}\).









NoteDefinition: Diffusion Distance and Diffusion Map Coordinates



The \(t\)-step diffusion operator \(\mathbf{M}^t\) represents running the random walk forward for time \(t\). The diffusion distance between points \(\bx_i\) and \(\bx_j\) measures the difference between their conditional transition distributions weighted by the stationary measure \(\boldsymbol{\pi}\): \[
\begin{align}
D_t(\bx_i, \bx_j)^2
    =
    \|\mathbf{M}^t(i, :) - \mathbf{M}^t(j, :)\|_{\bD^{-1}}^2
    =
    \sum_{k=1}^{N-1} \lambda_k^{2t} \big(\psi_k(i) - \psi_k(j)\big)^2.
\end{align}
\] The diffusion map embedding of point \(\bx_i\) into an \(m\)-dimensional Euclidean space is \[
\begin{align}
\Psi_t(\bx_i) = \begin{pmatrix} \lambda_1^t \psi_1(i) \\ \lambda_2^t \psi_2(i) \\ \vdots \\ \lambda_m^t \psi_m(i) \end{pmatrix} \in \fR^m.
\end{align}
\] Under this embedding, Euclidean distance in \(\fR^m\) equals the truncated diffusion distance: \[
\begin{align}
\|\Psi_t(\bx_i) - \Psi_t(\bx_j)\|_2^2 \approx D_t(\bx_i, \bx_j)^2.
\end{align}
\]








TipRemark



(Diffusion time as a geometric low-pass filter) The factor \(\lambda_k^{2t}\) in the diffusion distance acts as an exponential low-pass filter on manifold coordinates. Because \(\lambda_k &lt; 1\) for all \(k \geq 1\), increasing \(t\) rapidly suppresses high-frequency modes associated with small eigenvalues, leaving only the dominant macroscopic geometric coordinates.








TipRemark



(Density normalization) The random walk of the referenced result depends on how densely the points sample the manifold as well as on its geometry. Coifman and Lafon separate the two effects by renormalizing the kernel before forming the random walk (Coifman and Lafon 2006). For a parameter \(\alpha\in[0,1]\), they replace \(K_{ij}\) by \(K_{ij}/(d_i^\alpha d_j^\alpha)\) and then compute new row sums and the row-stochastic matrix as before. In the limit of many points and small bandwidth, \(\alpha=0\) gives the classical normalized graph Laplacian, whose limit depends on the sampling density. The choice \(\alpha=1/2\) gives the generator of a Fokker–Planck diffusion, and \(\alpha=1\) recovers the Laplace–Beltrami operator independently of the density. The renormalized kernel is still symmetric, so the referenced result applies with the new row sums.

32.2 Krylov Subspace Approximation of the Exponential Action
To compute the action \(e^{t\bA}\bv\) for large sparse \(\bA\), we project the evolution onto an expanding Krylov subspace \(\mathcal{K}_m(\bA, \bv) = \operatorname{span}\{\bv, \bA\bv, ..., \bA^{m-1}\bv\}\).






NoteTheorem: Arnoldi Approximation of \(e^{t\bA}\bv\)



Let \(\bA \in \fC^{n \times n}\) and let \(\bv \in \fC^n\) be a nonzero vector with \(\beta = \|\bv\|_2\). After \(m\) steps of the Arnoldi iteration starting from \(\bv_1 = \bv / \beta\), we obtain the Arnoldi relation \[
\begin{align}
\bA \bV_m = \bV_m \bH_m + h_{m+1,m}\bv_{m+1}\be_m^T,
\end{align}
\] where \(\bV_m \in \fC^{n \times m}\) has orthonormal columns and \(\bH_m = \bV_m^* \bA \bV_m \in \fC^{m \times m}\) is upper Hessenberg. The Arnoldi approximation to the exponential action is \[
\begin{align}
e^{t\bA}\bv \approx \beta \bV_m e^{t\bH_m}\be_1.
\end{align}
\]








TipProof





We approximate the solution of \(\bu'(t) = \bA\bu(t)\) with \(\bu(0) = \bv\) within the subspace \(\mathcal{K}_m(\bA, \bv)\) by setting \(\bu_m(t) = \bV_m \bz_m(t)\), where \(\bz_m(t) \in \fC^m\). The residual defect of the ODE is \(\br_m(t) = \bu_m'(t) - \bA\bu_m(t) = \bV_m \bz_m'(t) - \bA\bV_m\bz_m(t)\). Imposing the Galerkin orthogonality condition \(\bV_m^* \br_m(t) = \bzero\) yields \[
\begin{align}
\bV_m^* \bV_m \bz_m'(t) - \bV_m^* \bA \bV_m \bz_m(t) = \bzero \implies \bz_m'(t) = \bH_m \bz_m(t).
\end{align}
\] The initial condition \(\bu_m(0) = \bv = \beta \bv_1 = \beta \bV_m \be_1\) requires \(\bz_m(0) = \beta \be_1\). Solving the reduced \(m \times m\) linear system gives \(\bz_m(t) = \beta e^{t\bH_m}\be_1\). Multiplying by \(\bV_m\) recovers \(\bu_m(t) = \beta \bV_m e^{t\bH_m}\be_1\).









NoteTheorem: Residual Representation and A Posteriori Error Bound



The defect in the differential equation satisfies \[
\begin{align}
\bu_m'(t) - \bA\bu_m(t) = -\beta h_{m+1,m} [\be_m^T e^{t\bH_m}\be_1] \bv_{m+1}.
\end{align}
\] Integrating the defect via variation of constants yields the exact error identity \[
\begin{align}
e^{t\bA}\bv - \beta \bV_m e^{t\bH_m}\be_1 = \beta h_{m+1,m} \int_0^t e^{(t-\tau)\bA} \bv_{m+1} [\be_m^T e^{\tau\bH_m}\be_1]\,d\tau.
\end{align}
\] If the operator \(\bA\) generates a contractive semigroup satisfying \(\|e^{\tau\bA}\| \leq 1\) for all \(\tau \geq 0\), the error is bounded by \[
\begin{align}
\|e^{t\bA}\bv - \beta \bV_m e^{t\bH_m}\be_1\| \leq \beta |h_{m+1,m}| \int_0^t |\be_m^T e^{\tau\bH_m}\be_1|\,d\tau.
\end{align}
\]








TipProof





Using the Arnoldi relation \(\bA\bV_m = \bV_m\bH_m + h_{m+1,m}\bv_{m+1}\be_m^T\), the ODE defect is \[
\begin{align}
\bu_m'(t) - \bA\bu_m(t) &= \bV_m \bz_m'(t) - \bA\bV_m \bz_m(t) \\
    &= \bV_m \bH_m \bz_m(t) - \left( \bV_m\bH_m + h_{m+1,m}\bv_{m+1}\be_m^T \right) \bz_m(t) \\
    &= -h_{m+1,m} \bv_{m+1} \be_m^T \bz_m(t) \\
    &= -\beta h_{m+1,m} [\be_m^T e^{t\bH_m}\be_1] \bv_{m+1}.
\end{align}
\] The approximation error \(\be(t) = \bu(t) - \bu_m(t)\) satisfies \(\be'(t) = \bA\be(t) - (\bu_m'(t) - \bA\bu_m(t))\) with \(\be(0) = \bzero\). Integrating via variation of constants: \[
\begin{align}
\be(t) = \int_0^t e^{(t-\tau)\bA} \left[ \bA\bu_m(\tau) - \bu_m'(\tau) \right] d\tau = \beta h_{m+1,m} \int_0^t e^{(t-\tau)\bA} \bv_{m+1} [\be_m^T e^{\tau\bH_m}\be_1]\,d\tau.
\end{align}
\] Taking norms with \(\|e^{(t-\tau)\bA}\| \leq 1\) and \(\|\bv_{m+1}\|_2 = 1\) yields \[
\begin{align}
\|\be(t)\| \leq \beta |h_{m+1,m}| \int_0^t \|e^{(t-\tau)\bA}\|_2 \|\bv_{m+1}\|_2 |\be_m^T e^{\tau\bH_m}\be_1|\,d\tau \leq \beta |h_{m+1,m}| \int_0^t |\be_m^T e^{\tau\bH_m}\be_1|\,d\tau.
\end{align}
\]









TipRemark



(Superlinear convergence of Krylov actions) Hochbruck and Lubich bound the error of the Krylov approximation in terms of the spectrum and the time step (Hochbruck and Lubich 1997). For a symmetric negative semidefinite matrix with eigenvalues in \([-4\rho,0]\), the error in approximating \(e^{\tau\bA}\bv\) from \(\mathcal{K}_m(\bA,\bv)\) is bounded by a constant multiple of \(e^{-m^2/(5\rho\tau)}\) when \(\sqrt{4\rho\tau}\leq m\leq2\rho\tau\). For \(m\geq2\rho\tau\) the bound decays superlinearly in \(m\). The relevant scale is therefore \(\rho\tau\), not \(\|\bA\|_2\) alone, and in the first regime the required dimension grows only like the square root of \(\rho\tau\). The same paper gives analogous bounds for non-Hermitian matrices whose numerical range lies in a known region of the left half-plane. The projected approximation also adapts to the spectrum seen from the starting vector rather than to the full matrix norm alone. Grid-independent convergence requires additional spectral or semigroup assumptions and should be checked for the discretization at hand.

32.3 The Continuous-Adjoint Sensitivity Formula
In parameter inference, we estimate unknown coefficients in an evolution model from discrete observations. Consider an affine generator family $$ \[\begin{align}

    \bA(\btheta) = \bA_0 + \sum_{j=1}^{M_{\mathrm{lib}}} \theta_j \bA_j,
\end{align}\] $$ where \(\bA_0\) is a baseline operator, the matrices \(\bA_j\) represent candidate physical mechanisms, and \(\btheta = (\theta_1, ..., \theta_{M_{\mathrm{lib}}})^T\) is a vector of unknown rate parameters.






NoteTheorem: Adjoint Gradient Formula for Exponential Actions



Let \(\by = e^{h\bA(\btheta)}\bv\) denote the state propagated across an interval of length \(h\). Let \(\ell(\by)\) be a differentiable scalar loss with endpoint gradient \(\nabla_{\by}\ell\). Then the derivative of the loss with respect to coefficient \(\theta_j\) is \[
\begin{align}
\frac{\partial \ell}{\partial \theta_j} = h \operatorname{Re} \int_0^1 \blambda(s)^* \bA_j \bm{f}(s)\,ds,
\end{align}
\] where the forward path \(\bm{f}(s)\) and adjoint path \(\blambda(s)\) are defined by \[
\begin{align}
\bm{f}(s) = e^{sh\bA}\bv, \qquad \blambda(s) = e^{(1-s)h\bA^*} \nabla_{\by}\ell, \qquad 0 \leq s \leq 1.
\end{align}
\]








TipProof





Let \(\bm{f}(s)\) satisfy the ODE \(\bm{f}'(s) = h\bA(\btheta)\bm{f}(s)\) with \(\bm{f}(0) = \bv\) and \(\by = \bm{f}(1)\). Differentiating this initial-value problem with respect to \(\theta_j\) yields the tangent equation \[
\begin{align}
\frac{d}{ds} \left( \frac{\partial \bm{f}}{\partial \theta_j} \right) = h\bA \left( \frac{\partial \bm{f}}{\partial \theta_j} \right) + h\bA_j \bm{f}(s), \qquad \frac{\partial \bm{f}(0)}{\partial \theta_j} = \bzero.
\end{align}
\] Applying variation of constants at the endpoint \(s = 1\) gives \[
\begin{align}
\frac{\partial \by}{\partial \theta_j} = h \int_0^1 e^{(1-s)h\bA} \bA_j \bm{f}(s)\,ds.
\end{align}
\] Applying the chain rule to the scalar loss \(\ell(\by)\) and taking the real part produces \[
\begin{align}
\frac{\partial \ell}{\partial \theta_j} &= \operatorname{Re}\left( (\nabla_{\by}\ell)^* \frac{\partial \by}{\partial \theta_j} \right)
    = h \operatorname{Re} \int_0^1 (\nabla_{\by}\ell)^* e^{(1-s)h\bA} \bA_j \bm{f}(s)\,ds \\
    &= h \operatorname{Re} \int_0^1 \left( e^{(1-s)h\bA^*} \nabla_{\by}\ell \right)^* \bA_j \bm{f}(s)\,ds
    = h \operatorname{Re} \int_0^1 \blambda(s)^* \bA_j \bm{f}(s)\,ds.
\end{align}
\]









TipRemark



(The adjoint sharing principle) Evaluating a separate directional Fréchet derivative \(L_{\exp}(h\bA, h\bA_j)\) for each candidate matrix \(\bA_j\) repeats the forward exponential propagation \(M_{\mathrm{lib}}\) times. The adjoint identity separates propagation from the candidate directions. We compute the forward path \(\bm{f}\) and adjoint path \(\blambda\) once per observation interval, and evaluate their contractions against \(\bA_j\) via numerical quadrature.

33.2 Continuous-Time Markov Chains and the CME






NoteDefinition: Continuous-Time Infinitesimal Generator



A continuous-time Markov jump process on a discrete state space evolves according to \[
\begin{align}
\frac{d}{dt}\bp(t) = \bA \bp(t),
\end{align}
\] where \(\bp(t)\) is the probability distribution vector and \(\bA\) is an infinitesimal generator matrix satisfying:

\(A_{ij} \geq 0\) for all \(i \neq j\) (transition rates between distinct states are nonnegative).
\(\sum_{i} A_{ij} = 0\) for every column \(j\) (column sums vanish).
\(A_{jj} = -\sum_{i \neq j} A_{ij} \leq 0\) (diagonal entries equal the negative total exit rate from state \(j\)).









NoteDefinition: Chemical Master Equation (CME)



Consider a chemically reacting system with \(S\) molecular species and \(R\) reaction channels. The discrete state \(\bx \in \mathbb{N}_0^S\) records the copy number of each species. Each reaction channel \(k \in \{1,...,R\}\) is characterized by a stoichiometric change vector \(\bnu_k \in \mathbb{Z}^S\) and a propensity function \(\alpha_k(\bx; \bc) \geq 0\) parameterized by rate constants \(\bc\).
The Chemical Master Equation governs the probability \(p(\bx, t)\) of occupying state \(\bx\) at time \(t\): \[
\begin{align}
\frac{\partial p(\bx, t)}{\partial t}
    =
    \sum_{k=1}^R \Big[ \alpha_k(\bx - \bnu_k; \bc)\, p(\bx - \bnu_k, t) - \alpha_k(\bx; \bc)\, p(\bx, t) \Big].
\end{align}
\] Enumerating the reachable states yields the linear system of ordinary differential equations \[
\begin{align}
\dot{\bp}(t) = \bA(\bc) \bp(t), \qquad \bp(0) = \bp_0,
\end{align}
\] whose exact formal solution is the matrix exponential action \(\bp(t) = \exp(t\bA(\bc)) \bp_0\).

33.4 Finite State Projection (FSP)






TipRemark



(Infinite state bottleneck) The CME generator \(\bA(\bc)\) is defined over an infinite or very large discrete state space \(\mathbb{N}_0^S\). A finite computation therefore requires truncation, aggregation, or another representation that avoids enumerating the full state space.








NoteDefinition: Finite State Projection (FSP)



Let the discrete state space be partitioned into a finite retained index set \(J \subset \mathbb{N}_0^S\) with \(|J| = K\), and an infinite or omitted complement set \(J^c\). Permuting states so that \(J\) appears first partitions the full generator: \[
\begin{align}
\bA = \begin{pmatrix} \bA_J & \bA_{J, J^c} \\ \bA_{J^c, J} & \bA_{J^c} \end{pmatrix}.
\end{align}
\] The Finite State Projection of Munsky and Khammash (Munsky and Khammash 2006) approximates the true marginal probability on \(J\) by solving the isolated subgenerator system \[
\begin{align}
\dot{\bp}_J(t) = \bA_J \bp_J(t), \qquad \bp_J(0) = \bp_0|_J.
\end{align}
\] Here \(\bA_J\) has negative diagonal entries and nonnegative off-diagonal entries, but its column sums are strictly nonpositive because transitions into \(J^c\) act as probability leaks.








NoteTheorem: FSP 1-Norm Leakage Bound



Let \(\bp(t)\) be the exact probability distribution solving \(\dot{\bp} = \bA\bp\) with initial condition supported on \(J\), so that \(\mathbf{1}^T\bp(t) = 1\). Let \(\bp_J(t) = \exp(t\bA_J)\bp_0|_J\) be the FSP solution. Then:

Pointwise underestimation: \(\bzero \leq \bp_J(t) \leq \bp|_J(t)\) for all \(t \geq 0\).
The full-space error of the zero-padded FSP approximation equals the leaked mass: \[
\begin{align}
\|\bp(t) - \widetilde{\bp}_J(t)\|_1 = 1 - \mathbf{1}^T\bp_J(t),
\end{align}
\] where \(\widetilde{\bp}_J(t)\) is \(\bp_J(t)\) padded with zeros on \(J^c\). The retained-state error satisfies \[
\begin{align}
\|\bp|_J(t)-\bp_J(t)\|_1
         =\mathbf{1}^T\bp|_J(t)-\mathbf{1}^T\bp_J(t)
         \leq 1-\mathbf{1}^T\bp_J(t).
\end{align}
\]









TipProof





The exact retained dynamics satisfy the non-autonomous subgenerator ODE \[
\begin{align}
\frac{d}{dt}\bp|_J(t) = \bA_J\bp|_J(t) + \bA_{J,J^c}\bp|_{J^c}(t), \qquad \bp|_J(0) = \bp_0|_J.
\end{align}
\] The FSP approximation satisfies \(\frac{d}{dt}\bp_J(t) = \bA_J\bp_J(t)\) with the same initial condition \(\bp_J(0) = \bp_0|_J\). Subtracting the two equations gives the defect ODE for the difference \(\boldsymbol{\delta}_J(t) = \bp|_J(t) - \bp_J(t)\): \[
\begin{align}
\frac{d}{dt}\boldsymbol{\delta}_J(t) = \bA_J \boldsymbol{\delta}_J(t) + \bA_{J,J^c}\bp|_{J^c}(t), \qquad \boldsymbol{\delta}_J(0) = \bzero.
\end{align}
\] By Duhamel’s formula (variation of constants): \[
\begin{align}
\boldsymbol{\delta}_J(t) = \bp|_J(t) - \bp_J(t) = \int_0^t e^{(t-s)\bA_J}\bA_{J,J^c}\bp|_{J^c}(s)\,ds.
\end{align}
\] Because \(\bA_J\) has non-negative off-diagonal entries, it is a Metzler matrix, and its matrix exponential \(e^{\tau\bA_J}\) is entrywise non-negative for all \(\tau \geq 0\). Furthermore, the coupling matrix \(\bA_{J, J^c} \geq \bzero\) and exact probabilities \(\bp|_{J^c}(s) \geq \bzero\) are non-negative. Consequently, the integrand is entrywise non-negative, proving that \[
\begin{align}
\bp|_J(t) - \bp_J(t) \geq \bzero \implies \bzero \leq \bp_J(t) \leq \bp|_J(t) \quad \text{for all } t \geq 0.
\end{align}
\]
Because \(\bp_J(t) \leq \bp|_J(t)\), the marginal 1-norm difference on \(J\) simplifies without absolute value signs: \[
\begin{align}
\|\bp|_J(t) - \bp_J(t)\|_1
    =
    \sum_{i \in J} \big( p_i(t) - [\bp_J(t)]_i \big)
    =
    \mathbf{1}^T\bp|_J(t) - \mathbf{1}^T\bp_J(t).
\end{align}
\] The total probability across the full space satisfies \(\mathbf{1}^T\bp|_J(t) + \mathbf{1}^T\bp|_{J^c}(t) = 1\). The omitted probability mass on \(J^c\) is exactly \(1 - \mathbf{1}^T\bp|_J(t)\). Adding the unmodeled mass on \(J^c\) gives \[
\begin{align}
\|\bp(t) - \widetilde{\bp}_J(t)\|_1
    &=
    \|\bp|_J(t) - \bp_J(t)\|_1 + \|\bp|_{J^c}(t)\|_1 \\
    &=
    \big(\mathbf{1}^T\bp|_J(t) - \mathbf{1}^T\bp_J(t)\big) + \big(1 - \mathbf{1}^T\bp|_J(t)\big) \\
    &=
    1 - \mathbf{1}^T\bp_J(t).
\end{align}
\] Since total variation distance is one half of the \(1\)-norm distance, the zero-padded approximation has total variation error \((1-\mathbf{1}^T\bp_J(t))/2\). A sink-state comparison uses a different state space and must be stated separately.

NoteDefinition: Nonlinear Dynamics and Observables



Consider a discrete-time dynamical system \[
\begin{align}
\bx^{(k+1)} = \mathbf{F}(\bx^{(k)}), \qquad \bx \in \mathcal{M} \subseteq \fR^n.
\end{align}
\] An observable is a scalar function \(g: \mathcal{M} \to \fR\) belonging to a function space \(\mathcal{F}\) (such as \(L^2(\mathcal{M})\)). The Koopman operator \(\mathcal{K}: \mathcal{F} \to \mathcal{F}\) is defined by \[
\begin{align}
(\mathcal{K}g)(\bx) = g(\mathbf{F}(\bx)).
\end{align}
\] The operator \(\mathcal{K}\) is linear on \(\mathcal{F}\), even when the underlying map \(\mathbf{F}\) is nonlinear.








NoteProposition: Linearity of the Koopman Operator



The Koopman operator \(\mathcal{K}\) satisfies \[
\begin{align}
\mathcal{K}(\alpha g_1 + \beta g_2) = \alpha \mathcal{K} g_1 + \beta \mathcal{K} g_2
\end{align}
\] for all scalars \(\alpha, \beta \in \fR\) and observables \(g_1, g_2 \in \mathcal{F}\).








TipProof





Evaluating the composition at any state \(\bx \in \mathcal{M}\) gives \[
\begin{align}
[\mathcal{K}(\alpha g_1 + \beta g_2)](\bx)
    &=
    (\alpha g_1 + \beta g_2)(\mathbf{F}(\bx)) \\
    &=
    \alpha g_1(\mathbf{F}(\bx)) + \beta g_2(\mathbf{F}(\bx)) \\
    &=
    \alpha [\mathcal{K}g_1](\bx) + \beta [\mathcal{K}g_2](\bx) \\
    &=
    [\alpha \mathcal{K}g_1 + \beta \mathcal{K}g_2](\bx).
\end{align}
\] Because this equality holds for all \(\bx \in \mathcal{M}\), the operators are identical.









NoteDefinition: Koopman Eigenfunctions and Eigenvalues



A function \(\varphi \in \mathcal{F}\) is a Koopman eigenfunction with eigenvalue \(\mu \in \fC\) if \[
\begin{align}
\mathcal{K}\varphi = \mu \varphi.
\end{align}
\] Along system trajectories, Koopman eigenfunctions evolve linearly: \[
\begin{align}
\varphi(\bx^{(k)}) = \mu^k \varphi(\bx^{(0)}).
\end{align}
\] A sufficiently rich collection of Koopman eigenfunctions can provide coordinates in which part of the nonlinear dynamics evolves linearly. Such a collection need not exist globally, and finite dictionaries generally give only a projected approximation.

34.4 Continuous Rate Recovery and Logarithmic Noise Explosion






TipRemark



(Discrete-to-continuous inversion) Given an EDMD estimate of the discrete Koopman operator \(\widehat{\bK}_h \approx \exp(h\mathcal{A})\) over a sampling interval \(h\), the continuous generator is recovered via the principal matrix logarithm: \[
\begin{align}
\widehat{\bA} = \frac{1}{h} \operatorname{logm}(\widehat{\bK}_h).
\end{align}
\] If \(\widehat{\bK}_h\) is real and has no eigenvalues on the closed negative real axis \((-\infty,0]\), then its principal matrix logarithm exists and is real. A matrix with eigenvalues on this branch cut may possess other logarithms, but it does not possess the principal logarithm used here.








NoteTheorem: Logarithmic Noise Explosion on Fast Decay Modes



Let \(\lambda_i &lt; 0\) be a continuous eigenvalue of the generator \(\mathcal{A}\), corresponding to a discrete Koopman eigenvalue \(\mu_i = \exp(h \lambda_i) \in (0, 1)\). The relation between discrete and continuous eigenvalues is \[
\begin{align}
\lambda_i = \frac{1}{h} \ln \mu_i.
\end{align}
\] The sensitivity of the continuous eigenvalue with respect to perturbations in the discrete eigenvalue is \[
\begin{align}
\left|\frac{d\lambda_i}{d\mu_i}\right| = \frac{1}{h \mu_i} = \frac{1}{h \exp(h \lambda_i)}.
\end{align}
\] For fast decaying modes where \(-\lambda_i \gg 1\), \(\mu_i \to 0\) exponentially fast. Consequently, the noise amplification factor diverges: \[
\begin{align}
\lim_{-\lambda_i \to \infty} \left|\frac{d\lambda_i}{d\mu_i}\right| = \infty.
\end{align}
\] A tiny statistical fluctuation \(\Delta \mu_i\) in data-driven EDMD induces an exponentially magnified error \(\Delta \lambda_i\) in the recovered rate.








TipProof





Differentiating \(\lambda_i = \frac{1}{h}\ln \mu_i\) directly with respect to \(\mu_i\) yields \(\frac{d\lambda_i}{d\mu_i} = \frac{1}{h\mu_i}\). Substituting \(\mu_i = \exp(h\lambda_i)\) produces \(\frac{1}{h} \exp(-h\lambda_i)\). Because \(h &gt; 0\) and \(\lambda_i &lt; 0\), \(-\lambda_i &gt; 0\), so \(\exp(-h\lambda_i) \to \infty\) as \(-\lambda_i \to \infty\).
